\par\par \b \uc2 *Central Bankers Cope With A Bipolar Global Economy--WSJ's Hilsenrath\b0\par\par\uc2 570 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2015\par 11:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2015 05:59 ET *World Central Banks Caught Between Two Powerful, Opposing Forces--WSJ's Hilsenrath\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2015 05:59 ET *Central Banks Caught Between U.S. Possibly Raising Rates, China Weighing New Stimulus--WSJ's Hilsenrath\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2015 05:59 ET  Central Bankers Cope With A Bipolar Global Economy--WSJ's Hilsenrath\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jon Hilsenrath \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fourth quarter could be a turbulent period for world central banks caught between two powerful and opposing forces--\f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  officials say they are finally nearing their first interest-rate increase in a decade as the nation's economy strengthens, while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  considers new ways to stimulate flagging growth in the world's second-largest economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some central banks--including those in Mexico, South Africa and Turkey--are warily watching the Fed for signs that they need to raise rates too or risk allowing their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fall to uncomfortably to low levels. Other central banks - in South Korea, Australia and New Zealand among many--are weighing whether to reduce rates or take other steps to ease financial conditions to counteract the forces of slow global growth and downward pressure on commodities prices emanating from China. Big developed-economy central banks in Europe and Japan could take new easing measures, too.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Who moves up and who moves down is a function of economic fundamentals--and exposure to the world's two dominant central banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexico's economy is heavily influenced by its big northern neighbor, so it looks likely to raise rates soon after the Fed moves. South Korea is near China is inclined to do the opposite. Turkey's economy could be damaged by sharp declines in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while New Zealand would welcome a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  has been warning all year that it expected to raise rates in 2015. Fed Chairwoman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  repeated the message in late September, a week after Fed officials decided the global economic outlook was too uncertain to move at their September policy meeting. A U.S. employment report to be released Friday, Oct. 2, could play an important role in swaying Fed officials on whether to move this month or wait until December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many central banks in the fourth quarter will take their cues from the Fed's next move. If the U.S. holds off on moving rates higher as it did in September, \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials might decide they need to take their own steps to ease policy. A Fed in a standstill puts upward pressure on the euro, which hurts European growth and holds down inflation. A Fed rate increase, on the other hand, would push the euro down, which could help stimulate European growth and inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and China, meantime, are influencing each other's orbits. A Fed decision to raise rates could put downward pressure on the yuan, forcing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  further to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from falling. Weaker growth in China, on the other hand, could put renewed downward pressure on global commodities prices and add to squeamishness among Fed officials to act.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a bipolar world, investors can be excused for some days of manic-depressive confusion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Jon Hilsenrath at jon.hilsenrath@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 02, 2015 05:59 ET (09:59 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151002eba2000s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
