\par\par \b \uc2 China Begins Disclosing Reserves to \uc2 IMF\uc2  -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Talley And Lingling Wei   \par \uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2015\par 21:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China has begun to report its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  for the first time--a milestone in opening a key facet of the country's economy to the public view.\par \par \uc2 The move comes as Beijing seeks to have its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, included in the basket of reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that comprise the fund's lending instrument.\par \par \uc2 In a note accompanying new data on globally-held \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said China has reported a "representative portfolio on a partial basis," meaning that what it has shared represents the fractional breakdown of its holdings of different \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . China will gradually report its full \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings over two to three years, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 It was also the first time the \uc2 IMF\uc2  listed all of the countries that report their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings. Of the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's 188 member countries, 146 provide their reserves data to the fund, but only 96 agreed to have their names released.\par \par \uc2 Reported \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves world-wide jumped by around $600 billion in the second quarter of the year with China's new data. The \uc2 IMF\uc2 's numbers don't reveal how much each of the reporting countries holds. But based on the fund's breakdown of the aggregated share of different \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held, it seems China's portfolio matches most \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings: roughly 60% dollars and 20% euros and pounds sterling--with the Japanese yen and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  making up the difference.\par \par \uc2 For China, getting the yuan included in the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket is a symbol of the country's growing status as a global power.\par \par \uc2 To win reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  status China has begun to liberalize its exchange-rate regime and provide more transparency about its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. Last year China committed to providing more details about its reserve holdings, an effort that will allow economists to better gauge the degree to which the country is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its exchange rate.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  now discloses its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves every month, whereas in the past it reported the figures quarterly. It also has started to disclose its holdings of gold every month.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  has also opened the door wider for foreign central banks to invest in China's fast-growing yuan-bond market. On Wednesday, it issued new rules allowing foreign banks to trade in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Still, despite all the new measures, many in China are only cautiously optimistic about the yuan's chances of getting into the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket when the board votes in November. Even if the \uc2 IMF\uc2  approves yuan inclusion this year, it would only officially be added to the basket late next year.\par \par \uc2 "I give it a 50% chance," says Zhang Ming, a senior economist at the \uc2 Chinese Academy of Social Science\uc2 , a government think tank.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ian Talley at ian.talley@wsj.com and Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 30, 2015 15:30 ET (19:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150930eb9u0037b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
