\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan's Short-Term Rates Spike in Hong Kong as Liquidity Dries Up\b0\par\par\uc2 By Fiona Law   \par \uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2015\par 08:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG--Short-term borrowing rates in the Chinese yuan surged to their highest in a month in Hong Kong Tuesday, as banks hoarded cash ahead of China's long holiday and the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the offshore market dried up liquidity.\par \par \uc2 The rate increase comes amid recent China data that signal a weakening economy, and as the value of the yuan has strengthened against the U.S. dollar in both onshore and offshore trading. Traders said the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the offshore market, while a Wall Street Journal report in August revealed it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the onshore market.\par \par \uc2 In a fresh sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  offshore, the yuan abruptly strengthened to 6.34 against the dollar late Tuesday in Asia. For most of the day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been relatively calm, hovering around 6.36 to the dollar for most of the day after strengthening from roughly 6.4 per dollar last Friday.\par \par \uc2 "Chinese banks have been buying [the yuan] aggressively this evening," said one senior trader at a major local bank.\par \par \uc2 In Shanghai, the yuan closed at 6.36 against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, slightly stronger than 6.37 late Monday.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Tuesday, the overnight rate that banks in Hong Kong charge each other to borrow the yuan jumped to 8.73% from 3.38% Friday, according to the offshore fixing rate--a benchmark based on reference rates contributed by local banks and compiled by the Treasury Markets Association, the city's banking industry group.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's markets, which constitute China's biggest offshore yuan hub, were closed Monday and will be closed Thursday for holiday.\par \par \uc2 The liquidity squeeze has been the worst since the end of last month, when the overnight borrowing rate for offshore yuan hit as high as 20% amid a heavy selloff in dim-sum bonds--yuan-denominated securities mainly traded in Hong Kong--since investors realized yuan assets are no longer a sure bet.\par \par \uc2 At that time, when investors cashed out of yuan bonds, they were given yuan. But banks in Hong Kong had most of their yuan locked up in clients' time deposits, creating a liquidity mismatch for banks. Banks scrambled for short-term cash to meet their needs, driving up interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Rates fell shortly after as the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , injected cash to soothe market liquidity.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  was believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the offshore market by buying yuan through Chinese banks' Hong Kong branches, in an effort to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to traders in the city.\par \par \uc2 "The [offshore yuan] wouldn't have risen so much in last two weeks" without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Tommy Ong, Head of Wealth Management Solutions at \uc2 DBS Group Holdings Ltd\uc2 . in Hong Kong. "The market has been pretty bearish towards the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  given the slew of weak economic data."\par \par \uc2 Last week, a preliminary measure of Chinese factory output in September hit its lowest point since the financial crisis, underscoring China's vulnerability to global trade headwinds.\par \par \uc2 As a result, more banks, particularly Chinese ones, have been holding a large amount of yuan, leaving limited liquidity in the market.\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan is 1.9% stronger than its value three weeks ago, after falling as much as 6% since the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's move to devalue the yuan and its pledge to insert more market influence to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime on Aug 11.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Norman Chan\uc2 , said earlier this month that he wouldn't comment on whether the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the offshore market. But even it did, he said the authority wouldn't feel "awkward" as regulators world-wide have their own monetary policies to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more stable as the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its spot and derivatives markets to curb any selloff, according to The Wall Street Journal's report.\par \par \uc2 Traders say banks in Hong Kong are hoarding cash ahead of China's National Day holiday, which begins Thursday and lasts for seven days. Hong Kong markets will also be closed Thursday and trading activities here tend to be quiet during China's holiday. That is another reason offshore yuan liquidity has ground tighter.\par \par \uc2 Write to Fiona Law at fiona.law@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 30-09-15 0646GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150930eb9u0006l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
