\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China sees capital account deficit, volatile fund flows in H2\b0\par\par\uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2015\par 07:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator sees volatile capital flows amid market turmoil\par \par \uc2 * Expects capital account deficit, current account surplus in H2\par \par \uc2 * Says to keep yuan basically stable (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Sept 30 (Reuters) - China is likely to continue to see a deficit on its capital and financial account in the second half amid global market volatility, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Concerns over China's economic slowdown and possible interest rate rises by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  have fuelled a wave of capital outflows, which intensified after China's surprising yuan devaluation last month.\par \par \uc2 "Cross-border capital flows will be relatively volatile," the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) said in a statement on its website.\par \par \uc2 Expectations of an upcoming Fed interest rate rise and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility in emerging markets will continue to affect international financial stability and fuel risk aversion, the SAFE said.\par \par \uc2 "Looking at the domestic environment, the downward pressure persists in the process of economic transition, while domestic borrowing costs continue to decline," it said.\par \par \uc2 China posted a deficit of $125.6 billion on its capital account and financial account in the first half of 2015, including $40.6 billion in the March-June quarter.\par \par \uc2 China had a current account surplus of $148.6 billion in the first half, including $73 billion in the second quarter, the SAFE said in its international balance payment report.\par \par \uc2 China's current account surplus in the first half was equivalent to 3.1 percent of gross domestic product, it said.\par \par \uc2 China will keep the yuan basically stable while improving management of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to ensure safety, the regulator said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to keep the yuan steady after it shocked global markets by devaluing the yuan by nearly 2 percent on Aug. 11, running down its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by a record amount that month.\par \par \uc2 It also said that the authorities will strengthen monitoring of cross-border capital flows.\par \par \uc2 The authorities have taken some incremental steps to curb capital outflows that intensified since the country's surprising yuan devaluation last month.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting By China economics team; Editing by \uc2 Jacqueline Wong\uc2  & Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-30T06:38:47.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150930eb9u006t6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
