\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's yuan rises on pre-holiday demand, c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2015\par 07:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Sept 30 (Reuters) - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yuan firmed against\par dollar both onshore and offshore on Wednesday as demand for the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  soared ahead of a week-long  holiday, traders said.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in trading partly to\par converge the spread between the two markets, they added.\par     The onshore market in Shanghai will be closed for one week\par starting on Thursday for the Chinese National Day break, while\par the Hong Kong offshore market will be closed for two days on\par Thursday and Friday.\par     Yuan liquidity demand in banks typically surges ahead of\par such a long holiday as residents withdraw savings for tourism\par and firms make payments for employee benefits.\par     The offshore yuan was trading 0.14 percent firmer\par than the onshore spot at 6.3466 per dollar by midday. It rose on\par Tuesday to its strongest rate against the dollar since China\par surprised markets by devaluing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Aug. 11.\par     That represented a reversal of the trend. The offshore\par market once enjoyed a discount as much as nearly 2 percent in\par the days following the devaluation because of widespread\par expectations of more downside for the yuan in the long run.\par     "Demand for yuan soared partly due to the upcoming holiday,"\par said a trader at a foreign commercial bank in Hong Kong. "And\par some Chinese banks bought large amount of yuan in offshore\par market."\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 typically uses state-owned\par banks to trade on behalf of itself to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in trading to\par influence the yuan's value.\par     Traders said they believed Wednesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a\par sign that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hopeed to narrow the gap between the\par onshore and offshore market, in part to boost the yuan's chances\par of being included in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Special\par Drawing Rights (SDR) basket.\par     Offshore traders also said that the surprise appreciation of\par the yuan in the past few days had also sparked stop-loss orders,\par  which also boosted the yuan's value.\par     The greenback's weakness in global markets also added\par weight, traders said.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate at 6.3613 per\par dollar prior to market open, 0.07 percent firmer than the\par previous fix 6.366. \par     The onshore spot rate opened at 6.3510 per dollar\par and was changing hands at 6.3554 at midday, 0.13 percent firmer\par than the previous close, also the strongest against the dollar\par since Aug. 11.\par     The yuan is set to appreciate 0.33 percent in September if\par it closes at the midday level, reversing a 2.61 percent\par depreciation in August.\par     Surprised by the unexpectedly strong global market reaction\par to its abrupt devaluation, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has stepped up its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stabilise the yuan.\par     In a related development, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator\par said on Wednesday that the country would continue to see a\par surplus in the current account but would post deficits in the\par capital and financial accounts in the second half of 2015.\par  \par     \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint      6.3613   6.366     0.07%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.3554   6.3636    0.13%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    -0.09%             \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change ytd                       -2.38%\par  Spot change since 2005                30.23%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.3466    0.14%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              6.555     -2.95%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par     GRAPHICS:\par     The yuan in trade-weighted terms \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/sed74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/sed74t}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     The spot market versus the trading band \f28 {\field{\*\fld