\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE-GLOBAL ECONOMY WEEKAHEAD-Euro zone inflation, U.S. jobs set to dominate\b0\par\par\uc2 776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2015\par 09:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with results of Catalan election; otherwise unchanged)\par \par \uc2 * Euro zone annual inflation seen at zero in Sept\par \par \uc2 * Negative number would fuel stimulus expectations\par \par \uc2 * 203,000 more U.S. jobs seen in Sept, unemployment steady\par \par \uc2 By Catherine Evans\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 25 (Reuters) - Euro zone inflation and U.S. jobs data will offer clues to the health of major developed economies in the coming week while the malaise gripping emerging markets is expected to prompt India to cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 China may release monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve data indicating how much more the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent on steadying the yuan following Aug. 11's surprise devaluation.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, separatists won a clear majority of seats in Catalonia's parliament in an election that sets the region on a collision course with Spain's central government over independence, while polls point to no clear winner in Portugal's Oct. 4 election.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's flash reading of September's annual euro zone inflation is expected at zero, although core inflation, which excludes volatile energy prices, is seen at 0.9 percent for a third consecutive month.\par \par \uc2 A negative headline inflation reading, which would be the first since March, would fuel speculation about further \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus, six months after the euro zone's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched a 1 trillion-euro-plus asset-purchase programme.\par \par \uc2 Last Wednesday, however, a surprisingly hawkish-sounding \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  said \uc2 the ECB\uc2  needed more time to assess whether China's slowdown, particularly its impact on commodity prices, cheap oil and a rising euro, would slow inflation further.\par \par \uc2 Even if inflation turns negative again, deflation risks remain low, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  analysts said in a note, with a fading of the base effect from 2014's plunge in energy prices likely to push the headline rate higher by year-end.\par \par \uc2 Friday's non-farm payrolls data is expected to show the U.S. economy added 203,000 jobs in September with the unemployment rate holding steady after falling in August to 5.1 percent, its lowest since April 2008. Wage growth, a focus for \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  policymakers, also accelerated last month.\par \par \uc2 Buoyant labour market data would revive expectations of a first U.S. interest rate rise in nearly a decade, after a sharp selloff in global financial markets sparked by worries about China's economy prompted the Fed to hold fire this month.\par \par \uc2 Fed Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said on Thursday she expects the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to begin raising rates this year as long as inflation remains stable and the U.S. economy is strong enough to boost employment. Her comments lifted the dollar on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2  has two more chances to hike this year, at meetings in October and December. A Reuters poll in the past week found 72 of 93 economists expected a rise in December. Only nine foresaw a move next month and eight predicted the decision would be deferred to the first quarter of 2016.\par \par \uc2 "We continue to favour a December rate hike," wrote \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  economists in a note. "But on the back of Yellen's comments today the risk is that the Fed pulls the trigger in October."\par \par \uc2 A number of Fed policymakers are due to speak in the coming week, which could help hone views on the likely timing of a hike.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  is seen cutting interest rates for the fourth time this year when it meets on Tuesday, as falling energy prices have cooled inflation and the economy has slowed.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll forecast a 25 basis point reduction to 7.0 percent, a four-year low.\par \par \uc2 Purchasing Managers' surveys on Thursday will give further clues to the strength of China's economy, after a similar release this week showed factory activity at a 6-1/2 year low, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also release \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves data.\par \par \uc2 The $93.9 billion decline in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves in August, reflecting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the yuan's devaluation, was the biggest monthly fall on record and marked an 11 percent drop from a June 2014 peak.\par \par \uc2 Capital outflows have escalated as fears grow that the world's second-largest economy is slowing as U.S. interest rates look set to rise, although at $3.557 trillion in August, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves remain the world's biggest.\par \par \uc2 In an interview published this week, Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  described the drop in reserves as "moderate and manageable" and said there was "no need to overreact to it". (editing by John Stonestreet) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-28T08:07:55.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150928eb9s0080x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
