\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan firms as dollar demand eases, seen trading narrowly\b0\par\par\uc2 517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2015\par 05:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Sept 25 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed on Friday\par as dollar demand dropped after officials repeatedly said that\par there was no basis for the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to depreciate in the\par long run.\par     The yuan is set to weaken 0.2 percent for the week if it\par closes at the midday level, as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (PBOC)\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 has eased off from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in trading, they said.\par     "The yuan is largely moving in line with dollar demand and\par supply this week as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  largely stayed on the sidelines,"\par said a dealer at a Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai.\par     "With less dollar demand, the yuan is likely to trade\par narrowly in the near term."\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate at 6.3785\par per dollar prior to market open, almost unchanged from the\par previous fix 6.3791. \par     The spot market opened at 6.3800 per dollar and\par was changing hands at 6.3768 at midday, 0.09 percent firmer than\par the previous close.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has generally scaled back on onshore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in recent weeks, following a rapid-fire series of support moves\par after its surprise devaluation of the yuan in mid-August.\par     The devaluation triggered intense speculation that the\par government would allow the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken further to help\par struggling exporters, causing a short-lived rush to buy dollars.\par That caused top officials to reiterate that they saw no basis\par for more depreciation.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  official Sheng Songcheng told a forum on Friday that\par volatility in Chinese markets was not due to the opening of\par China's capital account but that the slow pace of reform was to\par blame for leaving domestic markets vulnerable to global\par volatility. \par     "China has never allowed the yuan to depreciate at will over\par the past decade," Sheng said. "There could be some excessive\par depreciation of the exchange rate in the short term, but it will\par finally strike an equilibrium." \par     Offshore yuan was trading 0.52 percent weaker than the\par onshore spot at 6.4102 per dollar. \par     \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint      6.3785   6.3791    0.01%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.3768   6.3828    0.09%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    -0.03%             \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change ytd                       -2.71%\par  Spot change since 2005                29.79%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.4102    -0.52%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              6.6075    -3.47%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par     GRAPHICS:\par     The yuan in trade-weighted terms \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/sed74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/sed74t}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     The spot market versus the trading band \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://reut.rs/1MhsqOi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://reut.rs/1MhsqOi}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \par  (Reporting by Lu Jianxin; Editing by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jacqueline Wong\f28 \uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-25T04:52:21.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150925eb9p00467\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
