\par\par \b \uc2 China Shares Rally on Stronger Sentiment -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2015\par 07:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0511 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China's markets gain amid recovering sentiment, as turnover rate on the smaller ChiNext board rises, suggesting higher risk appetite, say analysts. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index edges up 0.7% at 3177.02, with stocks in brokerage firms, insurance and public transportation leading gains. The Shenzhen Composite Index finishes the morning session up 0.1% at 1741.16, while the ChiNext falls 0.7% at 2065.37. "Fund managers are on the sidelines and waiting to return to the market once the chance is ripe, as their current positions are very low," says Deng Wenyuan, an analyst at \uc2 Soochow Securities\uc2 . However, rising turnover rate on the ChiNext shows investors are turning to a more positive outlook on the market, Deng adds. (Yifan.xie@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0511 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] MBA applications are up across the board this year, as are applications to several specialized master's programs in business, according to a new survey from the \uc2 Graduate Management Admission Council\uc2 , which tracks applicant data. Fifty-seven percent of two-year, full-time M.B.A. program saw increased application volume this year, according to GMAC, and 59% of such programs reported an increase in domestic applications. Some 51% of full-time one-year programs also reported application-volume increases, compared with just 37% that reported growth last year, GMAC said. Women made up a larger share of the pool this year in all programs tracked except Master of Accounting, where the share of women applicants remained constant at 57%, GMAC said. (lindsay.gellman@wsj.com; @lindsaygellman) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0500 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Inspired by the American "Black Friday" consumer spending spree, South Korea plans to launch its own version in October to boost the country's sluggish domestic demand. The finance ministry said in a joint government statement Tuesday that the country will organize a two-week "Korea Black Friday" event from Oct. 1 for consumers to buy goods for up to 70% off at about 27,000 retailers, markets and online vendors nationwide. The Oct. 1-14 event is designed as a part of "the pan-national efforts to boost domestic demand and consumer spending" when the country's exports are losing steam on weak global trade, the statement said. It added Korea considers making the event an annual one from now on. Still recovering from the impact of the MERS viral outbreak that badly hit consumer spending, Korea has recently taken a series of steps to fight sluggish domestic demand. Korea last month announced a plan to temporarily cut consumption taxes on cars and big home appliances goods. It also launched a separate "Korea Grand Sales" event in August for foreign visitors and tourists to get discounts while shopping at local stores.(kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0458 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Revenue growth for most Chinese companies will remain under pressure through 2016 amid economic slowdown, warns \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Leverage will also stay elevated for many, the rating agency adds. Steel and cement manufacturers will see their revenues fall by 15%-20% for all of 2015, and their profitability profiles will deteriorate, against the backdrop of weak demand and product price declines. Meanwhile, continued weak crude oil prices will pressure revenue growth for exploration and production, and oil field services companies. Most department store operators and food and beverage manufacturers will also see revenues decline in 2015, but they are expected to stabilize in 2016. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects auto companies to see revenue growth against the backdrop of a moderate increase in domestic vehicles sales. Construction companies will also see continued growth, owing in part to the strong order backlog on hand from their overseas and infrastructure projects, it says. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0453 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India's rupee is trading at a 1-month high to the U.S. dollar, fending off broad U.S. dollar strength that materialized overnight after hawkish comments from members of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 . USD/INR has fallen to its lowest since Aug 21 despite the greenback rising against other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , notably hitting a 17-year high versus the Indonesian rupiah. The rupee's strength - helped by the benchmark India stock exchange rallying 0.3% - runs counter to bearish rhetoric from economists. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asian Development Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued a report Tuesday revising lower its 2015 growth estimate for India to 7.4% from its March forecast of 7.8%. It also downgraded its GDP forecasts for other Asian economies, including China. But rupee speculators may stay bullish ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2 's monetary policy meeting at the end of the month, due to the chance of a growth-boosting interest rate cut - a possibility that was also mentioned in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ADP\f28 \uc2 \f2  report. USD/INR is now at 65.59 from its Monday close of 65.68. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0441 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] After the stock market turmoil sparked global concerns about China's decelerating economy, Chinese President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xi Jinping\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the government is preparing a full slate of economic reforms for the rest of the year. Already, Mr. Xi says in a written interview with The Wall Street Journal, the government this month committed to encourage private and other investment in state enterprises. Taking priority for the rest of the year, he says, will be measures that facilitate growth, prevent risk and further a structural shift in the economy. Mr. Xi didn't provide specifics.(charles.hutzler@dowjones.com; @charleshutzler) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0440 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chinese President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xi Jinping\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the downward pressure on the economy is providing an opportunity for the government to pursue more market-based reforms. In written answers to questions submitted by The Wall Street Journal, Mr. Xi says investors should take the long-term view. With China's high household savings rate, consumer spending and urbanization, Mr. Xi says the economy can maintain 'medium-high growth' in the coming years. But he likens the Chinese economy to a large ship, saying that any vessel "may occasionally get unstable sailing on the high sea." (charles.hutzler@dowjones.com; @charleshutzler) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0439 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chinese President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xi Jinping\f28 \uc2 \f2  is defending his government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this summer to arrest the plunge in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stock markets. In written answers to questions submitted by The Wall Street Journal, Mr. Xi says the government moves were necessary to 'defuse systemic risks' and were not different than acts 'taken in some mature markets.' Thanks to those moves, he says, the markets have entered 'a stage of self-correction and adjustment.' Mr. Xi tossed aside the market gyrations, saying that developing capital markets remains a key goal of his reform agenda. (charles.hutzler@dowjones.com; @charleshutzler) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0438 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Investors shouldn't fuss over ups and downs in China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves following last month's devaluation of the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xi Jinping\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In a written interview with The Wall Street Journal, Mr. Xi says the devaluation is part of a process to make the yuan's exchange rate more market-based. He says there's 'no basis for sustained depreciation' of the yuan. The decline in what he called China's abundant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves represented normal capital flows. As the yuan becomes a more internationally used \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Mr. Xi says, the foreign reserves will increase and decrease, and, he says, 'there's no need to overreact to it.'(charles.hutzler@dowjones.com; @charleshutzler) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0426 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities advance in the morning session taking cues from global benchmarks on Monday, despite the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asian Development Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  cutting its economic outlook for the region and downgrading Chinese growth to 6.8% from 7.3% last year. Chinese stocks are also lifted as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Conference Board\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Leading Economic Index rises 1% to 334.4 in August. The improvement "stemmed primarily from bank loans and better performance in housing starts," says Andrew Polk, resident economist at The Conference Board China Center: "However, compared to a year ago, housing starts remain deeply entrenched in contractionary territory, suggesting no real improvement in the property market", he says, adding conditions may weaken in the months ahead. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index gains 0.7% to 21,913.70, while H-shares keep up with a 0.7% gain in the Shanghai Composite. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index rises 0.5% to 9,951.89. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0422 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Singapore's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says it will enhance safeguards for nonconventional investment products such as precious metal buy-back arrangements and collectively managed investment programs. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  says it aims to give the same regulatory protection to retail investors in nonconventional investments as available to capital markets investors. The regulator says it will also change rules on "accredited investors" who meet certain wealth or income thresholds and are accorded a lower level of regulatory protection as they are considered to be better able to protect their own interests. Such investors will now have the option to choose the level of regulatory safeguards that best suit their individual circumstances, the Authority says in a statement. Amendments to the Securities and Futures Act to implement these changes will be introduced in Parliament in 2016, it adds. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DAH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 22 Sep 2015 01:12 ET \par China Shares Rally on Stronger Sentiment -- -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0419 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The global surplus of crude oil is likely to persist despite the recent declines in US oil production and inventories, says Tim Evans, an energy analyst at Citi Futures. "\f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  apparently prompted some buying with the leak of an internal report forecasting a price recovery to the $80 level by 2020, but in our view that's more of a hope than a promise that \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  will be working to target that level," he notes. He points out that although \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  projects a daily output of 30.45 million barrels in 2016 -- a million barrels per day below \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's August 2015 production level -- there could still be a major supply overhang if the Iranian oil ban is lifted as expected. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  oil futures are down 42 cents at $46.26/bbl, Brent futures are down 35 cents at $48.57/bbl. (jenny.hsu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 22, 2015 01:12 ET (05:12 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150922eb9m000bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
