\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong Stocks Still Cheap Despite Cash Inflows Into City\b0\par\par\uc2 By Wei Gu   \par \uc2 853 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2015\par 10:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong stock market was dragged down by China over the summer, and even a massive flow of cash into the city hasn't been enough to bring it back from its lowest valuation in years.\par \par \uc2 Normally, the combination of low valuation and money inflows signals a good buying opportunity, said Chris Wood, a strategist at Asian brokerage firm CLSA. While the \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index has risen 6% in the last two weeks, it is still at its cheapest level since the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Much of the inflows are driven by people converting their Chinese yuan into Hong Kong dollars after the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's devaluation in August. Many expect the yuan, known officially as the renminbi, to weaken further next year, and Hong Kong is usually the first stop when mainland Chinese start moving money abroad.\par \par \uc2 "For mainland investors looking to diversify away from home-country renminbi assets, Hong Kong just happens to be the easiest outward step," said Michael Kurtz, chief Asia strategist at Nomura.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged to the U.S. dollar, so it has risen along with the greenback in recent months. The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been strengthening on the same path until Beijing devalued it by 2% in August.\par \par \uc2 The money flowing out of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has forced the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market at least seven times since Sept. 1, spending HK$47 billion ($6.1 billion) by selling local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buying dollars to keep the Hong Kong dollar from rising too far. The last time the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling Hong Kong dollars was in April, when the Hong Kong stock market rallied to its highest level since 2007.\par \par \uc2 "Lots of money has come to Hong Kong recently," said Alan Yandong Wang, investment director at Hong Kong-based investment firm \uc2 Value Partners\uc2 , which manages $10 billion in Hong Kong, China and Taiwan. "But instead of going into investments, it seems so far they have only parked here."\par \par \uc2 Despite the strong inflows into the city, money has been flowing out of Hong Kong equities because of a lack of confidence from global investors. The market has experienced 13 consecutive weeks of fund outflows, the longest period since 2010 when the data became available, according to EPFR Global, which tracks such flows. A total of $1 billion has flowed out of Hong Kong markets in the last three months.\par \par \uc2 After a 15% fall since April, the \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index now trades at 8.4 times its trailing earnings, just above the low of 7.69 reached during the global financial crisis in 2008, according to Wind, a data provider that tracks Chinese markets. Currently, Hong Kong's price-to-book ratio is just 1.03, even lower than the 1.25 hit in October 2008.\par \par \uc2 Cash moving into Hong Kong also typically flows into real estate. But the residential market has been weak with shrinking volumes as buyers expect the retail downturn and higher interest rates to squeeze the local economy and property demand.\par \par \uc2 Two things are keeping investors away from Hong Kong markets--the prospect of higher U.S. interest rates and the large number of Chinese companies listed in Hong Kong. Because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, the increase in U.S. rates expected by the end of the year will drive up Hong Kong rates. For Chinese companies, the slowdown in growth at home could hurt profits.\par \par \uc2 "Any rally in Hong Kong can be short-lived," said Alan Xi Wang, portfolio manager and head of Greater China equity at \uc2 Principal Global Investors\uc2 , an asset management firm that oversees more than $300 billion globally. Mr. Wang said he has limited his Hong Kong market exposure to defensive stocks.\par \par \uc2 Value Partners\uc2  is also taking a cautious approach to Hong Kong. At the end of August, its funds were mostly 90% to 95% invested, slightly lower than its normal ratio of 95% to 100%.\par \par \uc2 As global investors flee emerging markets, one group of investors is turning more positive about Hong Kong. Chinese investors are moving cash into Hong Kong via Shanghai through the link between stock exchanges. Those flows have totaled $2.4 billion over nine consecutive weeks of inflows, according to \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2 . This has helped narrow the price premium of dual-listed companies' China shares versus their Hong Kong shares to 31% from 41% two months ago.\par \par \uc2 "After all, Hong Kong stocks have deeper value than mainland stocks and the Hong Kong dollar is an alternative to renminbi downside risks," added Mr. Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite, despite having fallen 39% from its June peak, still trades at 13.3 times of trailing earnings and 1.6 times its book value. Francis Cheung, head of China and Hong Kong Strategy at brokerage firm CLSA, expects Shanghai stocks to fall another 15% from current levels.\par \par \uc2 Fiona Law contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to \uc2 Wei Gu\uc2  at wei.gu@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 21-09-15 0813GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150921eb9l0007e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\p