\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Offshore yuan is poster boy turned problem child\b0\par\par\uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2015\par 07:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By Andy Mukherjee\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 21 (Reuters Breakingviews) - It was a shining beacon of China's promise to usher in a convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But now the country's offshore yuan has become a lightning rod for capital outflows from the mainland.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traded in Hong Kong and known as the CNH, has in recent weeks been persistently weaker than its more tightly controlled onshore cousin. It is currently 0.5 percent cheaper against the U.S. dollar than the onshore yuan, or CNY. The gap between the two has been as wide as 1.7 percent since the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Aug. 11.\par \par \uc2 This isn't what China's economic planners had in mind. They have been promoting the offshore yuan as a way of stimulating the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international status, before easing capital controls at home. It has been a hit. In the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 ' last survey of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , daily turnover in the spot offshore market was 70 percent of the $20-billion-a-day onshore market.\par \par \uc2 This situation worked well as long as both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were rising in value against the U.S. dollar. Exporters' dollars went to the mainland where they swelled the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pile. Holders of offshore renminbi deposits and yuan-denominated dim sum bonds were happy owning assets in an appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Graphic: Mind the gap: Offshore vs. onshore yuan: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://reut.rs/1FoKlCh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://reut.rs/1FoKlCh}}}\par \par \uc2 Now the tide has turned. The offshore yuan is weaker because \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy is cooling while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Chinese importers have a greater incentive to sell renminbi onshore. The same goes for a small group of investors who can access both markets. That's putting pressure on the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to provide dollars from its kitty, which has shrunk by 11 percent since hitting almost $4 trillion in June 2014.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  had hoped its devaluation would align the onshore exchange rate more closely with its offshore cousin. Since that backfired, the leadership is left with two bad choices. It could choke CNH liquidity to kill speculation, but only at the risk of undermining China's ambition to join the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's elite \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  club. Failling to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, puts it under pressure to devalue again.\par \par \uc2 It isn't clear which option Beijing will choose. But as long as capital is looking to get out of China, the offshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was once a poster boy for reform will remain a problem child.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/andymukherjee70"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/andymukherjee70}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par CONTEXT NEWS\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The offshore Chinese yuan is currently trading about 0.5 percent weaker against the U.S. dollar than in the more tightly controlled mainland market. The gap between the CNH and the CNY, as the offshore and onshore Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are known, had ballooned to 1.7 percent after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  devalued the yuan's exchange rate on Aug. 11.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The CNH market, open to Chinese exporters, importers, offshore financial institutions and hedge funds, saw daily spot volumes of $13.9 billion in April 2013, according to the last triennial survey by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank for International Settlements\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In the same month, the CNY market witnessed $20 billion of average daily trading in the spot market. Forward trading in CNH had a daily turnover of $8.4 billion, compared with just $2.6 billion in CNY.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - On the morning of Sept. 21, the CNY was 6.3674 against the U.S. dollar, while CNH traded weaker at 6.3969. The bid-ask spread in CNY was as high as 50 basis points, compared with a spread of 20 pips in CNH, according to broker data on Eikon at 10:20 a.m. (GMT 0220) in Hong Kong. One pip, or price interest point, is 0.0001 for the dollar-yuan exchange rate. A higher bid-ask spread shows a less liquid market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - BIS study on CNY and CNH differential: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/1L04G3a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/1L04G3a}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 - Graphic: Mind the gap: Offshore vs. onshore yuan: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://reut.rs/1FoKlCh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://reut.rs/1FoKlCh}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on (Editing by Peter Thal Larsen and Katrina Hamlin) \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-21T06:51:19.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150921eb9l004ev\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pi