\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China banks' Aug net  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  sales jump to $127 bln, show outflows rising\b0\par\par\uc2 318 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2015\par 12:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Sept 17 (Reuters) - China's banks sold a net 807 billion yuan ($126.8 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of clients in August, up sharply from July's 174 billion yuan, reinforcing signs of increased capital outflows in the wake of a surprising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation.\par \par \uc2 Commercial banks sold a net $43.5 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements last month, taking into account their own foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases, data from the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE), the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator, showed on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Earlier data showed \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commercial banks sold a record 723.8 billion yuan ($113.69 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August on a net basis, highlighting how capital outflows intensified.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sold a net 318.4 billion yuan worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August.\par \par \uc2 The data, along with a record drop in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in August, added to signs of dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem a slide in the yuan.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan devaluation of nearly 2 percent on August 11 stoked market concerns about a sharper slowdown in the world's second-largest economy, fuelling a wave of capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 The SAFE said market participants were more willing to hold \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seeking to adjust their debt structure and making faster payments on imports.\par \par \uc2 The SAFE said it will step up monitoring of cross-border capital flows to prevent regional and systemic financial risks.\par \par \uc2 China will conduct checks on firms' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying to prevent speculation and step up a crackdown on illegal cross-border money transactions, an official at SAFE said on Thursday. ($1 = 6.3645 Chinese yuan renminbi) (Reporting by Kevin Yao and Winni Zhou; Editing by \uc2 Jacqueline Wong\uc2  and Muralikumar Anantharaman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-17T11:41:07.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150917eb9h00c6y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
