\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-China yuan bears get cold feet as \uc2 PBOC\uc2  steps up intevention, Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  pessimism eases\b0\par\par\uc2 920 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2015\par 07:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Yuan short positions more than halve on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Won bearish bets lowest since late June on S&P upgrade\par     * Singapore dlr short positions 2-mth low on safe-haven bids\par \par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Sept 17 - Bets on further weakness in China's\par yuan fell by more than half in the last two weeks as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stabilise the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after its\par surprise devaluation last month, a Reuters poll showed.\par     The steadier yuan, in turn, helped calm nerves in\par the rest of Asia, with sentiment toward other emerging market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  becoming less bearish despite dollar strength ahead\par of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's interest rate decision later on\par Thursday.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been frequently spotted\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the yuan since its Aug. 11 devaluation,\par not only in spot and forward markets but also in offshore\par markets. \par     China will conduct checks on firms' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying\par to prevent speculation, an official at the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator said on Thursday. \par     Beijing has scrambled to steady the yuan since the\par devaluation spurred heavy selling and capital outflows. As a\par result, its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves in August fell by a\par record.\par     Pessimism over other emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eased,\par according to the survey of 19 fund managers, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders\par and analysts conducted from Tuesday to Thursday.\par     Investors are focusing on whether the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par will announce an interest rate hike later on Thursday for the\par first time in almost a decade, and if the resulting increase in\par U.S. yields could prompt investors to pull more money out of\par emerging markets.\par     Short positions on the South Korean won's <KRW=KFTCfell to\par the lowest since late June after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised the\par country's sovereign credit rating and foreign investors turned\par to net buyers of Seoul shares after dumping equities in\par the previous 29 consecutive sessions. \par     Bearish bets on the Singapore dollar shrank to the\par smallest since early July on growing demand for safer assets as\par uncertainty over the Fed's plans and China's economic slowdown\par roiled global financial markets.\par     Though its economy is cooling along with the rest of Asia's,\par Singapore has a triple-A credit rating.\par     Singapore's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also found support after the ruling\par party swept a general election last week by a bigger margin than\par many had expected, ensuring the continuity of fiscal and\par economic policies. \par     Short positions on the Indian rupee were the\par smallest in the region peers as improved economic stability\par attracted bond inflows. \par     Bearish bets on the Malaysian ringgit's bearish \par fell to the lowest since late July, even as Swiss authorities\par froze funds in Swiss banks amid investigations into Malaysia's\par troubled state investment fund \f28 \uc2 \f2 1Malaysia Development Berhad\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par (1MDB).\par     Traders reduced short positions after an unexpected, albeit\par small, increase in Malaysia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves in the\par last two weeks of August. \par     Though reserves remained near the lowest levels since 2009,\par traders believe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spending less in trying to\par support the ringgit as the market thinned.\par     Short positions in the Taiwan dollar, the Thai baht\par  and the Philippine peso also fell to the\par smallest since July.\par     Bearish bets on the Indonesian rupiah eased to the\par smallest since early August, with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spotted\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The poll is focused on what analysts and fund managers\par believe are the current market positions in nine Asian emerging\par market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : the Chinese yuan, South Korean won, Singapore\par dollar, Indonesian rupiah, Taiwan dollar, Indian rupee,\par Philippine peso, Malaysian ringgit and the Thai baht.\par     The poll uses estimates of net long or short positions on a\par scale of minus 3 to plus 3. A score of plus 3 indicates the\par market is significantly long U.S. dollars.\par     The figures include positions held through non-deliverable\par forwards (NDFs).\par     The survey findings are provided below (positions\par in U.S. dollar versus each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ):\par \par  DATE     CNY   KRW   SGD   IDR   TWD   INR   MYR   PHP   THB\par  17-Sept  0.26  0.60  0.62  1.27  0.73  0.27  1.48  0.54  1.00\par  3-Sept   0.62  1.09  1.31  1.45  1.15  0.66  1.96  0.86  1.51\par  20-Aug   0.91  1.56  1.50  1.55  1.58  0.75  2.21  1.04  1.86\par  6-Aug   -0.15  1.27  1.19  1.16  0.91  0.11  1.82  0.84  1.40\par  23-July -0.50  1.32  0.97  0.69  0.70  0.09  1.24  0.52  1.18\par  9-July  -0.13  1.09  0.38  0.78  0.56 -0.06  1.33  0.37  0.80\par  25-June -0.70  0.29  0.22  0.80  0.11 -0.26  1.10  0.30  0.57\par  11-June -0.47  0.77  0.61  1.12  0.48  0.05  1.39  0.43  0.69\par  28-May  -0.62  0.67  0.53  1.05  0.02  0.29  0.86  0.03  0.89\par  14-May  -0.45  0.11 -0.05  0.95 -0.30  0.22  0.41 -0.06  0.78\par  30-Apr  -0.48 -0.55 -0.44  0.43 -0.46 -0.18 -0.13 -0.12  0.33\par  \par  (Additional reporting by Shaloo Shrivastava in BENGALURU;\par Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-17T06:43:43.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150917eb9h005u4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
