\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA MARKETS: Chinese Stocks Slump 3.5%, Drag Asia Mostly Lower\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng   \par \uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2015\par 11:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Stocks in China led most Asian markets lower Tuesday ahead of a decision on interest rates in the U.S., while the Nikkei Stock Average eked out a gain despite a tempered assessment of Japan's economy by its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index slid 3.5% to close at 3,005.17, adding to its 2.7% loss Monday. That pushed down shares elsewhere in the region, including Australia's \uc2 S&P\uc2 ASX\uc2  200 , which fell 1.5%, and the \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index , which closed down 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average was up as much as 2% earlier in the session but ended only 0.3% higher after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  disappointed some investors by saying it would stand pat on its current easing program.\par \par \uc2 The region was weakening again after the Shanghai benchmark eked out gains last week for the first time since mid-August and helped lift neighboring markets. While \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up its domestic market, a recent batch of disappointing Chinese data has spurred further selling.\par \par \uc2 "Interest in the (China) market has completely dried up," with trading volumes falling to seven-month lows, said David Welch, managing director at brokerage Reorient Financial.\par \par \uc2 Recent data showed that China's shift away from certain kinds of manufacturing has hurt global trade growth. For the third year in a row, the rate of growth in global trade is set to trail the already sluggish expansion of the world economy, according to data from the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  and projections from leading economists. That follows economic reports from Sunday that showed China's factory output and fixed-asset investment fell short of expectations in August.\par \par \uc2 "I think the markets are still very much on edge ahead of the Fed meeting," said Sean Callow, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac Banking Corp\uc2 . "There is some nervousness exported from China, when their markets came under pressure yesterday."\par \par \uc2 Opinion: What will happen when prices in China tumble, as they must? ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/what-will-happen-when-prices-in-china-tumble-as-they-must-2015-09-14"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/what-will-happen-when-prices-in-china-tumble-as-they-must-2015-09-14}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Analysts say investors are staying on the sidelines ahead of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's two-day meeting starting Wednesday that could end with the first rate increase in nearly a decade. Higher borrowing costs threaten firms with dollar debt, including those in Asia.\par \par \uc2 Shares in China were also lower Tuesday, as forced liquidation pressure dented investor confidence. China's securities regulator said late Monday it had closed more than 60% of illegal trading accounts and that the remaining accounts hold shares worth 187 billion yuan ($30 billion).\par \par \uc2 "These illegal funds may not influence the market given its total size, but concentrated deleveraging may add to volatility on the market in the short term," said Jacky Zhang, analyst at BOC International.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement after a two-day policy meeting that exports and industrial production "have recently been more or less flat," having said last month they were "picking up." It also reworded its main overall description of Japan's economy to say it has "continued to recover moderately, although exports and production are affected by the slowdown in emerging economies."\par \par \uc2 Hisao Matsuura, chief strategist at \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2 , said some had bet there would be more stimulus, only to liquidate their positions after the meeting's outcome.\par \par \uc2 Following the BOJ's decision, the U.S. dollar briefly fell to as low as Yen119.91 from Yen120.34.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take further action, possibly next month, as recent data have suggested Japan's economy remains in a rut, shrinking 1.2% in the April-June quarter. The Nikkei is down 14% from its late-June peak.\par \par \uc2 In Australia, Tuesday's selling came despite a party coup that brought Malcolm Turnbull to power as the fourth prime minister in just over two years. Turnbull is widely considered to be more business friendly than his predecessor and potentially a more significant change than usual for investors, according to Michael McCarthy, chief market strategist at CMC Markets in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Minutes this morning from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 's latest policy meeting conveyed a neutral tone on the outlook for interest rates. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered rates to a record low 2.0% in May to counter slowing economic growth and falling commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 Australia grew at its slowest pace in four years in the second quarter, and analysts warn the country might slide into recession for the first time in close to 25 years. Its stock market is down roughly 16% from the late April peak.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2015 05:22 ET (09:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150915eb9f000vy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
