\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Markets Brace for  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Decisions\b0\par\par\uc2 1,575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2015\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par                Latest   Change  %Change \par DJIA         16370.96  -62.13  -0.38% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2         4805.76  -16.58  -0.34% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P 500\f28 \uc2 \f2        1953.03   -8.02  -0.41% \par FTSE 100      6084.59  -33.17  -0.54% \par Nikkei Stock 18240.60 +274.90  +1.53% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2     21606.82  +44.92  +0.21% \par Kospi         1934.67   +3.21  +0.17% \par SGX Nifty     7855.50  -18.50  -0.23% \par (Sep) contract \par  \par           Latest    %Change \par USD/JPY  120.46-47  +0.19% \par Range    120.65-120.18 \par EUR/USD  1.1312-15  -0.04% \par Range    1.1324-1.1302 \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBOT\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wheat Sep $4.856 per bushel (Settlement Price) \par                    Last   Change \par Comex Gold        $1,107.70  +$4.40 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,102.60 - $1,108.80 \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude (NY)     $44.08  -$0.55 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks slipped after a renewed selloff in China, as investors awaited an interest-rate decision from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  later in the week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Stocks across the U.S., Europe and Asia declined, with many investors staying on the sidelines ahead of a Fed meeting that could end with the first rate increase in nearly a decade. The two-day meeting begins on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 While expectations for a rate increase at this week's Fed meeting have declined over the past month, many investors remain hesitant to place big bets ahead of Thursday's announcement, said \uc2 John Brady\uc2 , managing director at futures brokerage R.J. O'Brien.\par \par \uc2 "It's very quiet," he said. "We just have to wait now," he added.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty over the impact that an increase in U.S. rates would have on riskier assets has led to a burst of volatility in global markets in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 But that eased at the end of last week as investors became more confident the Fed was unlikely to raise its short-term rates as early as this week.\par \par \uc2 About 46% of economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal in September expected the Fed to raise rates this week, compared with 82% in an August survey. Analysts at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , who previously expected the Fed to raise rates this week, have now pushed back their call for a first increase in rates to March 2016.\par \par \uc2 Trading volumes in the U.S. were muted, with only 5.3 billion shares changing hands, well below this year's average of 6.7 billion. Monday marked the first the day of Rosh Hashana, the Jewish New Year holiday, which some traders said was a factor in the subdued trading volumes. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Volatility in China unsettled Asian markets Tuesday, with stocks in Shanghai deepening losses ahead of key decisions on monetary policy by central banks in Japan and the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai Composite Index fell 2.8% early in the trading day, adding to its 2.7% loss Monday. That pushed down shares elsewhere in the region, including Australia's S&P/ASX 200, which fell more than 1%, and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index down 0.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei Stock Average gained 1.5% as investors covered short bets ahead of comments from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  expected later today.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up its domestic market, a recent batch of disappointing Chinese data has spurred further selling. Volatility was returning after the Shanghai benchmark had eked out gains last week for the first time since mid-August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two economic reports on Sunday--factory output and fixed-asset investment--fell short of expectations, adding to concerns that China could struggle to reach its full-year growth target of 7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think the markets are still very much on edge ahead of the Fed meeting," said Sean Callow, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac Banking Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "There is some nervousness exported from China, when their markets came under pressure yesterday," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The weakening of the region's markets also comes after U.S. stocks slipped Monday, with many investors staying on the sidelines ahead of a \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  meeting that could end with the first rate-increase in nearly a decade. The two-day meeting begins on Wednesday U.S. time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the Nikkei Stock Average rose on Tuesday, as Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wraps up its own meeting later today. Analysts say investors have been extra cautious given that the bank could shed light on potential easing later in the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some market participants may be covering short positions before the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's outcome, said Yoshihiro Okumura, general manager of research at Chibagin Asset Management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Nikkei is down 14% from its late-June peak as of Monday's close, analysts expect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  to remain on hold for now. Some expect additional easing in October, given that the pace of Japan's economic recovery has been slow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If the [bank] went now, this month, they'd be criticized by a lot of people. There'd be a lot of blowback from the rank and file members of parliament," said CLSA's managing director of equity strategy, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Christopher Wood\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Japan is currently committed to raising the monetary base by 80 trillion yuan on an annual basis until it reaches its 2% inflation target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's stock market is down roughly 15% from its late April peak. A party coup late Monday installed former investment banker Malcolm Turnbull as the country's fourth prime minister in just over two years, exposing deep unease about the country's sharply slowing economy. Recent data showed the economy grew at its slowest pace in four years in the second quarter, amid fresh warnings that Australia might soon slide into recession, its first in close to 25 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier Tuesday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  released minutes from its recent meeting that showed a neutral tone on its outlook for interest rates. Interest rates were lowered to a record low of 2.0% in May as the RBA reacted to slowing economic growth and falling commodity prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Malaysian ringgit and Korean won slipped slightly but the Australian dollar was up 0.3% against the U.S. dollar, its strongest since the beginning of the month. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have gotten more volatile in recent weeks ahead of the Fed meeting, said Sean Callow, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac Banking Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market seems to be paying more attention to equities" where trading has been rocky.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Malaysian ringgit and Korean won slipped slightly but the Australian dollar was up 0.3% against the U.S. dollar, its strongest since the beginning of the month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the yuan's daily benchmark 0.1% stronger against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, but in the spot market, the yuan is down marginally against the U.S. dollar, trading at 6.3697 from its Monday close of 6.3679. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices dipped in Asian trade on Tuesday. At 0151 GMT, spot gold was trading at $1,107.61/oz, down from the opening price of $1,108.46/oz.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper futures fell Monday as weaker-than-expected economic data out of China fanned fears about slowing growth in the world's largest consumer of the metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper futures for the most-actively traded December contract settled down 4.8 cents, or 2%, at $2.4055 a pound on the Comex division of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Copper prices have fallen nearly 15% this year as China's economy has showed increasing signs of slowing growth. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude-oil futures rose in early Asian trade Tuesday after losses in the previous session that saw Brent crude fall by nearly 4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0227 GMT, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  oil futures were up 37 cents at $44.37/bbl, Brent crude was up 27 cents at $46.64/bbl.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices slid Monday on fresh indications that the war for customers in the global crude market hasn't abated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Light, sweet crude for October delivery recently traded down 61 cents, or 1.4%, at $44.02 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent, the global benchmark, fell $1.1, or 2.1%, to $47.13 a barrel on ICE Futures Europe. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par  \par Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Sees Signs Mortgage Lending Curbs Working \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 GM\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 UAW\f28 \uc2 \f2  Agree to Extend Contract, Continue Bargaining \par North Korea Says It Is in 'Final Phase' of Developing New Satellite \par Worries Rise Over Global Trade Slump \par Hong Kong Jeweler Targets Gemstone Sales in Mainland China to Drive Growth \par Malcolm Turnbull Ousts Australian Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tony Abbott \par Alibaba\f28 \uc2 \f2  Affiliate Invests in China Insurance Unit of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cathay Financial\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Chinese Meat Processor to Take 50% Stake in New Zealand's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Silver Fern Farms\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Banking Regulator, 4 Banks Reach Pact Over New Chat Platform \par Apple: Preorders Strong for New iPhones \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 GM\f28 \uc2 \f2  Well-Positioned in China Despite Slower Demand, CEO Says \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Seven West Media\f28 \uc2 \f2  Unveils Buyback Plan After Shares Fall \par Iraq Warns Oil Companies of Spending Cuts \par Porsche Unveils Prototype Battery-Driven Sports Car \par Petrobras Chairman Taking Leave of Absence Until Nov 30 \par Worries Rise Over Global Trade Slump \par Manufacturing May Throw Wrench Into Fed's Plans \par Lower Diamond Prices Could Be a Lasting Affair \par Former \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard Chartered Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2  Chief Joins Deutsche Bank Board \par Gulf Oil Price War Continues as China, Supply Concerns Persist \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 14, 2015 23:00 ET (03:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150915eb9f0007z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
