\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-China grabs unused funds to spend on new projects as growth slows\b0\par\par\uc2 929 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2015\par 15:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales figures, Beijing seizes unspent local govt budget)\par \par \uc2 * Local government underspend irks authorities\par \par \uc2 * Beijing turns increasingly to stimulus to spur growth\par \par \uc2 * CSI300 ends down 1.97 pct, Shanghai Composite Index -2.67 pct\par \par \uc2 * Traders suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed-asset investment, factory output data below expectations\par \par \uc2 By Benjamin Kang Lim and Samuel Shen\par \par \uc2 BEIJING/SHANGHAI, Sept 14 (Reuters) - Chinese authorities have seized up to 1 trillion yuan ($157 billion) from local governments who failed to use their budget allocations, sources said, as Beijing looks for ways to spend its way out of an economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 The exclusive Reuters report came after China's stocks fell following data suggesting economic growth was running below the 2015 target level of about 7 percent, heightening concerns about the health of the world's second largest economy.\par \par \uc2 "China's economy faces relatively big downward pressure, so investor sentiment remains weak," said Gu Yongtao, strategist at Cinda Securities.\par \par \uc2 Two sources close to the government said budget funds repossessed from local governments would be used to pay for other investments.\par \par \uc2 The huge underspend, linked to officials' reluctance to splash out on big-ticket projects while authorities crack down on corruption, supports the argument of some economists that Chinese state investment has grown too slowly this year.\par \par \uc2 "In the past, local governments had asked for the money. Money was given, but no one acted," said one of the two sources.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, China's powerful economic planner, the \uc2 National Development and Reform Commission\uc2  (NDRC), said it had approved feasibility studies for two road projects worth a total of 6.2 billion yuan ($973.65 million).\par \par \uc2 Last week, the NDRC gave the green light for railway, highway and bridge projects worth a combined $23 billion, in a sign authorities are focusing on infrastructure spending rather than deeper reforms to shore up growth in the short term.\par \par \uc2 MORE LOSSES FOR STOCKS\par \par \uc2 China's stock markets have been on a roller-coaster ride since June, falling close to 40 percent and prompting frantic efforts by authorities to restore confidence.\par \par \uc2 Still, at their peak this year, they were up more than 150 percent compared with the lows of 2014.\par \par \uc2 A surprise devaluation of the yuan in August further roiled markets, underlining concerns the economy was weaker than previously thought and forcing China to burn through its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable.\par \par \uc2 A flurry of economic data in the past week has fed those concerns and prompted Premier \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  to try to reassure markets that China is on track to meet its main economic growth targets. The government has said it expects GDP growth of around 7 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Price data pointed to increased deflationary pressure and lower-than-expected industrial output and investment figures this weekend raised further doubts.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, the economy is very weak and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to continue cutting interest rates and banks' reserve requirement," said Zhou Hao, senior economist at \uc2 Commerzbank AG\uc2  in Singapore, adding he thought growth would dip below 7 percent in the July-September quarter.\par \par \uc2 China's benchmark CSI300 index of the biggest listed stocks in Shanghai and Shenzhen closed down 1.97 percent, while the Shanghai Composite Index dropped 2.67 percent.\par \par \uc2 China CSI300 stock index futures fell, some by as much as 7 percent, underlining investor scepticism in the stock market's upside potential.\par \par \uc2 REFORMS TO 'ZOMBIE' COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 Government plans announced at the weekend on restructuring of state-owned enterprises (SOEs), including allowing private investment, appeared to offer little cheer for investors.\par \par \uc2 The mammoth task could involve some 25,000 enterprises owned and managed by local governments and more than 100 managed centrally under the State-owned Assets Supervision and Administration Commission, or SASAC.\par \par \uc2 "The plan has long been expected," said Cinda's Gu. "So interest toward the theme could be short-lived."\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \uc2 Zhang Xiwu\uc2 , deputy head of SASAC, told a news briefing that China would centralise state-owned capital in key industries, while restricting state investment in industries not in line with national policies.\par \par \uc2 "We will make more efforts in reforming 'zombie enterprises', long-time loss-making enterprises and in disposing of those low-efficient and non-performing assets," Zhang said.\par \par \uc2 The yuan erased some early losses following suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  via state-owned banks, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was changing hands at 6.3692 per dollar in the spot market onshore, marginally higher than the previous close.\par \par \uc2 The price spread between the offshore and onshore yuan markets remained narrow, indicating overseas investors were heeding the indirect warning delivered by China last week, when state-owned banks, appearing to act on behest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , massively bid up the yuan in offshore markets in London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The offshore market continues to price in a slight discount however, suggesting expectations persist that the yuan will fall.\par \par \uc2 Reuters calculations based on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data released on Monday showed that China sold a record net 723.8 billion yuan ($113.69 billion) in August, showing the price the country is paying to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling.\par \par \uc2 "It also shows that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the coming months as depreciation expectation is still there," said Shen Jianguang, an economist at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2  in Hong Kong. (Additional reporting by Pete Sweeney; Writing by Neil Fullick; Editing by Rachel Armstrong and Mike Collett-White) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-14T14:46:48.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150914eb9e008ip\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
