\par\par \b \uc2 China Unveils Overhaul of Bloated State Sector\b0\par\par\uc2 1,488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2015\par 02:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING\u8212\'20\'14Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  set in motion the overhaul of the country's bloated state companies just ahead of his U.S. visit, in a bid to show Beijing is committed to change even as the economy slows.\par \par \uc2 A plan released Sunday, however, represents a modest fix to China's brand of state capitalism. It attempts to enhance state companies' returns by letting them add private investors. It also tries to improve state companies' competitiveness overseas by making them bigger, though that risks creating more inefficiency and further stymieing private enterprise.\par \par \uc2 Crucially, the plan doesn't specify whether underperforming state firms will be allowed to fail, a measure economists say would be critical to its success.\par \par \uc2 The plan reflects Mr. Xi's belief, according to officials familiar with his thinking, that the state sector, rather than taking a back seat, should be improved to continue to play a dominant role in the Chinese economy.\par \par \uc2 "Xi never had the intention to weaken the state's role in the economy, and the plan reflects that," said a government adviser.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Xi is struggling to maintain growth while trying to push ahead with overhauls that could squeeze the economy in the short run. Many of the country's big state companies are in industries that have suffered persistent overcapacity, such as steel and coal mining, and a lot of them are hoarding credit and other resources to the detriment of both private firms and consumers.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, data showed China's factory output and fixed-asset investment were both weaker than expected in August, highlighting the growing challenge for the government in reaching its full-year growth target of about 7%.\par \par \uc2 Disagreement among the government agencies involved in the drafting of the state-sector overhaul led to delays and revisions several times, people with knowledge of the process say.\par \par \uc2 One draft, submitted by the commission that oversees the largest state companies in China, was rejected by Mr. Xi earlier this year because he didn't think it went far enough to give the market greater sway, the people said.\par \par \uc2 "In the end, it's a compromise among different interest groups," the adviser said. "But it's still a step in the right direction."\par \par \uc2 The blueprint was released jointly by the Communist Party and the State Council, China's cabinet, on Sunday. Some details of it were reported by The Wall Street Journal on Sept. 7, including the fact that even though the plan calls for more private participation in state firms, it still emphasizes the Communist Party's control over the companies.\par \par \uc2 "In the process of deepening reforms of state-owned enterprises, the leadership of the party can only be strengthened, not weakened," said Zhang Yi, head of the State-Owned Assets Supervision and Administration Commission, or Sasac, in an official statement Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Officials with knowledge of Mr. Xi's thinking say his views on the role of state companies were partly shaped by his time as a top provincial official. In the 2000s, when Mr. Xi served as the Communist Party secretary of eastern Zhejiang province, he was struck by how a vibrant private sector had turned the coastal area into one of the wealthiest regions in the country, these officials said. At the same time, his dismay with state-owned enterprises, which that for years had been mired in the red, increased.\par \par \uc2 Then, in 2007, after he went on to run Shanghai and saw the successful transformation of some of the big state companies in the city, including \uc2 SAIC Motor Corp\uc2 ., now China's largest auto maker by sales, Mr. Xi arrived at a new view, according to the officials: State companies should continue to be the pillar of China's economy.\par \par \uc2 In SAIC, whose two subsidiaries were listed on the \uc2 Shanghai Stock Exchange\uc2  when Mr. Xi was running Shanghai, he saw the benefits of diversified ownership in state companies and having the market serve as a gate keeper of those companies, the officials say. The entire company of \uc2 SAIC Motor\uc2  went public in 2012 and has been one of the most profitable companies owned by the city government. It has joint ventures with many foreign car makers, including \uc2 General Motors Co\uc2 . and Germany's \uc2 Volkswagen AG\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That view formed the basis for the plan released Sunday, officials say. The central design of the plan is a departure from the approach of Mr. Xi's predecessors, who encouraged local experiments that could be given a broader trial if successful. The broad blueprint for overhauling state companies will be followed by more detailed rules to be issued by various government agencies in the coming months, officials say.\par \par \uc2 The plan gives no definitive time frame for reforms but says "decisive results" in reforming state companies must be made by 2020.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, China has moved in fits and starts to restructure its vast state sector. In the 1990s, Premier Zhu Rongji closed thousands of state-owned firms. But little has been done to improve the way state companies are run. Returns in the state sector have consistently lagged behind those of their private brethren.\par \par \uc2 Reform of state companies should be "the top priority" for the Chinese government, said Chen Xingdong, chief China economist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . But when it comes to how forceful and effective the new measures will be, Mr. Chen said, "I'm concerned."\par \par \uc2 Concerns over weakening Chinese growth are a top item on the agenda for Mr. Xi's visit to the U.S., along with contentious issues such as alleged Chinese cyberattacks and island-building in the South \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sea. Beijing's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the country's stock market as well as its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have led to questions over the Chinese leadership's commitment to giving market forces a greater role in the economy.\par \par \uc2 When drawing up the plan, some officials, including those at the Finance Ministry, looked at Singapore's holding company for state firms, \uc2 Temasek Holdings\uc2 , according to people close to the process. Under the \uc2 Temasek\uc2  model, the government would confine itself to the role of a stakeholder, receiving dividends but leaving day-to-day running of the companies to professional managers at the asset-investment firms hired at market rates.\par \par \uc2 The plan unveiled Sunday doesn't fully hew to the \uc2 Temasek\uc2  model, in part because of fierce opposition from some corners of the government, including from officials at Sasac who are concerned about seeing their roles diminished, according to officials close to the process. The plan calls for better supervision of the state companies by corporate boards, but the government will continue to appoint senior managers of the firms.\par \par \uc2 Officials at the Finance Ministry and Sasac couldn't be reached to comment Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 The document doesn't specify which industries will be targeted for consolidation. But people with knowledge of the government's thinking have listed the energy, resources and telecommunications sectors as ones marked for mergers. The merged entities would then be reorganized as asset-investment firms, with a mandate to make sure they run more like commercial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  than arms of the government. Upper management will be under orders to maximize returns and prepare many of the companies for listing on stock markets, these people say.\par \par \uc2 Still, the plan released Sunday stresses the need to "avoid the losses of state assets" during the diversification process. "That means nobody dares to privatize state companies in any big way," said an official in southwestern China's Guizhou province.\par \par \uc2 The reform plan also leaves open the question whether the government would choose to close down underperformers. State firms have thrived on access to loans from state banks and government-set rules that limit competition from private businesses, which complain of being muscled out of the market unfairly. Economists have said the special status has come at a cost to Chinese taxpayers and consumers. Huang Yiping, a \uc2 Peking University\uc2  professor of economics and a member of the monetary policy committee of \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said there are many "zombie" state companies in northeastern China, known as China's Rust Belt. "When they fail, they need to exit."\par \par \uc2 The plan encourages private funds to join forces in investing in projects with state companies in industries ranging from oil, natural gas, electricity, transportation and other public-services areas. But it reaffirms the government's controlling stakes in those strategic sectors.\par \par \uc2 Some local governments have started selling stakes in some of their companies to private investors. Last year, the Shanghai government sold a 12.4% stake in \uc2 Shanghai Jin Jiang International Hotels\uc2  (Group) Co. for about $488 million to \uc2 Hony Capital\uc2 , a Chinese private-equity firm. Executives at Hony expect the deal to encourage better management practices at Jin Jiang.\par \par \uc2 Grace Zhu\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 13, 2015 20:25 ET (00:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150914eb9e0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
