\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China c.bank ups flexibility in reserve requirement rules\b0\par\par\uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2015\par 13:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comment, detail, background)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Sept 11 (Reuters) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is making its reserve requirement rules more flexible for banks to reduce the risk of an abrupt tightening in liquidity in the world's second-largest economy as it cools.\par \par \uc2 The amount of deposits that banks must set aside as reserves at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon be regulated on an average basis as opposed to current daily assessments, the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 said in a statement on its website.\par \par \uc2 The change, effective Sept. 15, will help banks combat sudden funding pressures at a time of heightened volatility in the yuan that has driven capital from China.\par \par \uc2 In two online statements, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said the new rule would allow banks to set aside less reserves when they are strapped for funds, but will not be a free pass for lenders to lapse into overdrafts.\par \par \uc2 Under the changes, banks can report a daily reserve requirement ratio (RRR) that is up to 100 basis points lower than the rate set by the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 , but their daily average RRR in the assessed period cannot fall under the required level.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  did not state the period over which banks' RRR will be assessed.\par \par \uc2 "It could release a certain amount of liquidity when there is a shortfall," the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said of the rule, but added it would not affect the market now as there was ample liquidity.\par \par \uc2 The RRR is a main monetary policy tool in China, used to manage liquidity.\par \par \uc2 As China's economy eases towards its slackest growth in 25 years, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has lowered the RRR three times this year by a total of 200 basis points to bolster bank lending.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  sets different RRR requirements for banks depending on their size and type of loans. The RRR for the largest banks is around 18 percent, but the exact level for each bank is not disclosed.\par \par \uc2 "This helps to reduce volatility in liquidity," said Li Qilin, an analyst at Minsheng Securities in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 "The (yuan) exchange rate is under pressure to depreciate and money flows are very volatile, which could affect the cash positions (of banks)," Li said. "This helps smooth it out."\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market experienced its most volatility in a decade this year when the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  unexpectedly devalued the yuan by around 2 percent in August, saying it would deepen reforms.\par \par \uc2 Faced with suspicion China may have started a round of competitive devaluations, which authorities deny, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the yuan.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffered a bruising credit crunch in June 2013 when short-term interest rates shot as high as 30 percent after the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  allowed the money market to convulse to force banks to curtail risky lending. (Reporting by Kevin Yao, Shao Xiaoyi and Koh Gui Qing; Editing by Robert Birsel) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-11T12:26:47.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150911eb9b00c1y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
