\par\par \b \uc2 China Shares up as Brokers up Margin Account Selloff -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,564 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2015\par 05:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0334 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Chinese shares edged up despite heavy selloff pressure as brokers scrambled to clear illegal margin accounts, indicating recovering market sentiment, say analysts. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index gained 0.1% at 3201.07, with nearly 700 stocks winning. The Shenzhen Composite Index rose 0.8% at 1784.41, while the ChiNext board gained 1.4% at 2068.14. "The short-selling forces have been restricted heavily, limiting the downward pressure, but investors also worry about intensified selloffs due to unwinding margin accounts," said Tang Yonggang, analyst at Shenwan Hongyuan Securities. Turnover shrank to roughly 610 billion yuan Thursday on the A-share market, a sign that the market may bottom out soon, added Tang. (yifan.xie@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0323 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The South Korean won's recent weakness versus the dollar will provide some tailwind for \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) and \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE), partly offsetting their weak sales in China, says Nomura analyst Angela Hong. She adds a 1% depreciation of the won versus the greenback will raise \uc2 Kia\uc2 's operating profit by 2%-2.5% and \uc2 Hyundai\uc2 's by 1.5%-2%. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen 9% against the dollar so far this year. \uc2 Kia\uc2  is more sensitive to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate as exports from Korea represent 40% of its global shipments, higher than 24% at \uc2 Hyundai\uc2 . Shares of \uc2 Kia\uc2  fall 0.2% at 51,000 won. \uc2 Hyundai\uc2  is up 1% at 158,000 won. The broader Korean market is down 0.7%.(in-soo.nam@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0259 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Crude oil prices are up in Asian trade Friday after data showed that U.S. crude oil output fell last week to the lowest level since January. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Energy Information Administration\f28 \uc2 \f2  reported that the crude oil output slipped 83,000 barrels a week to 9.14 million last week. However, U.S. stockpiles continued to rise due to lower refinery utilisation, an ANZ report says. A glut of oil due to surplus production has weighed on oil prices though prices have risen a little since the last week of August. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  oil futures are up 6 cents to $45.77/bbl, Brent rose 19 cents to $48.83/bbl. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0250 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Gold prices rose marginally in Asian trade Friday from a month's low. Physical demand from Asia is moderate with slow Indian buying ahead of the start of the festival season this month. Scanty monsoon rains have hit incomes of farmers, who account for bulk of the gold purchases in India. Chinese safe haven buying that followed a period of extreme turbulence in stock markets last month has also eased. A firm dollar is also acting as a headwind to gold prices. Spot gold is trading at $1,112.51/oz, up from the opening price of $1,110.77/oz. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0250 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Investors will likely see such U.S. indicators as August PPI and September \f28 \uc2 \f2 University of Michigan\f28 \uc2 \f2  consumer survey later today. But amid speculation over the Fed rate decision ahead of the FOMC Sept. 17, or the biggest market focus, investors may play down each of these indicators, says Kumiko Ishikawa, analyst at Gaitame.Com Research Institute. "While watching stocks and yields, the euro may gain upside momentum if the gold-cross is formed," she says. The pair's 20-day moving average is about to break above the 200-day moving average to form so-called bullish "golden cross" patterns. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0239 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Vietnam rice production is expected to get a lift from a slight increase in paddy volume in 2015 despite El Nino, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Department of Agriculture\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note. "The increase of production of autumn crop in the Mekong River Delta also offsets the slight decrease of production of total Vietnam's spring crop, and mild decrease of autumn crop in other areas of Southern Vietnam." It adds that the south central coastland areas of Vietnam have been impacted by strong drought conditions caused by El Nino. However, it adds that local corn production is expected to fall due to a drought in the Central Highland that has reduced yields. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @lucy_craymer) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0232 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The offshore yuan pares some gains early Friday after sharply rebounding as much as 1.6% late Thursday. There was speculation that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the offshore market to prop up the yuan as Chinese banks' Hong Kong branches were heavily selling the U.S. dollar and buying the yuan. "It is opposite to the recent client flows with general expectation of further depreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," says a senior trader at a local bank in Hong Kong. "The guess is the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  hopes to narrow the spread between the yuan trading onshore and offshore, as (the dollar buying) has stopped when the gap was largely closed." The offshore USD/CNH currently stands at 6.4, after hitting as low as 6.3772 overnight. The USD/CNH was as high as 6.4821 Thursday in Asia, while the onshore pair--USD/CNY--is trading steadily at 6.37-6.38 in recent sessions. (fiona.law@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0212 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Gold rebounded slightly from near a month's low in Asian trade Friday as a firm dollar and investor outflows continued to keep a lid on prices. The precious metal has now retraced almost all of its gains seen last month post the yuan's devaluation that sparked a rush of safe haven buying. A fall in equity markets overnight in the U.S. did not lead to a fresh bout of gold purchases. The precious metal is trading at $1,112.47/oz, up from the opening price of $1,111.06/oz. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0211 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Last-ditch efforts are being made to resuscitate the Trans-Pacific Partnership free trade talks that collapsed in July. On Wednesday, talks resumed in Washington between Japan, U.S., Mexico and Canada to iron out discords on auto trade, a key sticking issue. The group's goal is to reach a broad TPP-wide agreement over the next month or so. For that to happen, Japan's top TPP negotiator Akira Amari said Friday, preparatory talks need to complete within next week. (mitsuru.obe@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0208 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Without a meaningful cut in supply, the manganese market faces a persistent glut through at least 2017, says Macquarie. After downgrading its forecasts for future steel production, the bank says it sees a weaker outlook for the commodity, which currently derives 95% of its demand from steelmakers and is already being used less intensively per ton of steel than it has been in the past. Now, "we see virtually no demand growth over the next five years," Macquarie says. "A medium-term turnaround in U.S.-dollar pricing will thus be dependent on mining industry-wide deflationary pressures abating, supply cuts materializing and a steeping of the cost curve," it says. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @RhiannonHoyle) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0202 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees 10 countries that are the most vulnerable to rare but calamitous natural disasters--those that can be expected once every 250 years--and which can affect sovereign ratings. The 10 countries are Bangladesh, Chile, Costa Rica, Dominican Republic, Japan, Peru, Taiwan, Thailand, Turkey and Vietnam. Sovereign ratings in Latin America and the Caribbean are most at risk, followed by those in Asia. Chile and Japan are among those most affected by a 1-in-250-year earthquake, and Bangladesh and Dominican Republic most at risk of tropical storm-induced downgrades. Potential downgrade could be as much as 2.5 notches in the case of Dominican Republic. \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  says catastrophe insurance can help lessen the economic and ratings impact of natural disasters. However, emerging and developing sovereigns, typically the most vulnerable to natural disasters, generally have low insurance coverage. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0146 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Voting is underway in Singapore in a general election that poses the broadest challenge to the ruling People's Action Party since the city-state gained independence 50 years ago. Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lee Hsien Loong\f28 \uc2 \f2  is hoping to reverse a setback at the 2011 election, when his party received a record-low 60.1% share of the popular vote, though it held on to 81 of the then 87 parliamentary seats. Discontent over the government's immigration policies and the rising cost of living in Singapore, one of the world's most expensive cities, contributed to the ruing party's fall in vote share. These issues have continued to dominate political discussion here, and a slowing economy has added to the pressure on Lee's administration. Voting closes at 8 p.m. local time (8 a.m. ET) and a final count is expected early Saturday. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 10, 2015 23:34 ET (03:34 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150911eb9b00059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
