\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Onshore, Offshore Yuan Near Parity: Did PBoC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2 ? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2015\par 03:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Overseas investors have been betting that the yuan will slide further.\par \par \uc2 Offshore yuan, which is not limited by the 2% trading band from the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 's daily fix rate, was trading as high as 6.54 early this week, while the onshore yuan, which is limited, was hovering at around 6.38.\par \par \uc2 But things changed all of a sudden. Offshore yuan rose sharply higher in late trading yesterday afternoon and stays firm at 8.3858, only 86 pips higher than the onshore yuan of 7.3772.\par \par \uc2 Triggering the sharp rise in offshore yuan was a Reuters article that China would now allow more offshore yuan inflow:\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 has increased the quota for net yuan inflows under a cross-border pooling scheme that enables companies to transfer the yuan between their onshore and offshore entities freely, two sources told Reuters on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has a formula to calculate how much funds are allowed to enter China on a net basis, and the coefficient it uses was increased from 0.1 to 0.5, said the sources with direct knowledge of the matter. Essentially, the move suggests the amount of net inflows will increase to five times the earlier amount.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the same time lowered the threshold of companies that can participate in the scheme.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg also reported that China will open its onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to foreign central banks.\par \par \uc2 Last night, in China's vibrant finance chat rooms, there was widespread speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the offshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Could it be just short covering though? Seeing yuan lower in the long-term, there have been a lot of arbitrage shorts on offshore versus onshore. Yesterday could just be a huge unwind. There were also stop loss trades, according to a source familiar with the matter. At this week's \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  meeting in Dalian, Premier \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  said China would not resort to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war.\par \par \uc2 See also Bloomberg article: " China Said to Take Rare Step of Bolstering Yuan's Offshore Value". \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 10, 2015 21:02 ET (01:02 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150911eb9b0006k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
