\par\par \b \uc2 China's Yuan Hasn't Dropped as Far as You Think Against the Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Davis   \par \uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2015\par 17:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 When China devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting Aug. 11, the financial world trembled. Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quick to point out that the devaluation wasn't aimed at aiding China's beleaguered exporters and wasn't the start of a long-term slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many investors were skeptical.\par \par \uc2 The devaluation, though, has turned out so far to be pretty much a one-time event--and the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to make sure the slide is limited.\par \par \uc2 On a nominal basis, the yuan has fallen by about 2.9% against the dollar since the devaluation. But \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  researcher Karim Foda, at the request of Real Time Economics, did some calculations to take into account the different levels of inflation in the U.S. and China.\par \par \uc2 When you adjust for inflation, the yuan has fallen just 1.5% against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Here are his calculations for U.S. and other nations, too.\par \par \uc2 The modest devaluation thus far doesn't mean that it will continue. For now, Beijing wants a very stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That's because Communist Party chief \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is coming to Washington for a state visit on Sept. 24 and wants to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off the agenda as much as possible, said China specialists. Afterwards, the Chinese may feel far less pressure to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in line.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  has urged the Chinese for years to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float according to market demands. China may yet take the Treasury up on that request--especially now when the market is pressing for further devaluation.\par \par \uc2 "They are doing what they have been told to do," said \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  economist Eswar Prasad, a former \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  China expert. He said the Chinese profess "bafflement" that the U.S. would object if the yuan fell in value over time.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 10-09-15 1557GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150910eb9a000fq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
