\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Australian Equities Roundup -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 4,791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2015\par 07:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0453 GMT -- The deputy governor of \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2 , has moved to calm nerves shaken by the volatility in China's stock market. As a major exporter of commodities to China, Australia watches closely for tumult or upheaval there, and the market's gyrations have raised fear levels for some, evident in recent consumer- and business-confidence readings. "A bubble developed. And then it burst," Mr. Lowe said. "While this attracted much attention, these movements in the Chinese equity market are likely to have only limited implications for the overall Chinese economy. It is noteworthy, though, that this increased volatility in equity and commodity markets has not led to dislocation in other financial markets. In particular, there has not been a deterioration in funding markets and credit spreads have mostly moved in a relatively narrow range." (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT -- For years now, \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been jawboning the Australian dollar. The case for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  became stronger in the past year as commodity prices fell sharply. But after a fall of more than 25% against the U.S. dollar in the last year, the RBA appears satisfied, saying at levels below US$0.7000, it is now supporting the economy. Deputy Governor of \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2 , says a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is key to growth. "When the terms of trade initially started to fall and the decline in mining investment was clearly in sight, the exchange rate was relatively slow to adjust. On a number of occasions, the RBA drew attention to this. But the exchange rate has now adjusted considerably," Lowe says at a business event in Melbourne. "Just as the appreciation helped stabilize the economy in the upswing of the boom in commodity prices and mining investment, the depreciation is helping in the down swing," he adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT -- \uc2 Virgin Australia\uc2  (VAH.AU) is losing share of the non-peak hours domestic travel market to \uc2 Qantas\uc2 's (QAN.AU) budget carrier Jetstar, a development that could keep growth in revenue per available seat kilometer, or RASK, measured over the near term, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . The broker cuts Virgin's stock to an 'Underweight,' noting that it sees the stock's valuation getting expensive: "At (circa) 6x EBITDAR [earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation, amortization and rent], VAH looks full relative to QAN at 3.6x, particularly with signs Jetstar is taking non peak share." (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT -- The era of sharply rising house prices in Sydney and other Australian cities could be over soon, thinks Katie Hill, an economist at \uc2 Australia and New Zealand Banking Group\uc2 . She says bank lending to housing investors slowed in July and will likely continue to moderate in the second half of the year in response to tighter housing market regulation. Meanwhile, it seems unlikely that borrowing by owner occupiers will accelerate to offset this slowdown in investor lending, as houses are becoming less and less affordable for ordinary home buyers. "Auction clearance rates in Sydney, which has been the epicenter of investor housing lending, are also weakening. Overall, this should flow through into slowing house prices growth in 2016," says Hill. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT -- Analysts at brokerage firm Baillieu Holst have cut their 12-month target for Australia's benchmark share index S&P/ASX 200 to 6,100 from 6,500 points due to the worsening outlook for global growth and the domestic Australian economy. Despite lowering the target, they think that the recent market selloff has gone too far and argue that record-low interest rates still make equities more attractive than government bonds. "Recent profit taking by global investors on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries has brought the market down to the fair value level of 5000 with no premium being paid for the substantially high dividend yield," says analyst Mathan Somasundaram. The S&P/ASX 200 was trading at 5199.4 points Wednesday afternoon. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0339 GMT -- Fresh signs that consumers continue to lose confidence in the Australian economy add to the view that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  may be forced to lower interest rates further in coming months. "The weak readings for confidence are consistent with our view that the Australian economy will need more help from further RBA rate cuts and a further decline in the value of the Australian dollar," says Shane Oliver, chief economist at AMP Capital in Sydney. He says confidence has been hit by a string of gloomy economic data reports in recent days and rising uncertainty following a selloff in global share markets that was sparked by fears over a sharp slowdown in China. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0337 GMT -- \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says \uc2 Qantas\uc2  (QAN.AU) is trading at its biggest discount to its historical 12-month forward multiple versus margin relationship in 10 years. "We see scope for a (circa) 40% re-rate, with near-term catalysts being: 1) evidence of EPS sustainability and 2) a further A$1 billion plus of capital returns in the coming 12 months," the broker says. It keeps an 'Overweight' rating and A$5.40 price target. The stock recently traded at A$3.575. (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 0337 GMT -- Cheap water has hurt \uc2 Coca-Cola Amatil\uc2  (CCL.AU) in Australia in recent years, helping Asahi and supermarket labels steal beverage market share. Now, the trend has hit New Zealand, with \uc2 Woolworths\uc2  introducing a 24-pack of its Signature brand last November. Private labels have since grown from 18% to 32% of the water category, which has captured virtually all the growth in non-alcoholic ready-to-drink beverages in supermarkets. Still, investors needn't be alarmed, says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . When the trend hit in Australia in 2011, \uc2 Coca-Cola Amatil\uc2  was virtually non-existent in supermarket water. In New Zealand, it has the highest volume share around 28%. Also, number two player Frucor is less likely to initiate a value-oriented strategy as Asahi did in Australia, because it is a higher-margin business. Importantly, a wiser \uc2 Coca-Cola Amatil\uc2  anticipated the trend, starting to cut prices of its Kiwi Blue and Pump water brands late 2013.(rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT -- Australia's mortgage market appears to be shifting back into balance, says Shane Oliver, chief economist at AMP Capital in Sydney following new official housing finance data released Wednesday. The figures showed that the value of bank loans to owner occupiers (excluding refinancing) rose by 1.7% to A$12,983 in July from June, while the value of loans to housing investors rose by only 0.5% to A$13,587 after falling 0.5% in June. "Lending to owner occupiers rose strongly for the second month in a row. It looks increasingly like the property lending market is starting to become more balanced," says Oliver. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT -- A fall in Australian consumer confidence--down 5.6% in September vs. August according to a Westpac-Melbourne Institute survey--comes as "no surprise" to \uc2 Westpac\uc2 's chief economist \uc2 Bill Evans\uc2 . "The deluge of disturbing news around violent gyrations in both Australian and overseas equity markets; poor economic data from China; a disappointing report on Australia's growth rate and the weakness in the Australian dollar" likely unnerved households, he says. Pessimists have now outnumbered optimists in 17 of the past 19 months. (rachel.pannett@wsj.com; Twitter: @RachelPannett)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT -- The AUD/USD pair maintains its strong upward momentum and is well supported by the ascending 20-period intraday moving average. Also the intraday relative strength index has exceeded the over-bought level of 70 but shows no signs of a bearish divergence. The first upside target at 0.7065 (around the September 3 high) is in sight, and the second one is set at 0.7100 (a base seen on September 1). Only a break below the key support at 0.6950 would turn the intraday outlook bearish and call for further decline toward the first alternative downside target at 0.6900. This content was produced exclusively for Dow Jones by Trading Central ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 ), an investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT -- Gold prices are marginally higher in Asia, but still trading close to a two-week low. The precious metal gained on some bargain-hunting and volatility in stocks. Gold is trading at $1,124.70/oz, up from the opening price of $1,121.74/oz. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT -- The premium that investors have been willing to pay for Woodside's (WPL.AU) capital discipline, as well as the energy company's own growth projects, is being brought into question by its bid for Oil Search (OSH.AU), Citi says. The house drops its call on Woodside to neutral from buy, suggesting the company is now in the unfavorable position of having the market assume the worst about its growth outlook, and questioning why it isn't offering cash unless its shares are actually over-valued. And with Oil Search trading above the level implied by Woodside's bid, and with a counter bid unlikely, Citi also drops its stance on Oil Search to sell from neutral. Woodside shares are down 0.9% at A$29.38, Oil search is down 1.9% at A$7.75. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 9 Sep 2015 01:13 ET  DJ Australian Equities Roundup -- Market Talk -2-\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT -- Following \uc2 Qantas\uc2 's (QAN.AU) strong fiscal 2015 result, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  reiterates its expectation that the airline's strong free cash flow provides a major opportunity for capital management over fiscal 2016 to fiscal 2017. \uc2 Qantas\uc2  gets the ball rolling on results day with the announcement of a A$505 million capital return. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says it expects the Australian national carrier to generate A$2.9 billion of free cash flow over fiscal 2016 and fiscal 2017. This is underpinned by strong earnings growth, well-controlled capex and no cash tax payments, due to accumulated tax losses. The broker expects \uc2 Qantas\uc2  to return A$900 million a year to shareholders in fiscal 2016 and fiscal 2017. Increases target price to A$4.45 from A$4.40. Keeps buy rating. (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT -- \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  is such a big believer in \uc2 BlueScope Steel\uc2 's (BSL.AU) plan to overhaul its business to counter the drop in steel prices and spreads that it has added the Australian company to its conviction buy list. BlueScope says it will either find more than A$200 million in savings by FY17 or close its Australian steel-making \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  thinks the former is most likely. "Despite ongoing deterioration in domestic demand and steel spreads, we believe this delivers a clear path to earnings improvement over the medium term," analyst Owen Birrell says. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expects the steel maker's net profit to rise at a compound annual rate of 28% over three years. The broker lifts its target price by 30% to A$5.85/share. BSL last traded at A$4.51.(david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT -- What's bad news for \uc2 Glencore\uc2  (GLEN.LN) (0805.HK) could be good for Australian agricultural commodities handler GrainCorp (GNC.AU), says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . The broker notes \uc2 Glencore\uc2 's announcement on Sept. 7 that it would seek to raise money from the minority participation of third-party strategic investors in some agriculture assets, including infrastructure. Analyst Grant Saligari thinks \uc2 Glencore\uc2 's move will likely re-emphasize the value of strategic agricultural assets. "The development is also likely to reduce the impetus for alternative capacity in the Australian market," Saligari says. "Potentially, GrainCorp could expand its export capability with a minority interest in some \uc2 Glencore\uc2  assets." \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  lifts GrainCorp to outperform from neutral, while lowering its price target by 7.6% to A$9.70/share. GNC last traded at A$8.15. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT -- Woodside (WPL.AU) may have opened the door for other suitors to make a play for Oil Search (OSH.AU). \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  reckons the most obvious and logical potential partner for Oil Search has to be \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2 , given it's the operator of the PNG LNG project, where Oil Search is a partner. Also, \uc2 Exxon\uc2 's U.S. dollar funding puts it at a competitive advantage relative to Woodside, which is largely reliant on Aussie dollar-denominated shares, the brokerage says. Other suitors might include Total, which operates the Papua LNG project with Oil Search, as well as liquefied natural gas companies such as Petronas and Inpex, Deutsche says. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT -- Investors have mostly squeezed all the value out of \uc2 Regis Resources\uc2  (RRL.AU) since the Australian gold producer unveiled an updated mine plan for 2016 in July, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , which drops the stock to neutral from buy. The mine plan was a positive catalyst for Regis's shares because it removed uncertainty around future output. Regis expects to produce between 275,000 ounces and 305,000 ounces of gold this year. "We believe that delivery on the plan will be achievable for Regis," \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. "However, considering the re-rating in the stock coupled with our view of gold prices falling to US$1,050/oz (from US$1,118/oz currently), we see limited upside in \uc2 Regis Resources\uc2  both in an absolute sense as well as sector-relative." RRL last traded at A$1.51, almost in line with \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 's A$1.50/share price target. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT -- \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  lifts \uc2 Oz Minerals\uc2  (OZL.AU) to neutral from sell as it now believes the mining company's share price more accurately reflects the weakness in the copper market. \uc2 Oz Minerals\uc2  shares have fallen 25% since late May, as copper prices tumbled on concerns around China's weak demand growth. "With copper prices now only around 11% above our floor price of US$2.04/lb (US$4,500/ton), we believe most of the downside risk is already priced in," the broker says. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  also lifts its price target for \uc2 Oz Minerals\uc2  by 8.6% to A$3.80/share after lowering the capital expenditure forecasts in its estimate of the company's net present value. OZL last traded at A$3.73. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT -- \uc2 Westpac Banking\uc2  (WBC.AU) shares are looking better value following the recent pullback, Morgans says. It shifts to an add recommendation from hold, and lifts its target to A$36.75/share from A$35.88. The shares are trading at about 11.9 times forecast earnings, with a dividend yield of 6.3%, the brokerage notes. Further, Morgans says that if \uc2 Westpac\uc2  is successful in its customer-focused strategy, outlined earlier in the week, then that could create significant value through improved cost-efficiencies and by building a platform for sustainable growth. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  currently is up 1.9% at A$31.11. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT -- \uc2 Qantas\uc2  (QAN.AU) says it will add more flights between Australia and Hong Kong and Manila. The airline has been carefully adding capacity on some international routes in a targeted response to demand after its long-haul division returned to profit last fiscal year with the help of a major cost-cutting drive including shutting down several loss-making routes. From October, \uc2 Qantas\uc2  will operate an additional four Sydney-Hong Kong services each week. Services from Sydney to Manila will increase from four to five per week between early December and late March to cater for the peak holiday season. \uc2 Qantas\uc2  says the new services have been made possible by its focus on more efficient use of aircraft. They follow an additional 140 international services recently announced to operate flights to Singapore, Jakarta and New Zealand over the upcoming summer holiday season. (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; Twitter: @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT -- Oil Search (OSH.AU) seems to have little to gain from a Woodside (WPL.AU) merger outside of an attractive bid price, Macquarie says. The Papua New Guinea-focused energy company's ventures mean it's working with \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  (XOM) and Total (TOT), both credible liquefied natural gas developers, operators and marketers in their own right. The brokerage thinks the scale and potential risks of successfully completing an offer for Oil Search speaks volumes about the headwinds facing Woodside's existing portfolio of energy assets. Macquarie maintains an underperform call on Woodside and outperform on Oil Search. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 0028 GMT -- Australian biotechnology firm Starpharma's (SPL.AU) multimillion-dollar commercial licensing agreement with European pharmaceutical major \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  (AZN.LN) is a vote of confidence in Starpharma's DEP drug-delivery platform and a source of near-term cash flow, says Bell Potter. "The commercial terms of the licensing agreement are very attractive and highlight the underlying value of SPL's DEP platform," the broker says. "We believe AZN as a partner is a good strategic fit for SPL given its focus on oncology and its specific experience and capabilities in antibody-drug conjugates." It lifts Starpharma's valuation to A$1.15 a share from A$1.06. (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; Twitter: @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 0027 GMT -- Most USD/Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs are under pressure from the opening bell, with the Malaysia ringgit and South Korea won clawing back from multi-year lows notched Tuesday. Volatility persists in global financial markets with Wall Street rallying 2.4% overnight to recover losses sustained pre-Labor Day, weighing on safe haven assets such as gold and the U.S. dollar, while lifting the risk-sensitive Australia dollar. The return of risk-appetite in the U.S. may have been triggered by a late rally on the Shanghai Composite index Tuesday, which saw the index close 2.9% higher despite trading most of the day in the red. Yesterday's surge in Chinese stocks had characteristics of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly in light of the dismal \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  August exports data published in the morning. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0025 GMT -- \uc2 Karoon Gas\uc2  (KAR.AU) has more cash on its balance sheet than its shares are worth, and that's what is keeping \uc2 UBS\uc2  from changing its buy call on the stock despite adopting a more bearish stance toward future oil prices. \uc2 Karoon Gas\uc2  last traded at A$1.77, but \uc2 UBS\uc2  thinks its cash hoard alone equates to a price of A$2.24/share. The problem is that Karoon is a bet on success with the drill bit, and investors remain unwilling to pay much--if anything--for exploration risk, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Karoon is currently exploring for oil and gas offshore Brazil, and planning to appraise its recent Echidna discovery there. "We expect that a recovery in oil prices and/or further progress in appraising Echidna will be needed to entice investors back to \uc2 Karoon Gas\uc2 ," says analyst Cameron Hardie. \uc2 UBS\uc2  has a A$3.10/share price target on the stock. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 9 Sep 2015 01:13 ET  DJ Australian Equities Roundup -- Market Talk -3-\par \par \uc2 2359 GMT -- With \uc2 Santos Ltd\uc2 .'s (STO.AU) share price now at 15-year lows, will the oil and gas company reconsider its policy of defending its investment grade credit rating? "Such an outcome would avoid value reduction via selling assets in a low oil price environment and avoid a large, dilutive equity raise, but comes at a likely cost of higher priced future debt," \uc2 UBS\uc2  analyst Cameron Hardie says. Santos has no debt covenants in place and with its low share price making a large equity raise increasing unpalatable, \uc2 UBS\uc2  believes such an option may be given due consideration. "Looking through the near term uncertainty, we do see value here, but with oil price volatility still high, investors should brace for a bumpy ride ahead," Hardie says. The broker retains a buy call, but lowers its price target by 13% to 6.10 Australian dollars/share ($4.28/share). STO last traded at A$4.41. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1947 EDT -- Technical analysis points to a near-term recovery for Australia's \uc2 ASX\uc2  200, but the price action is likely to be contained below the 5300 level, Morgans' Violeta Todorova says. She says the index had been trading in correction mode since April, but recently crossed 4900, where there's initial support. She says that so long as 4800 holds, despite recent "damage" to the chart, a primary upward trend is remains intact. A break below that level would have bearish implications over the longer term, and she estimates the next potential downside target would be 4550. The \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 was last at 5115.2. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 23:19 GMT -- The global rally in equities markets looks set to continue Down Under, with Aussie stocks building on Tuesday's strong gains after Wall Street advanced strongly in overnight trading. The Dow industrials gained 2.4%, the biggest one-day rise in almost two weeks as concerns about a slowdown in China eased following a rebound in shares there. IG forecasts the \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 will open 0.5% higher at about 5142, and chief market strategist Chris Weston says a nice move higher by the banks would be welcomed by bulls. He adds \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  (BHP.AU) look likely to open up about 1.2%, underpinned by strong moves in oil, copper and iron-ore futures. Investors also will have their eyes on Oil Search (OSH.AU) after Tuesday's rally following a takeover approach from Woodside (WPL.AU). The \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 climbed 1.7% Monday to 5115.2. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 23:13 GMT -- The good news just keeps on coming for \uc2 Sandfire Resources\uc2  (SFR.AU) and \uc2 Talisman Mining\uc2  (TLM.AU) at their Monty copper prospect in Western Australia. Sandfire and Talisman had initially discovered ore containing 19% copper at Monty, but that wasn't repeated until the latest round of drilling. Macquarie is particularly excited by the location of the latest hit: around 150 meters away from the initial find. "The latest assay results have materially increased the potential for a much larger high grade zone than was originally expected," says Macquarie. The broker, which has an outperform call on Sandfire, expects the release of a maiden resource for Monty to be the most material catalyst for the stock over the next six months. SFR last traded at A$6.00, well below Macquarie's A$8.00/share price target. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2303 GMT -- The falling Australian dollar has been a considerable boon to \uc2 Freelancer Ltd\uc2 . (FLN.AU), and Canaccord Genuity thinks its about to have an even bigger impact on growth. That's because Freelancer makes 90% of its revenue outside Australia, while around 70% of its costs are in Australian dollars. "We expect the benefit of this virtuous circle is likely to be underestimated by investors over the medium term," analyst Owen Humphries says. "A 5% depreciation in the Australian dollar results in an additional A$3 million ($2.1 million) in additional capital to reinvest in building/marketing its platform." Investors should note that Australian dollar futures curve points to further weakening against the greenback in the medium term. The broker rates Freelancer at buy, with a A$1.75/share ($1.23/share) price target. FLN last traded at A$1.21. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 22:54 GMT -- Oil Search (OSH.AU) ticks a numbers of boxes for \uc2 Woodside Petroleum\uc2  (WPL.AU), but the premium represented in Woodside's offer is too low, \uc2 UBS\uc2  argues. Oil Search's stake in the PNG LNG natural gas project is top tier, producing and performing well, the brokerage says. Oil Search also has a material interest in the proposed next stage for the project, PNG LNG T3, as well as the Papua LNG projects. At the offer price of one share for every 4 Oil Search share, the deal makes sense for Woodside, with an implied oil price of US$68.30/barrel, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. But it also implies A$7.65/share for Oil Search shareholders, which is an almost 14% premium to Monday's closing level and unlikely to be accepted, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. The brokerage adds the bid also raises an interesting question: does the approach on Oil Search indicate that Woodside's Browse LNG project is less likely to proceed? (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 2251 GMT -- Australian travel agency \uc2 Flight Centre\uc2  (FLT.AU) appears to be running hard to stand still, says Macquarie. The broker expects U.K. based Topdeck Tours and favorable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings to provide the biggest boost to profits in the current fiscal year, masking sluggish growth elsewhere in the business. \uc2 Flight Centre\uc2  expects underlying profits of between A$380 million and A$395 million in FY16, which at the mid-point represents 5.8% growth. Macquarie currently tips \uc2 Flight Centre\uc2  to make a A$382.6 million underlying profit. "However of particular note is that A$9 million of the growth in FY16 is from Topdeck and A$14 million from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which implies flat underlying growth to hit the midpoint of guidance," the broker says. "With store growth still expected to be 5% in FY16, \uc2 Flight Centre\uc2  appears to be running hard to stand still." Macquarie rates \uc2 Flight Centre\uc2  at underperform, with a A$35.46/share price target. FLT last traded at A$36.60. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2232 GMT -- -- How significant is the rollout of Yowie's (YOW.AU) chocolate in 4,300 stores owned by \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  in the U.S. for its revenue? Canaccord Genuity thinks \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  could initially order 604,800 units, which is equal to around US$997,000 of revenue. That estimate is based on an average stocking level per store of 216 units, comprising one box of 12 units per checkout, at 12 checkouts on average, plus six boxes of back stock. "In a scenario where on average \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  sells 4 boxes per week (48 units per store, per week), the full rollout to all 4,300 stores could result in monthly volumes of 891,000 units, driving monthly revenues of US$1.5 million," analyst Chris Northwood says. Canaccord rates Yowie at buy, with a A$1.80/share price target. YOW last traded at A$1.16. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2226 GMT -- Canaccord Genuity drops \uc2 Beadell Resources Ltd\uc2 . (BDR.AU) to hold from buy and cuts its price target by 57% to 13 Australian cents/share (9 cents/share), after taking a more bearish view of production from its Tucano gold \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil. The broker now thinks 160,000 troy ounces is a more likely sustainable production at Tucano, after Beadell recently confirmed that gold production from the high-grade Duckhead deposit will fall into the first quarter of 2016. Canaccord expects Beadell's 2015 gold production will total 124,000 ounces, down from its earlier forecast of 140,000 ounces. Also, analyst Tim McCormack notes that Beadell's cash fell sharply to A$21 million ($14.7 million) by the end of June. "While our model suggests the company can maintain comfortable cash levels while servicing its debt, there is little scope for additional capital spend or production misses going forward," he says. BDR last traded at A$0.11. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2015 GMT - After sharp AUD underperformance recently, the Aussie should start to find demand in the 1.64-1.67 area against the euro, says Sean Callow, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Sydney. "EUR has gained during periods of equity volatility, presumably via a squeeze on funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is not likely to prove a sustained source of EUR demand, with positioning now more balanced," Callow says. He notes ECB QE will also pick up near-term after being scaled down in August, while talk of ECB boosting QE should grow. EUR/AUD recently at 1.5961. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 09, 2015 01:13 ET (05:13 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150909eb99000a7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
