\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Up 3.5%; Rebound May Not Sustain -Strategist -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2015\par 02:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0032 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese stocks are up following a big rebound in overnight markets in an apparent respite of recent risk-averse trading caused by concerns about China's further slowdown and \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's potential rate increases. \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  rose 2.5% on Tuesday. Hideyuki Ishiguro, senior strategist at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 , says he doesn't think the latest rebound will sustain for long. Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's letter message to a \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 's Japan investor conference may be taken as positive catalyst, but Ishiguro says the market attention will soon shift away from that. "The market focus isn't there (on Japan). It's on China and the U.S.," he says. "The Federal Open Market Committee meeting isn't over yet." Abe said in the letter that deflationary mind-set was wiped out and the outstanding Pan-Pacific trade pact issues would be solved with one more ministerial-level meeting. Nikkei is up 3.5% at 18036.64.\par \par \uc2 (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0027 GMT [Dow Jones] Most USD/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs are under pressure from the opening bell, with the Malaysia ringgit and South Korea won clawing back from multi-year lows notched Tuesday. Volatility persists in global financial markets with Wall Street rallying 2.4% overnight to recover losses sustained pre-Labor Day, weighing on safe haven assets such as gold and the U.S. dollar, while lifting the risk-sensitive Australia dollar. The return of risk-appetite in the U.S. may have been triggered by a late rally on the Shanghai Composite index Tuesday, which saw the index close 2.9% higher despite trading most of the day in the red. Yesterday's surge in Chinese stocks had characteristics of government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , particularly in light of the dismal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  August exports data published in the morning. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2232 GMT [Dow Jones] -- How significant is the rollout of Yowie's (YOW.AU) chocolate in 4,300 stores owned by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wal-Mart\f28 \uc2 \f2  in the U.S. for its revenue? Canaccord Genuity thinks \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wal-Mart\f28 \uc2 \f2  could initially order 604,800 units, which is equal to around US$997,000 of revenue. That estimate is based on an average stocking level per store of 216 units, comprising one box of 12 units per checkout, at 12 checkouts on average, plus six boxes of back stock. "In a scenario where on average \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wal-Mart\f28 \uc2 \f2  sells 4 boxes per week (48 units per store, per week), the full rollout to all 4,300 stores could result in monthly volumes of 891,000 units, driving monthly revenues of US$1.5 million," analyst Chris Northwood says. Canaccord rates Yowie at buy, with a A$1.80/share price target. YOW last traded at A$1.16. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17:03 EDT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 BB&T Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  Markets initiates coverage of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hormel Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HRL) with a "hold" rating, saying a historically high valuation is keeping it on the sidelines despite the food maker's "enviable record" of profit and revenue growth over past decade. Strong brands like Spam, Hormel and Skippy have given Hormel pricing power, helping the company steer through volatile protein markets, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BB&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. But sluggish consumer demand for big packaged-food brands weighs on volume growth for Hormel, and "consequently, we believe Hormel will likely become more reliant upon acquisitions to drive growth, which increases risk," \f28 \uc2 \f2 BB&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. HRL shares have nearly tripled in past five years, and up 2.3% today to $62.09. (david.kesmodel@wsj.com; @davidkesmodel)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:58 EDT - Oil prices will be higher in 2016 than they are today, predicts \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bill Thomas\f28 \uc2 \f2 , CEO of EOG Resources. "The market is rebalancing as we speak," he tells a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  energy conference today. Many of the company's shale oil plays have a 100% rate of return when oil trades at $65 a barrel, but even if crude prices rebounded to that level the company has no interest in outspending cash flow to grow production in the lower price environment. EOG's focus remains on getting costs down even more and investor returns up, Thomas says. "That's the fundamental thing. Don't outspend. Don't increase the debt of the company, but stay disciplined." (lynn.cook@wsj.com; @LynnJCook)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:54 EDT - The latest outlook on 4Q hiring trends from Manpower Group indicate US and Canadian economies are headed in opposite directions. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BMO Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the spread between hiring plans in the two countries -- a strong US versus tepid Canada -- is now the widest in the US's favor in nearly two decades. Seasonally adjusted, a net 18% of US firms plan to add to payrolls compared to a net 6% in Canada. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Manpower\f28 \uc2 \f2  says Canadian hiring intentions declined three percentage points from 3Q to 4Q, and are now at weakest levels since 4Q of 2009. Canada's economy is struggling as firms deal with the fallout linked to the commodity-price rout, although signs of a rebound are slowly emerging. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:45 EDT - Copper's 5.3% surge may not last, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 BMO\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Jessica Fung. While \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 400,000 ton production cut is likely to reduce most analysts' expectations for next year's copper supply, other shifts in the market suggest that demand for copper is also running short of expectations, she says. Chinese data showing lower fixed asset investment, lower construction starts and weak auto sales points to flat-to-lower copper demand from the world's top metals buyer. "The demand numbers also need to come down and the question is how does the market balance out after that," she says, adding that the ultimately impact on price is unclear. (tatyana.shumsky@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:30 EDT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 DoubleLine Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Jeffrey Gundlach maintains his forecast that the Fed will not raise interest rates in 2015 on a conference call with investors today. A rate hike would be a "real problem," Gundlach says because of economic weakness in the US, pointing out a decline in nominal GDP this year. (matthieu.wirz@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:29 EDT - While it continues to hamper the fight against inflation, the sharp depreciation of the Brazilian real versus US dollar is providing some reasons to celebrate. In the first week of September, Brazil posted a trade surplus of $554M, bringing the trade surplus to $7.85B for the year, country's trade ministry said. The figure is nearing expectations for a trade surplus of $8.9B for this full year, according to economists surveyed by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in its weekly survey. Real was trading at 3.81 per dollar, compared with 2.655 per dollar at the end of 2014. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16:26 EDT - Macquarie is the latest bank to cut its oil-price forecasts through 2017. "We continue to expect the crude oil markets to remain dominated by oversupply, but expect the oversupply to last longer," the bank says. "Even when the physical markets begin rebalancing in late 2016, the massive overhang of crude oil in storage will act to depress prices." Macquarie sees WTI at $53/barrel in 2016, down from its earlier forecast of $62, and Brent at $58, down from $68. (nicole.friedman@wsj.com; @NicoleFriedman)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2015 GMT - After sharp AUD underperformance recently, the Aussie should start to find demand in the 1.64-1.67 area against the euro, says Sean Callow, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Sydney. "EUR has gained during periods of equity volatility, presumably via a squeeze on funding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . This is not likely to prove a sustained source of EUR demand, with positioning now more balanced," Callow says. He notes ECB QE will also pick up near-term after being scaled down in August, while talk of ECB boosting QE should grow. EUR/AUD recently at 1.5961. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2011 GMT - A broad improvement in market sentiment overnight and rebound in commodities is helping shore up NZD/USD, says BNZ senior markets strategist Kymberly Martin. BNZ's "global risk appetite index" has inched its way up to 28% from the lows of 16% in late August, she says. The pair is at 0.6350. Martin says near-term resistance is seen just above 0.6400, while support is likely to be felt on any pullback toward 0.6250. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 08, 2015 20:32 ET (00:32 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150909eb9900065\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
