\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 3-China imports sink in August, shares rally but volumes dive\b0\par\par\uc2 875 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2015\par 14:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds \uc2 PBOC\uc2  comments in paragraphs 9-10, 23)\par \par \uc2 * China August imports fell 13.8 pct y/y\par \par \uc2 * Exports slipped 5.5 pct y/y\par \par \uc2 * CSI300 index and Shanghai close up nearly 3 percent\par \par \uc2 * Volumes in Shanghai lowest since February\par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney and Samuel Shen\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI Sept 8 (Reuters) - China's imports tumbled in August, raising concerns about the health of the world's second-largest economy and its contribution to global growth.\par \par \uc2 The data will add to the pressure on Beijing policymakers trying to ensure China's economy avoids a hard landing, though authorities will take some comfort that their efforts to steady the country's stock markets were rewarded with a late rally on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Imports dived 13.8 percent from a year earlier, far more than analysts had forecast, and a tenth consecutive monthly drop, reflecting both lower global commodity prices and sluggish demand.\par \par \uc2 A surprise devaluation in the yuan early last month combined with slowing consumer demand will dent the prospects of imports picking up significantly anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Much of China's imports are commodities and other raw materials going into factories that turn them into goods for sale overseas, so the fall could be an ominous sign for exports in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Exports fell less than forecast, sliding 5.5 percent, but analysts were still doubtful that China can now achieve its year-end trade growth target of 6 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The yuan devaluation will have limited impact on exports, which are falling because demand is weak, not because the price is not good," said \uc2 Li Jian\uc2 , head of foreign trade research at the Chinese Academy of International Trade and Economic Cooperation, the Commerce Ministry's think-tank.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves posted their biggest ever monthly fall in August, reflecting Beijing's efforts to stabilise the yuan following its devaluation.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC)\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was one of the reasons for the drop in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, adding that any future fluctuations in reserves would be "normal".\par \par \uc2 The bank said in a statement late on Tuesday that China's economy could maintain medium- to high-speed growth in the long term, and the current account would remain in surplus, also over the long term.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS VOLUMES TUMBLE\par \par \uc2 Chinese policymakers have been trying to reassure financial markets that their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is stable and that the recent stock market turbulence is easing.\par \par \uc2 Stocks have fallen around 40 percent since mid-June, with the Shanghai Composite Index hovering around the 3,000 point level, having been above 5,000 less than three months ago.\par \par \uc2 Shares initially declined on Tuesday but rallied later in the day to finish almost 3 percent higher - though trading volumes in both stocks and futures were down sharply.\par \par \uc2 The CSI300 index of the biggest stocks listed in Shanghai and Shenzhen closed up 2.57 percent, while Shanghai was up 2.93 percent.\par \par \uc2 Volume in the Shanghai market was the lowest since February, a month when trading is usually thin due to the Chinese New Year Festival.\par \par \uc2 The stock futures market was hit by an abrupt reversal in policy that caused trading volumes to collapse. Last Wednesday, China raised the margin requirements for futures not being used for hedging purposes to 40 percent of the contract's value from 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 The futures contract for the CSI300 index maturing in September has seen volumes dive, logging 28,957 transactions on Tuesday, down almost 100 percent from a week ago. On Aug. 25, when markets were in a major sell-off, the contract saw 2.43 million transactions.\par \par \uc2 Chinese authorities have rolled out a series of measures to bring a sense of calm back to their stock markets and reduce short-term speculation.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the finance ministry dangled incentives to encourage longer-term investments, saying it would remove personal income tax on dividends from shares held for more than a year, and halve it on those held for between a month and a year.\par \par \uc2 That announcement came hours after regulators and exchanges proposed introducing a 'circuit breaker' on the CSI300 index to help steady the market.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said these measures are unlikely to encourage many investors to come back into the market for stocks in either mainland China, known as A-shares, or Hong Kong, given the longer-term economic concerns hanging over the market.\par \par \uc2 "While investors see value in Hong Kong-listed Chinese equities, they're concerned about an inevitable \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  rate hike and Beijing's ability to manage A-shares, growth, capital flight and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," analysts at Nomura wrote in a client note.\par \par \uc2 In its statement, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  also said new regulations imposed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards market this month were not a form of capital control. The measures, introduced after investors speculated in the market, would help stabilise China's financial system at a time when increased yuan volatility is likely to cause more companies to incur \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses, it said. (Additional reporting by Winni Zhou and Henning Gloystein in Singapore; Writing by Rachel Armstrong; Editing by Ian Geoghegan) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-8T13:47:02.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150908eb98005e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
