\par\par \b \uc2 Morning MoneyBeat Asia: China Data's Still Keeping Tensions High\b0\par\par\uc2 By Phillipa Leighton-Jones   \par \uc2 927 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2015\par 00:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Market Snap: At the New York close on Friday, for the week: \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  down 3.40% to 1921.22. DJIA down 3.25% to 16102.38. \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Comp down 2.99% to 4683.92. \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yields down; 10-year at 2.128%. In Europe on Monday, Nymex crude oil down 3.89% at $44.26. Gold down 0.29% at $1118.3/ounce. FTSE 100 closed up 0.52%, DAX up 0.70% and Stoxx Europe 600 up 0.48%.\par \par \uc2 To receive this morning newsletter via email, click here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/MoneyBeatAsiaSignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/MoneyBeatAsiaSignup}}}\par \par \uc2 How We Got Here: As Americans took in the last of the summer sunshine over a long Labor Day weekend that followed a mixed jobs report, Asia stole investor attention Monday as lingering fears over the state of China's economy dominated the mood.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves declined sharply in August, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday, a sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to limit money leaving the country and stem the yuan's fall after moving to devalue it against the dollar on Aug. 11.\par \par \uc2 China also revised its growth rate for last year to 7.3% from 7.4%. The country's growth target of about 7% this year is the slowest pace in a quarter century.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, China \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  said in remarks to the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  largest economies that the "correction in the stock market is almost done" and China's financial markets are expected to become "more stable" after the Chinese yuan steadies.\par \par \uc2 Amid the data, though, the Stoxx Europe 600 clawed back some of last week's losses to end the session 0.48% higher. Most Asian markets fell through Monday's session and the Shanghai Composite ended 2.5% lower amid jitters over the Chinese data.\par \par \uc2 Coming Up: With nerves still rattled over China, the prospect of a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  rate increase next week has markets braced for more volatility. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  meets Sept. 16 and 17 to decide its next move, and while economists have said a rate hike in September is basically a coin flip', strategists weighed in Monday, saying that the timing of the first increase was less important than the pace of subsequent moves.\par \par \uc2 From The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Glencore\uc2  Scraps Dividend, Raises Cash to Cut Debt: \uc2 Glencore\uc2  has scrapped its final dividend and plans to raise up to $2.5 billion through a stock sale among other measures valued at a combined $7.7 billion to reduce net debt.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  War Chest Takes Hit: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by a record $93.9 billion in August, a result of aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Cuts 2014 Economic Growth Estimate to 7.3% From 7.4%: China revised its 2014 growth rate to 7.3% from 7.4% due to a weaker-than-reported contribution from the service sector, casting doubt on an economic bright spot amid concerns about the health of the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Heard on the Street: China Bleeds \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserves: A $100 billion here, a $100 billion there, and pretty soon you're talking about real money.\par \par \uc2 Stocks Try to Regain Footing: Global stocks struggled to regain their footing amid lingering fears over the health of the Chinese economy.\par \par \uc2 Uber, Chinese Rival Score in Fundraising Drives: The U.S. ride-hailing app raised $1.2 billion and \uc2 Didi Kuaidi\uc2  was close to bringing in a combined $3 billion in the latest round to fuel a battle for China's ride-hailing market.\par \par \uc2 From MoneyBeat\par \par \uc2 An Investor's Field Guide to Bottom Fishing : While the sharp swings in the stock market in the past couple of weeks have scared many investors, others have been buying. But you can hit snags when you bottom-fish, so the key is to act on a plan rather than out of instinct.\par \par \uc2 On U.S. Rates Rise, Pace Matters More Than Timing -- Strategists :Friday's jobs report offered few fresh clues on the timing of a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's rate increases, but strategists said Monday that pace still matters more than timing.\par \par \uc2 These Hedge Funds Bucked Their Own Trend in August Selloff: Computer driven hedge funds have had a tough time over the past few months, but when the chips were down for global markets in late August, they came into their own.\par \par \uc2 Glencore\uc2 's Big Move -- The Numbers : Commodities giant \uc2 Glencore PLC\uc2  said Monday it would sell stock, scrap its dividend, suspend some mining \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sell assets to reduce its net debt amid global market turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Glencore\uc2  Has Hedge Funds in Mind in Equity Sale: Why is \uc2 Glencore\uc2  raising $2.5 billion in equity? One reason: it should allow the miner and commodities trader to avoid offering shares to its existing investors, usually required by pre-emption rights under U.K. rules.\par \par \uc2 Why the Euro Could Make Your Apple Upgrade Cost More: \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . is preparing to announce the launch of its new iPhones next week, but thanks to a stronger U.S. dollar, the company could raise prices in Europe by up to 15%, according to European investment \uc2 bank Societe G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gender Equality an 'Economic No-Brainer,' Says \uc2 IMF\uc2  Chief: International Monetary Fund Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  is pushing policymakers to get women into the world's labor force. \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 07, 2015 18:34 ET (22:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150907eb97001q4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
