\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Markets Closed For Holiday\b0\par\par\uc2 2,639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2015\par 22:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MARKETS AT A GLANCE \par (Data as of approximately 4 p.m. ET Monday) \par  \par                     LAST        CHANGE  % CHG \par DJIA                16102.38    -272.38 -1.66% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2               4683.92     -49.58  -1.05% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P 500\f28 \uc2 \f2              1921.22     -29.91  -1.53% \par Japan: Nikkei 225   17860.47    68.31   0.38% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2            20583.52    -257.09 -1.23% \par Shanghai Composite  3080.42     -79.75  -2.52% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  BSE Sensex      24893.81    -308.09 -1.22% \par Australia: S&P/ASX  5030.40     -10.20  -0.20% \par UK: FTSE            6074.52     31.60   0.52% \par  \par                     PRICE CHG   YIELD \par U.S. 2 Year         -1/32       0.713 \par U.S. 5 Year         2/32        1.470 \par U.S. 10 Year        9/32        2.129 \par Australia 10 Year   -9/32       2.669 \par China 10 Year       -5/32       3.380 \par India 10 Year       0/32        7.965 \par Japan 10 Year       -1/32       0.372 \par German 10 Year      -1/32       0.672 \par  \par                         LAST(MID)   CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)   0.6925      0.0013 \par Yen (USD/JPY)           119.31      0.30 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW) 1202.53     1.20 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)  6.3688      -0.0013 \par Euro (EUR/USD)          1.1166      0.0014 \par WSJ Dollar Index        88.99       0.03 \par  \par                 LAST    CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil       44.25   -1.80   -3.91% \par Brent Crude     49.42   -1.91   -3.72% \par Gold            1118.4  -3.0    -0.27% \par  \par SNAPSHOT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. financial markets were closed Monday because of a holiday. Global stocks, meanwhile, struggled to regain their footing amid lingering fears over the health of the Chinese economy. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPENING CALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's exports likely fell 5.2% in August from a year ago after a decline of 8.3% in July, according to a median forecast of 12 economists polled earlier by The Wall Street Journal. China's imports likely dropped 7.9% year-over-year last month, after falling 8.1% in July, the poll showed. The trade surplus in August likely stood at $48.6 billion, expanding from $43.03 billion in the previous month, according to the survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Sluggish external and domestic demand continued to weigh on foreign trade," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard Chartered\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists say in a note, adding that a modest yuan depreciation is unlikely to tangibly boost China's exports in the near term. The General Administration of Customs is set to release the trade data Tuesday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global stocks struggled to regain their footing Monday amid lingering fears over the health of the Chinese economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Stoxx Europe 600 clawed back some of the losses suffered last week to end the session 0.5% higher, but most Asian markets fell and the Shanghai Composite Index ended 2.5% lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock markets were closed Monday for Labor Day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With U.S. markets closed today, Asia continues to be the focus for investor attention and broadly speaking we continue to see a lot of nervousness in financial markets," said Mark Dowding, senior portfolio manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BlueBay Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which oversees $62 billion in assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves declined sharply in August, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Monday, a sign that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily to limit money leaving the country and stem the yuan's fall after moving to devalue it against the dollar on Aug. 11.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China also revised its growth rate for last year to 7.3% from 7.4%. The country's growth target of about 7% this year is the slowest pace in a quarter century.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It seems unlikely that the uncertainty on China, be it the real economy or policy, will fade anytime soon," Michala Marcussen, an economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote in a note to clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Over the weekend, China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zhou Xiaochuan\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in remarks to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of 20\f28 \uc2 \f2  largest economies that the "correction in the stock market is almost done" and China's financial markets are expected to become "more stable" after the Chinese yuan steadies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What concerns investors is another large yuan devaluation, which would be very detrimental for markets," said Keith Wade, chief economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Schroders\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which oversees around GBP310 billion ($472 billion) in assets. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor's comments soothed those concerns, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Randy Frederick, a managing director at brokerage \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charles Schwab Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., said that Chinese authorities have taken a number of steps to calm markets already and that investors are now "increasingly questioning [their] ability to control the market."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "China is going through a huge transition after years of very strong growth and that is making investors very nervous," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts are also bracing for more choppy trading ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Sept. 16-17 meeting, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will consider the first rise in short-term interest rates in nearly 10 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even if it does not come in 10 days' time, the pressure is building for a move. You are still going to have volatility in coming weeks. Everyone is looking at economic data very closely," said Nick Nelson, equity strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks and commodities suffered more sharp swings last week as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left the door open to more stimulus and a U.S. jobs report didn't give investors much clarity about the timing of a rate rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Depending on what the Fed does in September, \f28 \uc2 \f2 the ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  could announce an extension of its bond-buying program beyond September 2016 at the next monetary-policy meeting in October, strategists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Later this week, investors will also be focusing their attention on rate decisions from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  as well as the latest reading of second-quarter growth data out of the eurozone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There seems little prospect of any meaningful calming of market nerves in the week ahead, even in the absence of the U.S. today due to the Labor Day holiday," said Marc Ostwald, analyst at ADM Investor Services in London, in a note. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 China's yuan closed lower against the U.S. dollar Monday, despite Beijing guiding its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stronger via a reference exchange rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was about 0.2% higher from Wednesday's close of CNY6.3559. Markets in China were closed for a public holiday on Thursday and Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the dollar-yuan central parity rate, a reference exchange rate, at 6.3584, compared with 6.3619 on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro, meanwhile, was 0.2% higher against the dollar at around $1.1167. Traders said that the U.S. closure Monday for the Labor Day holiday contributed to lower trading volumes in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , seen as vulnerable to a U.S. rate rise because an increase in rates is expected to strengthen the dollar, weakened Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Turkish lira weakened to a record of 3.0475 lira against the dollar, partly because of violence and instability in the country. The Malaysian ringgit and Indonesian rupiah fell to fresh 17-year lows against the dollar. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. financial markets were closed Monday for Labor Day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. companies, meanwhile, have flooded eurozone bond markets with a record amount of new debt deals this year, a trend that bankers and investors say is likely to continue at a fast pace for the rest of 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the long list of U.S. companies that have crossed the Atlantic, Wells Fargo & Co. and American Honda Finance Co. issued bonds in euros last week, together raising more than EUR2 billion ($2.2 billion), continuing a long string of U.S. financial firms eager to diversify their funding bases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But nonfinancial corporations have also got in on the act this year because of the low borrowing rates in the euro area. In total, these U.S. companies have raised nearly EUR54 billion in eurozone bond markets this year as of Monday, according to data provider Dealogic. That already is a new yearly record and 40% more than they managed over the whole of 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We've seen huge amounts of bond issuance in euros this year from all international corporates, but especially U.S. companies. That is set to continue," said Frazer Ross, a managing director at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank AG\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. companies have accounted for more than 22% of all euro-denominated corporate bond issuance in 2015, more than French and Italian companies combined, and outpacing the 12% of the market the U.S. accounted for last year, according to Dealogic. They also make the U.S. the largest issuer in eurozone corporate bond markets by country. Last year, the U.S. came in at a distant third, with French companies issuing almost double the amount of bonds as U.S. companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many U.S. firms have been lured by cheap financing rates that have resulted from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's easy-money policies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The prospect of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  raising interest rates has also prompted a debt deluge from U.S. companies eager to lock in cheap dollar funding ahead of time. Dollar debt issuance is on track for a record year with $575 billion so far, according to Dealogic. This flood of debt has increased the cost of raising new money in dollars, prompting U.S. firms to look elsewhere.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It still makes a lot of sense for U.S. companies to come to Europe," said Russell Schofield-Bezer, head of debt capital markets in Europe for \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "With the prospect of Fed rate increases in the U.S. and QE just beginning in Europe, the historically low rates offered look very appealing, particularly for those companies with European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMMODITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices fell slightly Monday as traders start to position ahead of economic growth data from the eurozone due out this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said volumes were subdued, with the U.S. market closed for the Labor Day holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European nations are expected to report their second quarter growth numbers later this week, and disappointing data could depress the oil market, traders said. Increasing supply from members of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\f28 \uc2 \f2 , especially from Iran, further dampens any bullish sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7 Sep 2015 16:12 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Markets Closed For -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We cannot see anything in the short term that is going to prop up prices to any great extent," said Phillips Futures' analyst Daniel Ang. "However, higher seasonal demand should kick in soon and have an effect on inventories which in turn should have a positive impact on prices."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also this week, the Paris-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Energy Agency\f28 \uc2 \f2  will release its monthly report, which will contain the full outlook for crude oil in 2016, but traders aren't expecting any major surprises.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices were slightly lower Monday as demand remained subdued following a mixed U.S. labor report and as the U.S. marked a national holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts predicted a quiet day of trading with the U.S. on vacation for the Labor Day weekend, and following two days of national holiday in China at the end of last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't expect prices to move much," said Simona Gambarini, a commodities economist at Capital Economics.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices for the metal fell to two-week lows after the release of the August nonfarm payroll data, which indicated that U.S. hiring slowed against expectations, with 173,000 new jobs that month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper prices may have won some support in moving off their lowest levels in years, with an assist from one of the world's biggest mining companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Metals and commodities trading firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glencore PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Monday it's aiming to cut net debt by about $20 billion. The plan includes suspending \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at two copper mines for 18 months in a bid to lower operating costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Katanga and Mopani mines are located in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Zambia, respectively, and their suspensions will remove about 400,000 tonnes of copper cathode from the market, which are "substantial reductions" by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2  to copper output, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper prices this year have been driven to lowest since 2009. Recent losses for copper came on the heels of China's devaluation of the yuan, on the thinking that such a move may slow the country's imports of the industrial metal. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S HEADLINES \par Merkel Calls for Help in Europe's Migrant Crisis \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Chancellor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Angela Merkel\f28 \uc2 \f2  underlined her call on her European counterparts to do more to help find a solution to a historic wave of migrants seeking asylum. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par In Australia's Refugees, Ambition Thrives \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Humanitarian refugees show entrepreneurial spirit, a new study shows, as Canberra pledges to absorb more displaced Syrians. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  War Chest Takes Hit \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fell by a record $93.9 billion in August, a result of aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Economic Plan Calls for Mergers, Public Listings by 2020 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After months of haggling, China has approved a five-year plan to combine its lumbering state-controlled companies and push them to sell shares to the public. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Uber, Chinese Rival Score in Fundraising Drives \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. ride-hailing app raised $1.2 billion and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Didi Kuaidi\f28 \uc2 \f2  was close to bringing in a combined $3 billion in the latest round to fuel a battle for China's ride-hailing market. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2  Scraps Dividend, Raises Cash to Cut Debt \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2  has scrapped its final dividend and plans to raise up to $2.5 billion through a stock sale among other measures valued at a combined $7.7 billion to reduce net debt. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tesco\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sells South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Tesco\f28 \uc2 \f2  has agreed to sell its South Korea business for $6.1 billion in cash, the latest in a series of pullbacks by the supermarket chain. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amazon to Release $50 Tablet as It Struggles to Sell Pricier Devices\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Reform Plan Calls for Mergers, Public Listings By 2020\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Companies Dominate Eurozone Debt Issuance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Capital Call Just Gives More Reasons for Soul-Searching\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Fosun Bets Big on Private Banks \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par (Times in GMT, followed by country and event) \par 2245  NZ Q2 Economic Survey of Manufacturing \par 2350  JPN Jul Balance of Payments \par 2350  JPN Aug International Transactions in Securities \par 2350  JPN Aug Provisional Trade Statistics for 1st 20 days of Month \par 2350  JPN Aug Bank Lending \par 2350  JPN Q2 2nd Preliminary Quarterly GDP Estimates \par 2350  JPN Q2 Quarterly External Debt & International Investment Position \par 0030  TAI Aug Price Indexes (CPI/WPI) \par 0130  AUS Aug NAB Business Survey \par 0401  US Q4 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Manpower\f28 \uc2 \f2  Quarterly U.S. Employment Outlook Survey \par 0430  JPN Aug Corporate Insolvencies0600 GER Jul Foreign Trade \par 0500  JPN Aug Economy Watchers Survey 0600 GER Jul Manufacturing turnover \par 0600  GER Q2 Labour cost index \par 0645  FRA Jul Foreign trade \par 0900  EU Q2 GDP 2nd estimate \par 1000  US Aug NFIB Index of Small Business Optimism \par 1000  FRA Jul \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  Composite Leading Indicators \par 1400  US Aug Employment Trends Index \par 1400  US Q2 Quarterly Financial Report - Industry \par 1435  UK Chancellor \f28 \uc2 \f2 George Osborne\f28 \uc2 \f2  gives evidence at Lords Economic \par Affairs Committee \par 1900  US Jul Consumer Credit \par 2300  SKA Aug Unemployment Jobless Rate \par 2301  UK Aug Shop Price Index \par 2350  JPN Aug Money Stock, Broadly-defined Liquidity \par 2350  JPN Aug Money Stock, Broadly-defined Liquidity \par N/A   CHN Aug Commodities Trade Data \par N/A   CHN Aug Energy Trade Data \par N/A   CHN Aug Trade \par N/A   US Robert Steven Kaplan becomes Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas President \par N/A   US New IMF Economic Counsellor Maurice Obstfeld begins his work at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par N/A   FRA \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  Belgium DAC Peer Review launch \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 07, 2015 16:12 ET (20:12 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150907eb97001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NZ Morning Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 ANZ   \par \uc2 1,228 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2015\par 21:39\par \uc2 Fuseworks Media\par \uc2 FUSMED\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015. Fuseworks Limited.   \par \par \uc2 HIGHLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 - A very quiet start to the week with the US on their Labor Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves recorded the largest monthly decline on record.\par \par \uc2 OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 UPCOMING TODAY: It's a busy day for local releases, with August readings for the activity and pricing sides of the economy from the ANZ Truckometer (10am) and the ANZ Monthly Inflation Gauge (1:00pm). A moderate increase is expected for Q2 manufacturing sector volumes at10:45am, with our Q2 GDP pick currently a modest +0.3% quarterly increase. The NAB business confidence survey for August is the pick of the Australian data. Chinese August trade data is due to be released today.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 : ANZ proprietary indicators will set the tone for the NZD this morning, while AUD will look to NAB business confidence and today's RBA speech on property markets and financial stability. However the event for the day is likely to be Chinese trade.\par \par \uc2 RATES: There is marginal upward pressure for kiwi rates, with some payside interest evident in London overnight.\par \par \uc2 REVIEW\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The NZD underperformed overnight as markets continue to look toward the RBNZ for a catalyst. GBP found solid strength at the start of BoE week. AUD was stable as China sentiment stabilised.\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS OVERVIEW: Monday's US holiday led to very thin trading and quiet trading conditions, with stocks, yields and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading in tight ranges overnight. European equity indices were up despite yesterday's modest fall in the Shanghai composite. Core and peripheral euro area sovereign bond yields rose, with a widening in peripheral spreads to bunds, whilst UK gilt yields eased. The CRB index was weighed down by large falls for oil prices. Gold prices also eased.\par \par \uc2 ANZ'S ASSESSMENT\par \par \uc2 POLICY DIVERGENCES CREATE DILEMMA. The G20 meeting at the weekend highlighted an overall global growth outlook falling short of expectations. The Chinese economy is slowing, despite the best efforts of policymakers who face a tricky balancing act of trying to prop the economy up versus putting in place structural reforms that will improve competitiveness. The weaker emerging market backdrop looks to have been the catalyst behind more dovish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric of late, and hence the good old fashioned \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18put' looks set to remain in place for a while yet. With odds of a September Fed hike at just 32%, the market seems to be betting that concerns over the Chinese economy and the associated financial market volatility will be enough to stay the Fed's hand, although there are sound domestic grounds for the Fed to start hiking. The reality is that there is unlikely to be a perfect time to raise rates in a fashion that would minimise fallout to emerging market economies and wider asset prices, outside of the gradual and data dependant route in which Fed members have been at pains to emphasise they will take. There are limits to what low interest rates can do, and delivering durable growth can be a bridge too far.\par \par \uc2 OVERNIGHT SPECIFICS AND KEY EVENTS\par \par \uc2 - Germany: Industrial production gained 0.7% m/m (mkt: 1.1% m/m) in July, while June was revised higher to -0.9% m/m from -1.4% m/m. The gains were broad based, with construction (3.2% m/m), energy (1.9% m/m) and manufacturing (0.3% m/m) all gaining.\par \par \uc2 - China: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for August came in at USD3557.4bn, down "only" by USD93.9bn from July. This is the largest monthly decline on record. Positive \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuation and interest gains amounted to roughly USD16bn in the month, so the actual drawdown (i.e. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) was around USD110bn, which should be seen as the minimum \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted given that we suspect PBoC also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  via forwards which don't show up in the headline \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Markets will likely read the figure as a bit on the light side. We await the PBoC net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase data due in a week or so to provide another indication of the likely size of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 - Australia: Recent market volatility and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related news flow may have weighed on today's Australian NAB business confidence report but we will watch business conditions more closely given the positive trend in the AiG business surveys.\par \par \uc2 - Despite yesterday's 2.5% fall in the Shanghai composite, European equity indices were up overnight. The Euro Stoxx 50 gained 0.6%, the DAX was up 0.7% and the CAC40 rose 0.6%, which follows falls towards the end of last week. Miners posted the biggest gain of the 19 industry groups on the Euro Stoxx 600. \uc2 Glencore Plc\uc2  jumped 7 percent after saying it will sell assets and shares to cut net debt. The FTSE 100 was up 0.5% overnight.\par \par \uc2 - European Government bond yields were up in thin trading. Ten year German bund yields were up 1bp to 0.67%, with yields for Italy, Spain, and Portugal up 2bps, 7bps and 6bps respectively. Yields on 10 year UK gilts fell 2bps to 1.81%, likely helped by the reinvestment of maturing debt into longer-dated gilts as part of the BoE QE program\par \par \uc2 - Commodity prices were down again, with the CRB index down 0.9% at the time of writing (7am NZT). Energy prices led the falls (down 2.5%), with milder falls for livestock, precious metals and industrials. Prices for soft commodities and grains rose. Gold prices were down 0.2% to a two week low as investors continued to sell metal from exchange-traded products.\par \par \uc2 - Concerns that producers will be unable to co-ordinate production cuts continued to weigh on oil prices, with WTI and Brent crude down 3.9% and 4% respectively to USD44 and USD47.60 per barrel. Bloomberg reports another Russian official ruled out co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \uc2 OPEC\uc2  on cutting production, adding to over-supply concerns.\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD: ANZ PROPRIETARY INDICATORS\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 The NZD underperformed overnight continuing to slide lower despite a lack of impetuous on a quiet US Labour Day holiday session. We look to today's ANZ Truckometer, and ANZ Inflation Gauge to provide direction. Markets continue to look forward to this week's RBNZ meeting as a reason for further weakness, a theme we feel is misplaced.\par \par \uc2 Expected range: 0.6230 - 0.6320\par \par \uc2 NZD/AUD: CHINA...\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD held steady as NZD/USD fell overnight as China sentiment was more optimistic. Chinese equities dropped 2.5% after a four day weekend, but markets felt this fall was less than could have been expected. Attention will be on NAB business confidence and RBA Head of Financial Stability Luci Ellis speaking on property today. Both have potential impact for AUD.\par \par \uc2 Expected range: 0.8980 - 0.9090\par \par \uc2 NZD/EUR: GERMAN IP...\par \par \uc2 German industrial production rebounded and European equities held steady, in turn keeping EUR stable. NZD is likely to remain the driver today.\par \par \uc2 Expected range: 0.5580 - 0.5660\par \par \uc2 NZD/JPY: RELUCTANT TO RALLY\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Despite markets reducing demand for safe havens like JPY this cross remains reluctant to rally. It continues to signal heightened risk concerns.\par \par \uc2 Expected range: 73.80 - 75.30\par \par \uc2 NZD/GBP: GBP BOUNCE\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Sterling snapped a losing streak overnight bouncing off support ahead of 1.51. While there was little data to drive it GBP was the strongest G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, and NZD the weakest.\par \par \uc2 Expected range: 0.4050 - 0.4140\par \par \uc2 Dokument FUSMED0020150907eb97003e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
