\par\par \b \uc2 Stocks Try to Regain Footing -- 3rd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chiara Albanese   \par \uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2015\par 17:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Global stocks struggled to regain their footing Monday amid lingering fears over the health of the Chinese economy.\par \par \uc2 The Stoxx Europe 600 clawed back some of the losses suffered last week to trade 0.4% higher in late trade Monday, but most Asian markets fell and the Shanghai Composite Index ended 2.5% lower. U.S. stock markets are closed Monday for Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 "With U.S. markets closed today, Asia continues to be the focus for investor attention and broadly speaking we continue to see a lot of nervousness in financial markets," said Mark Dowding, senior portfolio manager at \uc2 BlueBay Asset Management\uc2 , which oversees $62 billion in assets.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves declined sharply in August, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday, a sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to limit money leaving the country and stem the yuan's fall after moving to devalue it against the dollar on Aug. 11.\par \par \uc2 China also revised its growth rate for last year to 7.3% from 7.4%. The country's growth target of about 7% this year is the slowest pace in a quarter century.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, China \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  said in remarks to the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  largest economies that the "correction in the stock market is almost done" and China's financial markets are expected to become "more stable" after the Chinese yuan steadies.\par \par \uc2 "What concerns investors is another large yuan devaluation, which would be very detrimental for markets," said Keith Wade, chief economist at \uc2 Schroders\uc2 , which oversees around GBP310 billion ($472 billion) in assets.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor's comments soothed those concerns, he said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts are bracing for more choppy trading ahead of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's Sept. 16-17 meeting, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will consider the first rise in short-term interest rates in nearly 10 years.\par \par \uc2 "Even if it does not come in 10 days' time, the pressure is building for a move. You are still going to have volatility in coming weeks. Everyone is looking at economic data very closely," said Nick Nelson, equity strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 The slight rebound in European shares Monday was being driven by investors' confidence that central banks will step in and support the economy if needed, said Daniel Fermon, a strategist at \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stocks and commodities suffered more sharp swings last week as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left the door open to more stimulus and a U.S. jobs report didn't give investors much clarity about the timing of a rate rise.\par \par \uc2 Depending on what the Fed does in September, \uc2 the ECB\uc2  could announce an extension of its bond-buying program beyond September 2016 at the next monetary-policy meeting in October, strategists at \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 Stocks fell Friday after mixed figures from the \uc2 U.S. Labor Department\uc2  showed that hiring slowed in August and the unemployment rate improved. It was the last major gauge of the job market's health before the Fed's meeting.\par \par \uc2 The Stoxx Europe 600 index closed 2.5% lower Friday, taking losses for the week to 2.8%, while U.S. stocks capped their second-worst weekly performance of the year.\par \par \uc2 "There seems little prospect of any meaningful calming of market nerves in the week ahead, even in the absence of the U.S. today due to the Labor Day holiday," said Marc Ostwald, analyst at ADM Investor Services in London, in a note.\par \par \uc2 The euro was recently 0.1% higher against the dollar at around $1.1162. Traders said that the U.S. closure contributed to lower trading volumes in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seen as vulnerable to a U.S. rate rise because an increase in rates is expected to strengthen the dollar, weakened Monday.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish lira weakened to a record of 3.0475 lira against the dollar, partly because of violence and instability in the country. The Malaysian ringgit and Indonesian rupiah fell to fresh 17-year lows against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil was down 2% at $48.60 a barrel. Gold was 0.3% lower at $1,118.50 a troy ounce.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chiara Albanese at chiara.albanese@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 07, 2015 11:00 ET (15:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150907eb970018c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
