\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Higher, Led by China\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng   \par \uc2 1,452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2015\par 04:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Shares in Shanghai traded higher Monday, lifting some markets in Asia, although investors were nervous that China's mainland market remained a potential source of volatility for the region.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite was last up 1.6%, as mainland markets reopened after a long weekend. The Nikkei Stock Average was up 0.8% and Korea's Kospi was up 0.4%.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were under pressure with the Malaysian ringgit and Singaporean dollar hitting fresh lows earlier, after the U.S. jobs report failed to provide much clarity on when the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise short-term interest rates. Higher interest rates in the U.S. are likely to give the dollar a boost, as they make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive to yield-seeking investors.\par \par \uc2 "Trading in Asia today will be driven by two major factors: The delayed response to Friday's nonfarm payrolls (NFP) number out of the U.S. and the reopening of the Chinese stocks markets after a four-day break," said Angus Nicholson, market analyst at IG in a morning note. Markets in China were closed for a national holiday on Thursday and Friday.\par \par \uc2 Worries about higher U.S. interest rates are adding to fears that China's market, down nearly 40% from its peak in June, is signaling a worse slowdown in China's economy than investors had thought. Last week, the Nikkei Stock Average fell 7%, the worst performing stock benchmark in Asia. China shares came out relatively unscathed last week, helped by a shortened trading week and government-led stocks buying.\par \par \uc2 Fears that Chinese markets will continue to drive volatility in the region persisted even after soothing comments from the governor of \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the weekend. The "correction in the stock market is almost done" and China's financial markets are expected to become "more stable" after the Chinese yuan steadies following a devaluation last month, said \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  in remarks to central bankers and finance ministers from the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  largest economies on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Most shares were higher, but Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit hit a fresh 17-year low against the U.S. dollar, which traded as high as 4.3090 ringgit. The ringgit was last down more than 1% from its level late in Asia on Friday. It has hit a string of lows recently, as the prospect of a rate increase in the U.S. draws near, weakening emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  broadly.\par \par \uc2 The Singaporean dollar fell to a fresh six-year low against the U.S. dollar which traded as high as 1.4255 Singaporean dollars. The Singaporean dollar was last up 0.1% from its level late in Asia Friday.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., the headline number on August jobs creation missed expectations, but the report provided plenty of positives that investors latched onto, including the unemployment rate falling more than forecast and stronger-than-expected 2.2% growth in average hourly wages.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fell Friday, capping their second-worst weekly performance of the year.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen was roughly flat after hitting a fresh-four month high against the euro during Asia trade Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar hit a fresh six-year low of $0.6893, adding to its gains from last week. It was last up 0.7% from its late level in Asia Friday.\par \par \uc2 --Corrie Driebusch and Lingling Wei contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Story corrected at 0449 GMT because the original misstated the movement of the Australian dollar in the 13th paragraph.\par \par \uc2 7 Sep 2015 00:45 ET  Correction to Asian Shares Higher Article\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar hit a fresh six-year low of $0.6893 against the U.S. dollar on Monday. "Asian Shares Higher, Led by China," at 0204 GMT, misstated the movement of the Australian dollar in the 13th paragraph.\par \par \uc2 7 Sep 2015 04:48 ET  China Volatility Pressures Asian Markets\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Chao Deng \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China-driven volatility returned to Asian markets Monday, with Shanghai leading most of the region's markets lower on renewed concerns about China's economy. At the same time, Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit a series of lows on worries about a U.S. interest rate rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares in Japan rose slightly but most stock benchmarks in the region fell, after China cut its growth rate for last year to 7.3% from 7.4%. Though tiny, the difference suggests that China's struggle last year to meet its official growth target was more difficult than previously realized. China's growth target last year was about 7.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, a jobs report in the U.S. Friday refocused investors' attention on the possibility the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  would raise short-term interest rates in September, sending \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Malaysian ringgit and Indonesian rupiah to fresh 17-year lows against the U.S. dollar. Higher interest rates in the U.S. are likely to give the dollar a boost, as they make the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more attractive to yield-seeking investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Shanghai, the main benchmark veered between positive and negative, before closing down 2.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors took the intraday gains--the market was up as much as 1.8% in the morning--as an opportunity to take profit after the market had been closed last Thursday and Friday for a national holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investor sentiment remains fragile," said Zheng Chunming, analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the market close, Chinese authorities announced that the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fell by $93.9 billion in August from July, as the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to shore up the yuan and prevent capital flowing out of the world's second-largest economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts had expected a drop after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily to prop up the Chinese yuan following its devaluation in mid-August. The weakening of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sparked worries that the Chinese economy was weaker than the government was letting on, sending global financial markets tumbling last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The day's losses came despite soothing comments from the governor of \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the weekend and more efforts by authorities to contain market turbulence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The "correction in the stock market is almost done" and China's financial markets are expected to become "more stable" after the Chinese yuan steadies following a devaluation last month, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zhou Xiaochuan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in remarks to central bankers and finance ministers from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of 20\f28 \uc2 \f2  largest economies on Saturday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comments from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Securities Regulatory Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  that the government will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent drastic selloffs, and is even studying a "circuit breaker" system to halt trade during times of extreme volatility also made little difference. This came after authorities tightened rules over stock-index futures trading and recently penalized three online trading platforms for the illegal provision of margin loans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, the revision of China's growth rate last year reinforced the biggest fear for global investors: China's slowdown is worse than thought.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's top economic planner said Monday that the economy is on track to meet its annual growth target of 7% this year, following a series of supportive policies by the government. China was on track to hit that target in the first half of the year, but data released in August suggested China's economic growth was off pace, contributing to a slump in global markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were under pressure after the U.S. jobs report, with the dollar trading as high as 4.31 ringgit and 14,245 rupiah. The Thai baht hit a fresh six-year low of 36.04 against the U.S. dollar, while the Philippine peso notched a five-year low of 46.95 against the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's still not a big consensus on which way the Fed is going to go" in terms of raising or not raising interest rates on September, so "the market is treading cautiously" said David Welch, managing director at Hong Kong-based brokerage Reorient Financial.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, the Singaporean dollar and Australian dollar fell to fresh six-year lows against the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks in Southeast Asia fell hardest with benchmarks in the Philippines, Malaysia and Indonesia down 1.8%, 0.9% and 2.3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei Stock Average closed up 0.4%, but shares in Australia, Hong Kong and South Korea finished lower by a fraction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Grace Zhu, Yifan Xie and Lingling Wei contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 07, 2015 04:48 ET (08:48 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150907eb97000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
