\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Investors buckle up for expected drop in China's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves\b0\par\par\uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2015\par 02:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to preview data now expected this week)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Global investors are bracing for data this week that could show a big drop in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its shock devaluation last month.\par \par \uc2 China has been so surprised by the global market reaction to its devaluation that it is likely to keep the yuan on a tight leash in the near-term to head off fears of a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, policy insiders told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 But many traders believe there is political pressure to allow a deeper depreciation in coming months as the world's second-largest economy slows.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's largest, have been falling from a record $3.99 trillion in June 2014 due to a wave of capital outflows fuelled by jitters about its economic slowdown and an expected move by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by $42.5 billion in July to $3.65 trillion, the sharpest monthly drop since March.\par \par \uc2 The decline appeared to have quickened after China's near 2 percent devaluation of the yuan on Aug. 11 stoked fresh concerns about its economy. The move sparked heavy selling of the yuan, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly since then via state banks to sell dollars to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  estimate the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to $122 billion in August from about $50 billion in July.\par \par \uc2 "If the current pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues, we estimate that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  could lose up to around 14 percent of its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves (ex valuation adjustments) between June and December," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 "We estimate the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  would have to reduce the reserve requirement ratio by around 40 basis points a month just to offset the impact of its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on domestic liquidity."\par \par \uc2 Moreover, much of the money released into the economy by cutting banks' reserve requirements may merely replace funds being moved offshore, rather than finding its way into new loans to companies which would support the real economy.\par \par \uc2 "We do not believe the present policy is sustainable given the associated costs in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves depletion and liquidity imbalances," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "As such, we maintain our view that the yuan will need to depreciate further to stabilise capital outflows; we forecast a further 7 percent fall by year-end."\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll on Friday showed strategists expect the yuan to weaken another 2 percent in six months, though some were more pessimistic and predicted it would dive to 6.80 in 12 months from around 6.35 on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Nomura said on Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August could be around $170 billion, an unprecedented amount for just one month.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at Dutch bank \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  estimated that China sold up to $200 billion of reserves in the last few weeks of August alone.\par \par \uc2 Central banks will sell $1.5 trillion of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by the end of next year as they try to counter capital outflows stemming from China's slowdown, low oil prices and a rise in U.S. interest rates, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 That would mark a major shift in global capital flows, ending two decades of reserve accumulation by emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  in July shifted to reporting its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves monthly from a quarterly basis.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Judy Hua and Kevin Yao; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-7T01:14:12.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150907eb97000b7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
