\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-G20 promises transparency on rate moves as global economy disappoints\b0\par\par\uc2 1,099 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2015\par 20:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 * Chinese turmoil, Fed outlook weigh on G20 meeting\par \par \uc2 * G20 does not directly caution against Fed hikes\par \par \uc2 * Delegates seek more from China on market strains\par \par \uc2 * Experts propose two-stage introduction of bank buffers\par \par \uc2 * Inclusion of yuan in \uc2 IMF\uc2  basket under discussion (Adds U.S. Treasury Secretary)\par \par \uc2 By Jan Strupczewski and Randall Palmer\par \par \uc2 ANKARA, Sept 4 (Reuters) - World financial leaders will agree to calibrate and communicate monetary policy carefully to avoid triggering capital flight, but will not call an expected U.S. rate rise a risk to growth, a draft communique showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Many emerging market economies are concerned that when the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raises borrowing costs, investors will withdraw from other markets and buy dollar assets, weakening other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and creating turbulence as capital flees.\par \par \uc2 Officials from emerging markets wanted the communique from finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  biggest economies, meeting in Turkey, to say that a U.S. rate rise now would be a risk to growth.\par \par \uc2 But the draft avoids such wording.\par \par \uc2 "We note that in line with the improving economic outlook, monetary policy tightening is more likely in some advanced economies," the draft communique, seen by Reuters, said.\par \par \uc2 "We will carefully calibrate and clearly communicate our actions to minimize negative spillovers, mitigate uncertainty and promote transparency," said the draft, which may yet change before it is finally agreed on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 An earlier version of the text said policy tightening in developed economies "may remain one of the main sources of uncertainty in financial markets".\par \par \uc2 "In one of the wild formulations it said that this was the biggest threat to the world economy. This was killed immediately and forever," a Russian source said earlier.\par \par \uc2 The text welcomed strengthening activity in some economies but said that global growth fell short of expectations, although it expressed confidence a recovery would gain speed.\par \par \uc2 It also indirectly addressed Chinese moves that weakened its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August, in a sign these were not seen as a competitive devaluation to prop up Chinese exports. G20 members reiterated their commitment to exchange rate flexibility and would "refrain from competitive devaluations and resist all forms of protectionism," it said.\par \par \uc2 Reinforcing that message, U.S. Treasury Secretary Jack Lew told Chinese Finance Minister Lou Jiwei that it was important that China let the yuan move up as well as down, and avoid any move to lower its value to gain a competitive edge in global trade, a U.S. official said.\par \par \uc2 China told the group it was committed to continuing structural reforms and to supporting economic growth, Europe's Economic Commissioner Pierre Moscovici told reporters after the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Slower growth in China and rising market volatility have boosted the risks to the global economy, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  warned ahead of the G20 meeting, citing a mix of potential dangers such as depreciating emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and tumbling commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 But the G20 had been seen as unlikely to come up with any concrete new measures to address the spillover from instability in the world's second-largest economy, or to call directly on Beijing to address structural issues such as rising bad debts.\par \par \uc2 EASY MONEY\par \par \uc2 Luxembourg Finance Minister Pierre Gramegna, whose country holds the rotating presidency of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , shrugged off the prospect of U.S. interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 "We cannot live all the time on easy money ... One has to be realistic that at one point in time the curve of interest rates will have to change," he told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Haruhiko Kuroda said any Fed rate rise would be a positive sign for the global economy, despite the unease in some emerging markets that such moves could cause capital outflows and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 "If the U.S. were to raise rates, that would speak to the underlying firmness and growth in the U.S. economy, and that would actually be a plus for the global economy," he said.\par \par \uc2 One specific idea being examined at the Ankara meetings is a proposal from a group of financial stability experts to adopt a two-stage approach for introducing Total Loss Absorption Capacity (TLAC) buffers for big banks, a G20 source said.\par \par \uc2 The buffer is a new layer of debt big banks like \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  and \uc2 Deutsche Bank AG\uc2  must issue to write down in a crisis and bolster their capital.\par \par \uc2 The proposal would introduce a buffer of 16 percent of a bank's risk-weighted assets from 2019 and 20 percent from 2022, the source said.\par \par \uc2 The United States had pushed for 20 percent, while some in Europe had been arguing for 16 percent on the grounds that their banks were still recapitalising after the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The draft pencilled in that a deal should be ready for the endorsement of G20 leaders at their summit in southern Turkey in November, but some countries were concerned there would not be enough time to reach a final agreement by then.\par \par \uc2 There was no clear pronouncement on China's desire to have the yuan included in the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's Special Drawing Rights basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the draft said G20 finance chiefs expected progress in November, when the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has a board meeting on the issue.\par \par \uc2 "China has moved in the direction in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy ... that is necessary if they want to achieve the goal of getting China into the \uc2 IMF\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket," German Finance Minister \uc2 Wolfgang Schaeuble\uc2  told reporters, welcoming Beijing's near 2 percent yuan devaluation last month.\par \par \uc2 China is keen for the symbolic boost it would get from the yuan's inclusion.\par \par \uc2 Bundesbank chief Jens Weidmann said he is open to discussion on including the yuan in the \uc2 IMF\uc2  basket, and said China's recent market upheavals should not pose a lasting danger to the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket should in principle reflect relative global economic strengths," he told Reuters, but added China must fulfil the conditions for inclusion.\par \par \uc2 One delegate said it was possible that the likely failure of the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  to approve an \uc2 IMF\uc2  quota reform that would give China and other emerging markets more say could work in Beijing's favour on the SDR issue.\par \par \uc2 The reasoning goes that benefiting the leading emerging economy, China, could help offset the perennial failure to boost emerging market quotas.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 IMF\uc2  members will also be examining whether \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yuan market was befitting of a freely convertible reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the delegate said. \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-4T19:38:50.000Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150904eb9400hdn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
