\par\par \b \uc2 Investor flight from U.S. stocks fails to lift bond market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss  \par \uc2 1,008 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2015\par 12:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Reuters) - The "flight to safety" into bonds many expected when U.S. stocks slumped last week never took off, making big losers out of prominent fund managers and further confusing investors at a volatile time in the market.\par \par \uc2 Stocks plunged in the second half of August, largely on fears of China's worsening economy, but \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields did not see the kind of safety bid that many were expecting and has been typical in times of stock-market stress in the past.\par \par \uc2 Strategists link the lack of a move to bonds to a number of events: Hawkish rhetoric from Fed officials even as the equity market stumbled; a bout of selling by hedge funds that had expected a rally in the bond market that they didn't get; and bond sales by central banks in China and other emerging market economies trying to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from depreciating.\par \par \uc2 Reduced appetite from overseas, along with the outlook for the Fed, will be crucial in coming weeks if equities fall again and bonds don't respond. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  decision on September 17 could mark the first rate increase in almost a decade, and uncertainty surrounding that decision is likely to keep many in the bond market on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 "The correlation between bonds and stocks is more situational now because it's the central banks calling the shots," said Robert Vanden Assem, head of developed markets investment grade fixed-income at \uc2 PineBridge Investments\uc2  in New York.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 During the recent U.S. stock market sell-off in the third week of August, for instance, the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  <.SPX> dropped 9 percent, but U.S. 10-year \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yields, which move inversely to prices, fell by only 12 basis points.\par \par \uc2 That's not typical. According to \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2 , the relationship between stocks and bonds that has held since 2009 suggests 10-year yields should have declined by 22 basis points.\par \par \uc2 Bond yields since then have drifted higher and buying interest has been minimal. The lack of a rally in the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  market made big losers out of notable hedge funds, including \uc2 Bridgewater Associates\uc2 ' All-Weather Fund, which fell 4.2 percent in August.\par \par \uc2 These funds borrowed heavily to augment their returns, but as things became turbulent, that leverage generated losses that forced them to wind down that borrowing.\par \par \uc2 Strategists say part of what kept \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yields from reflexively falling through a run to safe-haven debt were hawkish signals from the Fed, particularly Fed Vice Chair \uc2 Stanley Fischer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gathering in Jackson Hole, Wyoming, several Fed officials, including Fischer, seemed to boost the odds on a rate increase if not in September, then certainly in December. The message the Fed delivered over the weekend came between a Friday and Monday that saw the U.S. Standard & Poor's 500 lose 7 percent of its value.\par \par \uc2 Leading brokerages, including \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , commented that though it's a close call, the odds favor an increase in the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Unless the U.S. economy shows signs of slowing, the bond market is likely to keep yields relatively firm," said Alan Gayle, director of asset allocation at \uc2 RidgeWorth Investments\uc2 , even if the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  falls in the weeks ahead on concern about growth in China and other emerging market economies, he said.\par \par \uc2 Interest rate futures this week saw a more than 50 percent chance of a rate hike in December <FFZ5> and a 28 percent probability in September <FFU5>, according to \uc2 CME Group\uc2 's FedWatch program.\par \par \uc2 If that happens, bonds won\u8217\'20\'19t look as attractive. Higher interest rates diminish the value of an investor's bond holdings, resulting in lower portfolio returns.\par \par \uc2 "Treasuries have been a good diversifier to portfolios, but their benefit as a diversifier has been reduced because yields are already extremely low and the Fed is starting to normalize rates," said Rick Rieder, chief investment officer of fundamental fixed income at BlackRock in New York.\par \par \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CHINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Further supporting yields on U.S. Treasuries was the sell-off in reserves by China and other emerging market economies to shore up their slumping economies. U.S. Treasuries represent the bulk of the Chinese and emerging market reserves.\par \par \uc2 Fears over weakened growth prospects and plunging commodity prices in some emerging markets have taken a toll on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As China sold \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in recent months, real yields have moved higher since the beginning of the year, while inflation expectations have declined.\par \par \uc2 China and emerging markets led the build-up in global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves following the 1997 Asian crisis to a peak of $12 trillion last year. This cash pile shielded them from the 2007-08 crisis, and it looks as if it is once again being deployed.\par \par \uc2 It's not clear whether China has sold U.S. Treasuries over the last month, or if so, how much. \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  speculated in a research note that if China sold between $7 billion to $10 billion a day of U.S. Treasuries in the three weeks since the Chinese yuan devaluation on Aug. 11, it might have dumped as much as $150 billion in \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds.\par \par \uc2 These are large numbers given the fact that the total net issuance of Treasuries this year will only be about $500 billion, said \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  in its research note. As of June 2015, China held $1.27 trillion in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  securities, according to capital flows data from the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The presence of central banks within our markets is over-riding and it's all-encompassing and has driven the activity since 2008," said PineBridge's Vanden Assem.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss; Additional reporting by Michael Connor, Richard Leong, and David Randall; Editing by David Gaffen and John Pickering) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-4T10:41:24.000Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150904eb94004xt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
