\par\par \b \uc2 China Could Keep up Its Phenomenal Yuan Support for Years: DB -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 186 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2015\par 07:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] How much did \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month? \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  estimates the amount could be in the range of $100-$150 billion, by looking at daily transaction volume in the yuan market after China devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month. "The most intensive episode of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have passed," it says. This does not strain \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves. Deutsche estimates that, even if the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  continued to put money to support the yuan at the phenomenal rate of US$90 billion per month, it could continue to comfortably go on at that pace for another 28-30 months. Moreover, as China is still running a sizable trade and current account surplus, its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves would be replenished over that period, meaning that it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  longer, it says. (jacky.wong@wsj.com; Twitter: @jackycwong)\par \par \uc2 Editor JSM\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 04, 2015 00:09 ET (04:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 04-09-15 0549GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020150904eb940000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
