\par\par \b \uc2 India Shares Fall in Early Trade -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2015\par 06:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0428 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian shares fall in early trade as investors remain concerned over the reforms process and economic growth. The \uc2 S&P\uc2  BSE Sensex is down 1.9% at 25,273.85, while the National Stock Exchange's Nifty index is also 2% lower at 7,669.20. Analysts say the market is likely to remain volatile as foreign fund selling is also weighing on the sentiment. Among the major losers, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) declines 3.6%, Vedanta (500295.BY) is 3.5% lower, while \uc2 Axis Bank\uc2  (532215.BY) falls 3.1%. However, pharmaceutical firm Cipla (500087.BY) bucks the trend and gains 1% after it agrees to buy two U.S. generic drug makers for $550 million in cash to scale up its drug offerings to American patients. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0425 GMT [Dow Jones] The oil market has already snacked on the market bulls once in the last few days with the false promise of high oil prices, and investors should watch out for another bull trap, Mohab Kamel, managing director at Magma Oil Sarl, says. On the demand side, things are looking gloomier, and once refineries start maintenance there will be large oil stock increases. "This further motivates our view that, whatever short-term volatility we get these next few days, WTI will eventually reach $32.20 and Brent $36.40," he says. \uc2 Nymex\uc2  oil futures are down 22 cents at $46.53/bbl, Brent is down 21 cents at $50.47/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] Most base metals slipped in Asian trade on Friday due to demand concerns from deteriorating economic conditions in emerging markets. The prospect of more economic stimulus in Europe had briefly pushed up prices of some metals such as copper on Thursday when the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  promised to use all tools at his disposal to support an economic recovery. Three-month copper prices on the London Metal Exchange are down by 0.8% to $5,236/ton, while aluminum futures are lower by $5 at $1,625 per ton. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] The Australian dollar may have further to fall, says Capital Economics Chief Australia & New Zealand Economist Paul Dales. "Our view that the annual rate of GDP growth in Australia will slow further in the second half of the year suggests that the Australian dollar may yet weaken from its current six-and-a-half-year low of US$0.70 to US$0.65, if not to US$0.60," he says. In particular this would be the case if "we are right in expecting the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to cut interest rates further, from their current level of 2.0%," he adds. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DAH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0419 GMT [Dow Jones] Joyce Boutique (0647.HK) plunges as much as 8.1% to HK$0.285 after the high-end fashion retailer says it expects to swing to a net loss for the first-half ended September amid tough market conditions. The multi-label boutique, which is founded by the city's fashion icon Joyce Ma, has been severely hit by reduced numbers of mainland tourists and tightened spending amid the nation's anti-corruption drive and uncertain economic outlook. The luxury retailer made a loss for the first four months (April-July) of the fiscal first-half, and expects to remain loss-making in the Aug-Sept. period. "This unfavourable performance was caused by a decrease in revenue and gross profit amidst difficult trading conditions, coupled with an increase in operating costs," it says. It posted a first-half net profit of 32.8 million Hong Kong dollars ($4.23 million) a year earlier. (joanne.chiu@wsj.com, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @joannechiuhk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] How much did \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last month? \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  estimates the amount could be in the range of $100-$150 billion, by looking at daily transaction volume in the yuan market after China devalued the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last month. "The most intensive episode of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have passed," it says. This does not strain \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's reserves. Deutsche estimates that, even if the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  continued to put money to support the yuan at the phenomenal rate of US$90 billion per month, it could continue to comfortably go on at that pace for another 28-30 months. Moreover, as China is still running a sizable trade and current account surplus, its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves would be replenished over that period, meaning that it could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  longer, it says. (jacky.wong@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jackycwong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0351 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/JPY is losing ground to hit fresh four-month low of 132.93, with weakness in the benchmark Nikkei (down 1.1% at the morning close) causing a flight to the perceived safety of yen. The pair is now at 133.02 and may fall further, says Yasuaki Amatatsu, senior analyst at global markets research at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  UFJ, adding that the pair has a room to fall further--to 132 or 131--due to the interest-rate gap between the eurozone and Japan. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks worries about capital outflow in China are overblown. "A common misperception in the market is that a decline of foreign reserves is equivalent to capital outflows," says the bank. It says the net foreign asset position of the entire banking system actually rose in the past few quarters as increase in private foreign assets held in domestic banks offset the drop in those held by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 . By the same token, even though Deutsche expects a big decline in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's foreign reserves for August, the overall capital outflow will be much smaller after accounting for private holdings. "The capital account is closed. The majority of decline in reserves likely stayed onshore in the banking sector instead of flowing out of China," it says. "This is different from balance-of-payment crises [that] happened in other emerging markets with open capital accounts, where capital flight led to a downward spiral." (jacky.wong@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jackycwong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei Hong Kong Purchasing Managers Index fell to more than a six-year-low of 44.4 in August from 48.2 in July, showing an intensified slowdown of the city's private sector economy amid a gloomy global outlook, index compiler Markit says Friday. "The data continue to point to weaker demand conditions amid an uncertain global economic outlook, as fears continue to mount over China's growth prospects," says Annabel Fiddes, economist at Markit. "The latest set of PMI data signalled that new business from Mainland China placed at Hong Kong's private sector firms dropped at the sharpest rate since late-2008, and remains a key factor weighing on overall new work," she says, suggesting the PMI is likely to remain in contraction mode (below 50) in the near term. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] Angel Broking expects India markets to open flat tracking the SGX Nifty and global cues. The SGX Nifty is down 0.6%. U.S. markets closed lackluster as traders look ahead to the release of the Labor Department's closely watched monthly jobs report on Friday for signs whether the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will raise interest rates at its meeting later this month, the broker says. If India's 50-stock CNX NIFTY trades above 7,808 levels, during the first half-an-hour of trade then it may rally up to 7,861-7,899 levels, Angel says. Conversely, if the NIFTY trades below 7,808, it may fall to 7,770 -7,716 levels. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors are clearly pessimistic about China's economy, says a Nomura client survey. Most respondents think China's economy is growing at just 5%--in a range of 4% to 7% offered by the questionnaire--and most think a sharp slowdown is coming in the second half of 2015. Most also expect the yuan to weaken further and end the year at about 6.40 to 6.80 to the U.S. dollar. (\f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last set USD/CNY at 6.36 Wednesday before onshore markets closed for the World War II holiday.) Capital outflows from China remain the biggest risk and heighten the need for more government spending to boost growth. "If there is no large stimulus, half of the respondents do not expect a growth recovery before Q2 2016," Nomura says. (Dominique.fong@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as an asset class, are not sufficiently cheap to warrant a bullish assessment on valuation grounds, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Although major emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have registered a 25% depreciation against the US dollar since the greenback rally that began in July 2014, the average decline on real effective exchange rate (REER) was only 7%, which has been modest. In terms of valuations, emerging market REER's are just 5% below their long term (10-year) average, despite the significant differences across regions and countries. Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are 3% stronger than their long-term average, primarily due to overvaluation in China, Philippines and Thailand, while Latin America and EMEA (Europe, Middle East, & Africa Countries) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are cheap compared to their historical averages by 17% and 9% respectively. However, "given near-term headwinds, such as uncertainty surrounding US monetary policy and burgeoning concerns over Chinese growth, our near-term outlook on EM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is decidedly bearish," the French bank says.(carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 04, 2015 00:28 ET (04:28 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150904eb94000bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
