\par\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Loses 747-8 Freighter Deal -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2015\par 17:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:42 EDT - \uc2 Boeing\uc2  (BA) says it's lost an order for four 747-8 freighter jets, another blow for the program as BA prepares to trim production, and a sign of the still-tough air cargo market. Net orders for the 747 are now zero for the year, though it's yet to firm up a preliminary deal for 20 from Russia's \uc2 Volga-Dnepr\uc2 . Japan's Nippon Cargo is the largest outstanding 747-8F customer, with six jets left from an order of 14. BA also added new orders for 70 of its 737 jets in weekly bookings update, including 27 for UK-based Jet2.com and 30 for China's Ruili Airlines. The Pentagon last week ordered nine 737-based P-8 surveillance jets for the Navy and four for Australia, which have been added to BA's backlog. Shares recently up 0.8% at $131.,66 (doug.cameron@wsj.com; @dougcameron)\par \par \uc2 11:37 EDT - On the surface, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve--the world's largest--gives it a lot of dry power in fending off capital flight. Yet strategists at BofA/\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  warns that while the $3.6T war chest is large, a continued burn rate of $200B/quarter could be a threat to global markets. A reversal of the QE era flows into China since 2010 would suggest another $400B could leave, while a reversal since 2008 would amount to a $700B outflow, the report warns. BAML adds that the most likely outcome is that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  may suspend \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in propping up the yuan, which would accelerate the pace of CNY weakening and be a further global disinflationary shock. Another option is that China reverses course and tightens capital controls, which would however hit its SDR inclusion chances. The third and least likely outcome is that status quo remains, which would be negative for cyclical assets linked to China. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:31 EDT - \uc2 Juniper Networks\uc2  (JNPR) quietly tweaked its executive compensation in a way that sometimes portends a takeover. Now, if a top JNPR executive is effectively demoted in a sale of the company, that constitutes a "change of control" that would trigger severance payments and other perks. Under a previous agreement, such a backhanded demotion -- for example, the CFO remaining CFO of JNPR as a subsidiary business, rather than of the combined entity -- wasn't grounds for a payout. That language has been removed this time around. It's a minor tweak, though boards rarely revisit the terms of these agreements in a vacuum. Add to that, activist hedge fund Elliott own 4.5% of JNPR and has said it thinks the company could be sold. The new contract applies to CEO Rami Rahim (due $18.7M in a change of control, per the company's latest filings) CFO \uc2 Robyn Denholm\uc2  ($11.3M), CTO \uc2 Pradeep Sindhu\uc2  ($7.2M) and others. (liz.hoffman@wsj.com; @lizrhoffman)\par \par \uc2 11:26 EDT - For the second quarter in a row, \uc2 Joy Global\uc2  (JOY) didn't get a single order for an electric mining shovel in the three months ended July 31, company officials tell analysts in a call. Joy says it is the world's biggest maker of the giant shovels, used to scoop up coal, iron ore and other materials. \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  (CAT) is a big competitor. Joy says its factories are operating at about 50% of capacity but more closures loom. It's also weighing whether it needs to write down certain assets. Fiscal 3Q earnings fell short of expectations. Shares down about 17%. (bob.hagerty@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:24 EDT - After an up-and-down start to summer, natural gas direction looks decidedly down. Prices near the end of two full weeks hovering just above three-month lows. It is the weakest stretch for gas since April. EIA says Thursday that gas stockpiles grew by 86B cubic feet last week, meeting the average forecast of analysts surveyed by The Wall Street Journal. While unsurprising, the surplus is still 40% above the five-year average for the same week. Any hope from earlier this summer that oversupply would dwindle isn't getting any support from the weekly data. (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:21 EDT - Are a stronger US economy and a weak loonie finally giving a shot in the arm to Canada's beleaguered non-resource export sector? The latest trade data for July suggests yes. Strong advances in autos and aerospace exports helped exports rise 2.3% in July. Volumes, meanwhile, rose a decent 1%. Economists say the trade report was better than expected and will provide some reassurance to the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  by suggesting non-resource exports will be a strong contributor to 3Q GDP and help offset weakness in Canada's resource sector. "Assuming markets don't totally melt down over the next week, it looks like the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  will very likely hold policy rates steady at next week's meeting," says \uc2 BMO\uc2  senior economist Benjamin Reitzes. (carolyn.m.king@wsj.com; @CarolynKing1)\par \par \uc2 11:17 EDT - Massive harvests and declining storage space in France are driving a sharp fall in European wheat prices, according to Gabriel Omnes at French research firm Strategie Grains. Milling wheat futures traded in Europe are down more 3.59% today to $147.50, while prices offered in response to a tender called by the Egyptian state wheat buyer today hit new lows for the marketing year. "There is very tough competition that is starting now for the next campaign," Omnes says, with bumper wheat crops in nearly every producer. For France to sell wheat when prices everywhere are falling, they will need to sell at a discount, he notes. (katherine.dunn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:13 EDT - Are drivers actually "saving" money by paying lower gasoline prices this holiday weekend? Motor club AAA and price-tracking website GasBuddy.com both think so. "Motorists to save $1.4B at the pump this Labor Day Weekend," GasBuddy says, noting average US prices are $2.44/gallon vs $3.43/gallon a year ago. But many drivers and consumer watchdog groups remain unimpressed, saying that paying less than the hyper-inflated prices of a year ago is not a "savings." Whatever the case, AAA makes clear: "Most U.S. drivers will pay the lowest gas prices since 2004 for the busy Labor Day weekend." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:09 EDT - Brazilian bankruptcy-protection filings increased this year as the country's sluggish economy along with high interest rate and inflation, hurts companies' \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to consumer credit-rating bureau Serasa Experian says. A total of 766 companies filed for bankruptcy-protection in the first eight months of this year, up 41.6% from the year-ago period. This is the highest level of bankruptcy-protection request by companies since 2006, when a new bankruptcy-protection law took place in Brazil. The country's economy is expected to contract 2.26% this year, according to economists. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:07 EDT - Turmoil on Chinese markets will have a "particularly negative impact" on emerging markets that benefitted from China's boom, writes RBC Capital analyst Rob Stallard, putting pressure on airlines to defer \uc2 Boeing\uc2  jet deliveries. \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's non-China and fast-growing Middle East airline backlog accounts for roughly 17% of its total order book (i.e. Indonesia, Turkey & Africa) and "even if \uc2 Boeing\uc2  sees 10% of its 2017 deliveries deferred by EM airlines, demand and double-bookings should see other customers stepping into the slots." But investor sentiment may not hold up, RBC writes, citing a 30% underperformance by aerospace stocks during the 1997-1998 Asian crisis. (jon.ostrower@wsj.com, @jonostrower)\par \par \uc2 11:01 EDT - Brazil's fight against inflation is far from over, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its benchmark rate unchanged at 14.25% Wednesday. The economy is shrinking by around 2% or more, economists say, making rate increases less likely. Pundits blame the government for prices remaining strong amid a recession. Case in point is the 2016 budget plan with a primary deficit of 0.5% of GDP, admitting that there won't be savings to pay down debt. This pokes inflation because it reduces confidence and scares off investors, weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , thus making imports more expensive. The unbalanced budget also means the government isn't reducing costs such as salaries, effectively flushing the economy with cash. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 11:01 EDT - So far only one proposed LNG plant has been deferred during this energy downturn: BG's U.S. project in Lake Charles, La., according to a new analysis from Wood Mackenzie. "But BG's postponement has been an exception," said VP Noel Tomnay. \uc2 Exxon\uc2 , Eni, Anadarko, Shell, Petronas and Woodside are holding out until the end of 2016 before they decide whether to pull the plug on their projects, postpone them or move forward. "Postponement could invalidate contracts for the portion of project LNG sold so far, and jeopardize hard-won stakeholder support, including from local communities. Some developers may be worried that a loss of momentum could favour their competitors and that a project postponement may be tantamount to a cancellation," Tomnay said. "Development of even half of this proposed supply could prolong the Asian oversupply to 2025," he said. Wood Mackenzie projects that the global LNG market is awash in gas and doesn't need this many new plants. "As companies confront this reality, a raft of project postponements will follow." (lynn.cook@wsj.com; @LynnJCook)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 03, 2015 11:42 ET (15:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150903eb93001w8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
