ddress \par info@carrywealth.com                           info@carrywealth.com \par  \par  \par  \par Stock Code \par 643                                            643 \par  \par  \par  \par Board Lot Size \par                                                10,000 \par 10,000 shares \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Sep 2015 04:38 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 4   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par     The board of directors (the "Board") of Carry Wealth \par  \par  \par  \par     Holdings Limited (the "Company") presents the Interim \par  \par  \par     Report and condensed consolidated financial information of \par  \par     the Company and its subsidiaries (collectively the "Group") for \par  \par     the six months ended 30 June 2015. The consolidated income \par  \par     statement, consolidated statement of comprehensive income, \par  \par     consolidated statement of changes in equity and consolidated \par  \par     cash flow statement of the Group for the six months ended \par  \par     30 June 2015, and the consolidated balance sheet as at 30 \par  \par     June 2015 of the Group, all of which are unaudited and \par                                                                              1532 \par     condensed, along with selected explanatory notes, are set out \par     on pages 15 to 32 of this Report. \par  \par  \par  \par     MANAGEMENT DISCUSSION AND \par     ANALYSIS \par  \par  \par     Results and Business Review \par  \par     For the six months ended 30 June 2015, the Group's \par  \par     revenues amounted to HK$111.7 million (2014: HK$78.6 \par  \par     million) and gross profit was HK$42.2 million (2014: HK$9.4 \par  \par     million). Profit attributable to equity holders was HK$14.3 \par  \par  \par     million (2014: loss of HK$13.8 million). The basic and diluted \par  \par     earnings per share were 1.77 HK cents and 1.75 HK cents \par  \par     respectively (2014: basic and diluted loss per share were both \par                                                                               1.77  1.75 \par     1.85 HK cents). \par  \par                                                                        1.85 \par  \par  \par     During the period under review, the US economy has \par  \par     continued its moderate growth. With the improvement in the \par  \par     job market, lower fuel prices and strengthening housing \par  \par     market, consumer sentiment has gradually revived. However, \par  \par     the world's second largest economy, Mainland China, was \par  \par     losing growth momentum. Economic data has indicated that \par  \par     factory activity and market consumption in the country were \par  \par     weakening. While in the Euro zone, consumer confidence was \par  \par     still weak even after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had launched \par  \par     quantitative easing to stimulate economic growth. Countries \par  \par     such as Greece, Spain and Italy still faced crippling levels of \par  \par     debt and high unemployment. Despite this unstable business \par  \par     environment, the Group's revenue from the garment \par  \par     manufacturing and trading business was maintained at \par     HK$79.5 million (2014: HK$79.0 million) and the gross profit \par     amounted to HK$9.9 million (2014: HK$9.8 million). \par  \par                                                                                                      5 \par                                                                        Interim Report 2015 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                                    7% \par With a target to achieve GDP growth of 7%, Mainland \par  \par China's government has implemented monetary policies \par  \par including reduction of bank interest rates and reserve ratios \par  \par to encourage funds to flow into the financial market so as to \par  \par stimulate the real economy. With such a massive injection of \par  \par liquidity further boosted by margin financing of stock trading, \par  \par the Hong Kong \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index and particularly the \par  \par Shanghai Composite Index skyrocketed during the review \par  \par period. For the six months ended 30 June 2015, the Group's \par  \par securities investment business recorded a revenue and gross \par  \par  \par profit of HK$32.3 million (2014: gross loss of HK$0.4 million). \par  \par  \par Despite a general increase in factory costs, the selling \par                                                                             1.8 \par  \par expenses to the garment manufacturing and trading revenue \par                                                                   1.8 \par  \par ratio was maintained at 1.8 (2014: 1.8) and the \par  \par administrative expenses increased slightly to HK$22.7 million \par  \par  \par (2014: HK$22.3 million). \par  \par  \par  \par Segment Analysis \par  \par  \par a)   Garment manufacturing and trading segment                    a) \par  \par  \par      In the US, both the job and consumer markets \par  \par      continued their improvement. Household consumption \par  \par      and spending was also increasing. As a result, revenue \par                                                                        18.2% \par      from the US customers increased by 18.2% to HK$29.7 \par  \par      million (2014: HK$25.2 million) and accounted for \par                                                                            37.4% \par      37.4% (2014: 31.8%) of the segment revenue. \par  \par                                                                        31.8% \par  \par  \par      Affected by the slowest economic growth rate in \par  \par      Mainland China since 2009, market demand and \par  \par      household spending dropped. However, with the \par  \par      concerted effort of the Group's sales team, revenue \par  \par      from Mainland China customers remained at HK$39.4 \par  \par      million (2014: HK$39.4 million) which accounted for \par                                                                           49.5% \par      49.5% (2014: 49.8%) of the segment revenue. Revenue \par                                                                        49.8% \par      from European customers was HK$7.8 million (2014: \par  \par      HK$7.6 million) and accounted for 9.8% (2014: 9.6%) of \par  \par      the segment revenue. \par                                                                         9.8%9.6% \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Sep 2015 04:38 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . -4-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 6   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par          In summary, revenue from the garment manufacturing \par  \par          and trading segment was HK$79.5 million (2014: \par  \par  \par          HK$79.0 million) and recorded a loss of HK$2.3 million \par  \par          (2014: HK$2.5 million). \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par     b)   Securities investment segment                              b) \par  \par  \par          In the period under review, triggered mainly by massive \par  \par          funds from Mainland China investors, the Hong Kong \par  \par          stock market surged with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index reaching \par                                                                            28,442 \par          a seven-year high of 28,442 points on 28 April 2015. \par  \par          The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index closed at 26,250 points on 30 \par                                                                                26,250 \par          June 2015 (31 December 2014: 23,605 points). The \par                                                                            23,605 \par  \par          Group's securities investment revenue recorded a net \par  \par          gain of HK$32.3 million (2014: net loss of HK$0.4 \par  \par          million) and the profit from this segment was HK$26.1 \par  \par  \par          million (2014: loss of HK$2.1 million). As at 30 June \par  \par          2015, the Group held a portfolio of Hong Kong-listed \par  \par          equity securities with a fair value of HK$155.2 million \par  \par          (31 December 2014: HK$113.7 million). \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par     Liquidity and Financial Resources \par  \par     Adhering    to   a   conser vative   financial   management \par  \par     methodology, the Group continued to maintain a healthy \par  \par     financial position. As at 30 June 2015, the Group's cash and \par  \par     bank deposits totalled HK$111.5 million (31 December 2014: \par  \par     HK$118.6 million). Working capital represented by net current \par  \par     assets amounted to HK$266.1 million (31 December 2014: \par  \par     HK$248.0 million). The Group's current ratio was 9.2 (31 \par  \par     December 2014: 8.1). \par                                                                            9.2 \par  \par                                                                      8.1 \par  \par  \par  \par     Capital Expenditure \par  \par     For the period under review, the Group incurred a total \par  \par     capital expenditure of HK$0.4 million (2014: HK$0.1 million) \par  \par  \par     for the additions of leasehold improvement and office \par     equipment. \par  \par                                                                                                       7 \par                                                                         Interim Report 2015 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Exposure \par  \par The Group's sales are principally transacted in US dollars and \par  \par Renminbi. With a factory located in Mainland China and \par  \par offices in Hong Kong and Mainland China, operating \par  \par expenses of the Group are primarily denominated in Hong \par  \par Kong dollars and Renminbi with some expenses also in US \par  \par dollars. The Group is mainly exposed to US dollar exchange \par  \par rate risk arising from sales transactions. \par  \par  \par The Group will closely monitor fluctuations in the value of the \par  \par US dollar and, if necessary, will enter into forward exchange \par  \par contracts to reduce exchange fluctuation risks. \par  \par  \par  \par Credit Policy \par  \par Consistent with prevailing industry practice, the Group's \par  \par business was transacted on an open account basis with its \par  \par long-standing customers. The credit ratings of customers are \par  \par constantly reviewed and their respective credit limits adjusted, \par  \par as and when necessary. \par  \par  \par  \par Charges on Assets \par  \par As at 30 June 2015, the Group had no charges on assets. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par Contingent Liabilities \par  \par As at 30 June 2015, the Group had no contingent liabilities. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par Human Resources and Remuneration Policies \par  \par The Group provides a harmonious working environment to \par  \par employees whose commitment and expertise are critical to \par  \par the long-term success of its business. The Group offers \par  \par employees rewarding careers and provides them with a \par  \par variety of training programmes aimed at enhancing their \par  \par professionalism. It rewards employees according to prevailing \par  \par market practices, individual experience and performance. To \par  \par attract and retain high calibre employees, the Group also \par  \par offers discretionary bonuses and share options to staff \par members based on performance of the individual as well as \par the Group. \par  \par  \par As at 30 June 2015, the Group had a total of 940 (31 \par                                                                       940 \par December 2014: 957) full-time employees in Mainland China \par                                                                     957 \par  \par and Hong Kong. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2 Sep 2015 04:39 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . -5-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 8   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par     Outlook \par  \par     At the start of second half of 2015, the volatile Mainland \par  \par     China stock markets and Greek rejection of the austerity \par  \par     measures imposed by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  had created \par  \par     instability which could adversely affect the continued global \par  \par     economic recovery. Looking ahead, growth in major \par  \par     economies could well follow divergent courses. \par  \par  \par  \par     For the US market, the Group expects the economy there to \par  \par     recover gradually even if interest rates hikes are raised during \par  \par     2015. With stronger economic fundamentals than before, the \par  \par     anticipated rise of interest rates should not significantly affect \par  \par     the momentum of the US recovery. Instead, the interest rate \par  \par     hikes would deliver a positive message that US economy is \par  \par     strong enough to wind down the unconventional monetary \par     policy and return to normal. \par  \par  \par     In Mainland China, the central government had injected \par  \par     liquidity to the economy with an aim to meet the growth \par  \par     target set for 2015. While we have yet to see whether the \par  \par     looser monetary policy will stimulate real economic growth, \par  \par     money had flowed into the stock markets which \par  \par     subsequently experienced significant adjustments starting in \par  \par     mid-June 2015. Drastic administrative measures were \par  \par     immediately taken by the central government to avoid a \par  \par     further stock crash. Such measures are essential to avoid \par  \par     material set backs on structural reforms. Short term market \par  \par     disruption is unavoidable and it will take time to restore \par     market confidence in China's stock market. \par  \par  \par     While in the Euro zone, the region has shown signs of slow \par  \par     recovery following the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's large scale \par  \par     quantitative easing. Though Greece represents less than 2% \par                                                                           2% \par     of the Euro zone GDP, markets are concerned the recent \par  \par     Greek debt crisis will unleash a chain reaction in which other \par  \par     heavily indebted countries such as Portugal, Italy and Spain \par  \par     may initiate similar policies and affect Euro zone unity. \par  \par     Indeed, mutual commitments and trust among Euro zone \par  \par     countries are vital for the region's economic recovery to \par     persist. \par  \par  \par     As for the securities investment business, the stock market in \par  \par     Hong Kong had been adversely affected by Mainland China's \par  \par     stock markets. The aggressive administrative measures \par  \par                                                                                                    9 \par                                                                      Interim Report 2015 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par executed by China and a sea of Chinese companies halting \par  \par trading of their shares have aroused the concern of \par  \par international investors. There were signs of capital withdrawal \par                                                                     A \par from Hong Kong listed A-share funds immediately after \par  \par Mainland \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  launched harsh and direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its \par  \par stock markets. On the other hand, the expected US interest \par  \par rates hike and any disruption of the unity of countries within \par  \par the Euro zone could well trigger disturbances in stock \par  \par markets. Though the current low valuation of the Hong Kong \par  \par stock market and the expected opening of the Shenzhen- \par Hong Kong connect could be positive factors for investment \par opportunities, the Group anticipates pressure on its securities \par investment result during the second half of 2015 in view of \par the recent plunge in the Hong Kong stock market. \par  \par  \par  \par Looking ahead, we will continue to execute the established \par  \par business strategies and seize development opportunities \par  \par through focusing on our current business segments. At the \par  \par same time we will carefully explore diversification into other \par  \par potentially lucrative areas. As always, the Group pledges to \par  \par seek and evaluate every opportunity and will strive to achieve \par  \par long-term sustainable growth to maximize returns to its \par shareholders. \par  \par  \par  \par INTERIM DIVIDEND \par  \par  \par The Board has resolved not to declare any interim dividend \par  \par for the six months ended 30 June 2015 (2014: nil). \par  \par  \par  \par  \par PURCHASE, SALE OR REDEMPTION OF \par  \par THE COMPANY'S LISTED SECURITIES \par  \par  \par Neither the Company nor any of its subsidiaries purchased, \par 