\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's yuan slips on pre-holiday dollar demand despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2015\par 06:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Sept 2 (Reuters) - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yuan weakened slightly\par on Wednesday as dollar demand ahead of public holidays the\par following two days offset a stronger official midpoint and\par state-bank dollar sales on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders\par said.\par     "Many people bought dollars today as banks need \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prepare for possible demand from their customers\par during the holiday," said one trader at a foreign bank in\par Shanghai.\par     On Wednesday, "some major state-owned banks sold dollars to\par help keep the yuan steady in line with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's firmer\par midpoint," said another trader a foreign bank in Shanghai.\par     Despite holidays on Thursday and Friday, most Chinese banks\par will keep their main businesses running to meet corporate demand\par from companies doing business globally. \par     Financial markets will reopen on Monday.\par     For the rest of Wednesday, traders said they expected the\par yuan was likely to retain the trends seen early in the day.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate \par at 6.3619 per dollar prior to market open, 0.21 percent firmer\par than the previous fix of 6.3752. \par     The spot market opened at 6.3637 per dollar and\par was changing hands at 6.3650 at midday, 0.01 percent weaker from\par the previous day's close.\par     Trading was relatively sluggish ahead of the holiday and\par because of reduced speculation after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  informed\par commercial banks about new rules traders said it plans to\par implement curbing anticipation of yuan depreciation in the\par forwards markets.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will tighten rules on trading of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par forwards from October, sources with direct knowledge of the\par matter told Reuters on Tuesday, in a move to curb speculation\par and volatility in the wake of the Aug. 11 shock devaluation of\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     The offshore yuan was trading 1.10 percent weaker\par than the onshore spot at 6.4355 per dollar.\par     Although that is still a steep discount to the onshore spot\par level, the gap has closed somewhat since news of the coming\par forwards policy, which traders believe will reduce arbitrage\par opportunities between the onshore and offshore markets.\par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par , considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.602, or 3.64 percent weaker than Wednesday's midpoint.\par     \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint      6.3619   6.3752    0.21%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.365    6.3645    -0.01%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    0.05%              \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change ytd                       -2.53%\par  Spot change since 2005                30.03%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.4355    -1.10%\par           *                      \par  Offshore              6.602     -3.64%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par     GRAPHICS:\par     The yuan in trade-weighted terms \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/sed74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/sed74t}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     The spot market versus the trading band \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://reut.rs/1MhsqOi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://reut.rs/1MhsqOi}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \par  \par \par  (Reporting by the Shanghai Newsroom; Editing by Richard Borsuk)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-2T05:41:57.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150902eb92006bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
