\par\par \b \uc2 AXA-Iggo\u8217\'20\'19s insight 28 August 2015 - A view from the bond market\b0\par\par\uc2 2,361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2015\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2015, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 Release date - 28082015\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been quite a week for equity and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The fear that is driving volatility in emerging markets is that growth is very soft in China and that this will generate another round of global deflationary concerns. Those that believe this think a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  (Fed) rate hike will make things worse. The fears are probably overdone. We know China is growing more slowly but there is not universal agreement on just how weak it is. One interesting thing in the bond market is that core bond yields are pretty much unchanged on the week. In this week\u8217\'20\'19s note I discuss some of the structural developments that may be associated with the current emerging market malaise and how that plays out in global markets. Emerging market (EM) \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are under pressure, EM central banks need to prevent nasty \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shocks so they are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That means selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy their own. That foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  they own has been in the form of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve holdings, mostly invested in US Treasuries and other developed market government bonds. As EM reserves fall, Treasuries are sold and yields go up. Where is this not happening? Europe, that\u8217\'20\'19s where. So, stay long European fixed income versus the US. It\u8217\'20\'19s not a case of \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) versus the Fed, it\u8217\'20\'19s more like ECB versus the \uc2 People\u8217\'20\'19s Bank of China\uc2  (\uc2 PBoC\uc2 ). One is buying core bonds, the other is selling.\par \par \uc2 The role of reserves\par \par \uc2 One of the stories to come out of this week\u8217\'20\'19s market volatility is that of a potential reversal of one of the structural factors that has been a key influence on global bond markets in recent years. It is again a story with China at its core. Simply put, China\u8217\'20\'19s export driven growth success of the last two decades generated a large trade surplus for China and a structural increase in the demand for global commodities that pushed up commodity prices. In turn this boosted trade surpluses of commodity producing nations. In a totally free market these flows would result in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation but China and other emerging market economies did not want their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continuously strengthen against the dollar. So they engaged in an ongoing process of selling their domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buying dollars as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. One can think of this as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  balance sheet expansion. The dollars earned from exports were rising faster than domestic monetary growth, thus creating a demand/supply mismatch that would lead to the price of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising versus the dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the form of printing money to buy the incoming dollars, increases the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  balance sheet - assets, in the form of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, grow, liabilities, in terms of the private sectors claims on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , grow as money is created. Not only emerging market central banks did this. Famously, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  had to abandon this policy earlier in 2015 and let the Swiss Franc appreciate as its balance sheet was just getting too big.\par \par \uc2 $12trillion\par \par \uc2 These mercantilist policies did not go unchallenged. The United States has frequently criticised China\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy over the last 20 years. As I mentioned last week, the narrative for a long time was that at some point the Chinese authorities would need to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate to help reduce global imbalances. That never happened and now things have changed. But back to the story. Reserve accumulation of current account surplus countries is basically treated as a capital outflow in balance of payments accounting (each dollar incoming on the current account is mirrored by a dollar outgoing - into reserves - on the capital account). Those dollars need to be invested in something and the asset class of choice has been US Treasuries. Just to give some idea of the scale: according to \uc2 IMF\uc2  data, the combined dollar value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of the BRIC countries (Brazil, Russia, India and China) stood at $1.3trillion in August 2006. By August 2014 this had grown to $4.8trillion. A rapid increase in reserves was also seen amongst Middle Eastern and other oil producers. Total global foreign reserves grew from just under $5trillion in 2006 to just under $12trillion by the end of 201.\par \par \uc2 Potential reversal of bullish global bond factors\par \par \uc2 Globalisation helped create these large surpluses and reserve growth and probably also contributed to the moderation in world inflation as cheap exports undercut the ability of traditional exports to compete. The combination explains a lot of the secular decline in bond yields. Lower inflation allowed interest rates to decline while the increase in reserves created a massive demand from central banks and sovereign wealth funds for high quality bonds - US Treasuries and other G7 government assets. A big thesis here then, is that this process has ended; China is no longer the cheapest producer. While in the heyday of globalisation, emerging markets were able to capitalise on cheap real exchange rates (because labour costs were so much lower) this is no longer the case and now slower growth points to weaker real exchange rates again through nominal depreciation. So the \u8216\'20\'18cheap Chinese exports\u8217\'20\'19 argument for continued disinflation is not as strong as it was in the 80s, 90s and 00s. Secondly, the reserve accumulation has come to an end. Reserves of emerging market economies are falling. China\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have dropped by $200bn this year (end of July) and the annual growth of its reserves has turned negative. This means, all other things being equal, that the marginal buying of US Treasuries and other bonds has diminished. The reason they are selling bonds and reducing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is to prevent a serious collapse of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when export and growth dynamics have turned negative. As private capital exits emerging markets at the same time that trade surpluses are falling, this puts downward pressure on the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The central banks then has to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings in order to buy domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and try and stabilise the value. The market chatter is that the \uc2 People\u8217\'20\'19s Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been very actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the renminbi from falling further against the dollar in the wake of the recently official adjustment to the peg. It is selling reserves to finance buying of its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to offset capital outflows that are being driven by fears of much slower Chinese growth.\par \par \uc2 US Treasuries at risk\par \par \uc2 Since the financial crisis and until 2014 the bond market also benefited from Quantitative Easing (QE). So not only were emerging market central banks buying Treasuries, but so was the Fed when it started to increase its balance sheet. The whole thing became supercharged as zero US interest rates encouraged capital to flow into (the then) growing emerging market economies, boosting domestic capital growth and requiring central banks to offset that even more through reserve accumulation. The taper tantrum of May 2013 probably marked the beginning of the end. Now we could be on the verge of a scenario that sees a reversal in the trend of declining global goods prices, a partial reversal in US monetary policy and a reversal of the balance sheet expansion that allowed emerging market central banks to grow their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The upshot? Significantly higher \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  yields. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  is no longer a marginal buyer of Treasuries and emerging markets have become marginal sellers. This could be offset with private sector buying, but I somehow doubt it when the macro evidence in the US points to a need for monetary tightening.\par \par \uc2 Shifting sands\par \par \uc2 Much has been written about the longer-term secular reasons for lower long-term bond yields. Demographic trends supporting the need for fixed income asset accumulation in private pensions, regulatory pressures for financial institutions to hold more fixed income, the great moderation in inflation as well as massive reserve accumulation. China\u8217\'20\'19s great development adjustment process will inevitably lead to lower external surpluses as it emphasises domestic demand over external trade. While China still has a huge pile of foreign reserves it can\u8217\'20\'19t keep on defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for ever so we are likely to see either \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  yields a lot higher as China runs down its holdings to hold the renminbi, or we will see a sharply lower exchange rate. With those risks in mind it is important that the growth story improves. China cut interest rates this week and there is a lot of speculation about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market, as well as in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but what is really needed is evidence that the economy is still growing.\par \par \uc2 Hysteria\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been a cracking week for commentators. The level of extrapolation from every move in equities, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds is quite breath taking. A more sober assessment of what is happening has to consider the structural backdrop for financial markets - valuations today have been driven by extraordinary monetary policies and the globalisation of capital flows. There may be trouble ahead as these things change. There is still no strong signal from the Fed about its near term intentions, but a September hike is looking less likely even though I strongly believe that the Fed should raise rates. The data has been strong - the Q2 GDP revision was better than expected at 3.7% and durable goods order rose by 2.0% in July, perhaps pointing to some improvement in capex spending. Inflation is still not seen as a concern for the Fed but if you take the last six months\u8217\'20\'19 worth of CPI data, the annualised increase is well above 2.0%.\par \par \uc2 3-year extremes in valuation\par \par \uc2 In the bond market the adjustments to valuations have been mostly seen in inflation break-evens, high yield spreads and in emerging markets. Inflation break-evens are well correlated with high yield and emerging market spreads and all are close to 3-year extreme valuations. In the short term we might see some correction to these moves and fixed income investors looking for ways to add alpha in the remainder of the year are more likely to find opportunities in these sub-asset classes than in investment grade or government bonds. The \u8216\'20\'18safest\u8217\'20\'19 part of the market is probably European government bonds where QE remains a considerable force and where the market is not as subject to reserve selling as in the US. So a combination of peripheral debt, Bunds, US break-evens and high yield looks an attractive strategy at the moment. For emerging markets the pressure might remain on for a while. Outflows from the asset class have been significant and the worries about China are not going away. However, we may have seen peak selling.\par \par \uc2 The Road to Milano\par \par \uc2 So Rooney didn\u8217\'20\'19t score against Newcastle (but the one disallowed for offside should have stood) but he did bag a hat-trick against Brugge in the week. That confirmed United\u8217\'20\'19s place in the Champions League proper. There should be some great matches ahead with Barcelona and Bayern Munich being the favourites in my view. English clubs need to make a better fist of it this year and on current form and with the potential recruitment of Kevin de Bruyne, City should do better than they have the last few times out. Of course, if they hire the Wolfsburg player it does the Reds a favour - we\u8217\'20\'19ve got the last season\u8217\'20\'19s Bundesliga runners-up in our group. Amazing to see no Milan team in this year\u8217\'20\'19s competition. A shame because the final is at the San Siro. Yet there are teams from Kazakhstan and Israel. Europe can mean many things.....\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend everyone.\par \par \uc2 Chris\par \par \uc2 Chris Iggo\par \par \uc2 CIO Fixed Income, \uc2 AXA Investment\uc2  Managers\par \par \uc2 All data sourced by \uc2 AXA\uc2  IM as at 28 August 2015.\par \par \uc2 Press contacts\par \par \uc2 Jayne Adair\par \par \uc2 Tuulike Tuulas\par \par \uc2 Amy Butler\par \par \uc2 +44 20 7003 2232\par \par \uc2 +44 20 7003 2233\par \par \uc2 +44 20 7003 2231\par \par \uc2 Jayne.Adair@axa-im.com\par \par \uc2 Tuulike.Tuulas@axa-im.com\par \par \uc2 Amy.Butler@axa-im.com\par \par \uc2 The opinions expressed here are the views of the author and do not constitute investment advice. This is not a recommendation to purchase, sell or subscribe to financial instruments, an offer to sell investment funds or an offer of financial services.\par \par \uc2 About \uc2 AXA Investment\uc2  Managers\par \par \uc2 AXA Investment\uc2  Managers is a global asset management company combining rigorous risk monitoring with expertise across multiple asset classes to help clients meet their financial needs. With approximately EUR694 billion in assets under management as of the end of June 2015, \uc2 AXA\uc2  IM employs over 2,300 people around the world and operates out of 28 offices in 21 countries. \uc2 AXA\uc2  IM is part of the \uc2 AXA Group\uc2 , a global leader in financial protection and wealth management.\par \par \uc2 Follow us on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @AXAIM\par \par \uc2 Visit our media centre\par \par \uc2 AXA Investment Managers UK Limited\uc2  is authorised and regulated by the \uc2 Financial Conduct Authority\uc2 . This press release is as dated. This document is aimed solely at the media and is for information purposes only. It must not be forwarded to retail investors under any circumstances. The opinions expressed here are the views of the author and do not constitute investment advice. This is not a recommendation to purchase, sell or subscribe to financial instruments, an offer to sell investment funds or an offer of