\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's yuan up on suspected  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , set for August loss\b0\par\par\uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2015\par 05:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI Aug 31 (Reuters) - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yuan firmed on Monday\par after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a stronger guidance rate while it\par also appeared to continue using state-owned banks to support the\par Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said.\par     For August, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is set to depreciate 2.7 percent if\par it closes at the midday level. Most of the losses came after the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 surprised markets by devaluing the\par yuan by nearly 2 percent on Aug. 11.\par     "Major state-owned banks bought a large amount of yuan this\par morning," said a trader at an Asian bank in Shanghai. \par     These banks were suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  around 6.38 against the dollar, several traders\par said.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate at 6.3893 per\par dollar prior to market open, 0.15 percent firmer than the\par previous fix 6.3986. \par     The spot market opened at 6.3890 per dollar and\par was changing hands at 6.3805 at midday, 0.13 percent stronger\par than the previous close.\par     After the abrupt devaluation on Aug. 11, Beijing appears to\par have been so surprised by the global reaction that it tends to\par keep the yuan on a tight leash to head off a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war that\par could spark a broader financial crisis, policy insiders say.\par  \par     Premier \f28 \uc2 \f2 Li Keqiang\f28 \uc2 \f2  reiterated at the weekend earlier remarks\par that there's no basis for continued depreciation of the yuan\par following its devaluation.\par     The yuan "will stay basically stable as a reasonable and\par balanced level," he said. Most market watchers, however, believe\par there is political pressure for it to weaken further to support\par exports and reflecting expectations of further policy easing.\par     The offshore yuan was trading 1.03 percent weaker\par than the onshore spot at 6.4469 per dollar by midday. \par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par , considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.6075, or 3.30 percent weaker than Monday's midpoint.\par \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint      6.3893   6.3986    0.15%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.3805   6.3885    0.13%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    -0.14%             \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change ytd                       -2.77%\par  Spot change since 2005                29.72%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.4469    