\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's yuan up on suspected  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , set for August loss\b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2015\par 06:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI\u65292\'FF\'0C (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan firmed on Monday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a stronger guidance rate while it also appeared to continue using state-owned banks to support the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.\par \par \uc2 For August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to depreciate 2.7 percent if it closes at the midday level. Most of the losses came after the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 surprised markets by devaluing the yuan by nearly 2 percent on Aug. 11.\par \par \uc2 "Major state-owned banks bought a large amount of yuan this morning," said a trader at an Asian bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 These banks were suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 6.38 against the dollar, several traders said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  set the midpoint rate <CNY=SAEC> at 6.3893 per dollar prior to market open, 0.15 percent firmer than the previous fix 6.3986.\par \par \uc2 The spot market <CNY=CFXS> opened at 6.3890 per dollar and was changing hands at 6.3805 at midday, 0.13 percent stronger than the previous close.\par \par \uc2 After the abrupt devaluation on Aug. 11, Beijing appears to have been so surprised by the global reaction that it tends to keep the yuan on a tight leash to head off a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war that could spark a broader financial crisis, policy insiders say.\par \par \uc2 Premier \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  reiterated at the weekend earlier remarks that there's no basis for continued depreciation of the yuan following its devaluation.\par \par \uc2 The yuan "will stay basically stable as a reasonable and balanced level," he said. Most market watchers, however, believe there is political pressure for it to weaken further to support exports and reflecting expectations of further policy easing.\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan <CNH=D3> was trading 1.03 percent weaker than the onshore spot at 6.4469 per dollar by midday.\par \par \uc2 Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts <CNY1YNDFOR=>, considered the best available proxy for forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded at 6.6075, or 3.30 percent weaker than Monday's midpoint.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by the Shanghai Newroom; Editing by \uc2 Jacqueline Wong\uc2 ) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-31T04:08:19.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150831eb8v0040h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
