\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Trader: China Still A Threat To Stocks -- Barron's\b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2015\par 06:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par M7\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM BARRON'S 8/31/15) \par    By Shuli Ren \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A big factor in the market turmoil early last week was investors' fear that China's economy is slowing. But, even as stocks around the globe recovered, many investors appeared to be ignoring a crucial fact: There are no signs that the Chinese have a solid plan to solve their economic problems. So, stocks' scary roller-coaster ride could continue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Ahead of a military parade celebrating the 70th anniversary of the World War II victory over Japan this coming Thursday, Beijing seems to be trying to prop up the markets again. That helped stocks rally 10% in the last two days of the week, and on Friday, the yuan had its biggest one-day gain since April.\par \par \uc2 The perception of manipulation hurts China's credibility, because it had said that it wouldn't try to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets again. On Aug. 14, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Securities Finance Corp., which in July shot to fame by writing big checks to purchase shares, said that it would no longer do that, unless there was a "systemic risk." The government also said that China's National Social Security Fund and insurers wouldn't be required to "do their service to the country" by buying stocks. But on Friday, Beijing announced that the nation's two trillion yuan ($313 billion) provincial pension funds would be ready to invest in equities soon.\par \par \uc2 In addition, two weeks ago, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  devalued the yuan, but after letting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slip for three days, it began supporting it with large buy orders, draining liquidity out of the banking system. As a result, China recently had to cut interest rates and lower banks' reserve requirements, to make sure there wasn't a liquidity crunch.\par \par \uc2 The seemingly conflicting moves might indicate that Beijing is seeking a gradual, orderly devaluation. But this might only exacerbate capital outflows, rather than boost the economy and markets. Normally, devaluations take a big-bang approach, so people don't have the time to take their money out and exchange it for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  before it loses more value, says David Cui, a \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  strategist.\par \par \uc2 China's $3.6 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve looks huge, but \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  estimates that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have sold more than $100 billion in reserve assets in the past two weeks alone. In July, China sold $50 billion of its foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 Some economists fear that China's policies won't prevent a recession. Industrial profits in July fell 2.9% from the level a year earlier, after slipping 2.6% in June. Wall Street now expects MSCI China stocks to report only 4% earnings growth this year -- disappointing for a country that in recent years has consistently boasted double-digit gains.\par \par \uc2 This hasn't scared some foreign investors. After stocks plunged last week, they scooped up insurance and bank shares, which had been battered. For example, blue chip \uc2 Ping An Insurance\uc2  (ticker: 601318.China) had lost more than 20% of its value this year, even though the value of its new business -- a reliable indicator of future revenue -- grew 43% in the first half. But Cui advises against buying, in part because he is "not comfortable with financial companies' balance sheets."\par \par \uc2 What China needs is more domestic consumption. But reforms that could make that happen appear unlikely. China's leadership seems in disarray, and dragging more social-security funds into the stock market is hardly reassuring.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 e-mail: shuli.ren@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 To subscribe to Barron's, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/subscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/subscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 29, 2015 00:07 ET (04:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150829eb8t0006s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
