\par\par \b \uc2 Fed, China Contributing to Higher Brazil Budget Deficit -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2015\par 16:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:49 EDT - Brazil's growing fiscal deficit has many locally grown causes, but it also has links to the potential Fed tightening and China's slowdown. Both those factors have made the Brazilian real lose about a third of its value in the past year. The falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost BRL57B this year as of July on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap contracts, by which the bank bets on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so markets can bet against it and hedge their BRL positions instead of selling them. For comparison. In the whole of 2014, the loss was just BRL17B. That's helping to push up Brazil's budget deficit to a worrisome 8.81% of GDP in July, from 3.8% a year earlier. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 10:48 EDT - \uc2 BB&T\uc2  thinks \uc2 Big Lots\uc2 ' (BIG) 2Q beat and raise "provides further evidence management's merchandising-driven turnaround continues to have a long runway." Changes including roll-out freezers and coolers (offering ice cream, eggs and milk) helped drive food sales and the sixth consecutive quarter of SSS growth. \uc2 BB&T\uc2  says the moves have made the business more predictable -- and likely to make the company more attractive to institutional investors. \uc2 Cantor Fitzgerald\uc2  thinks the launch of the e-commerce platform next year will "provide the necessary boost to drive SSS growth higher." BIG is up 15% at $48.27. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com; @patmsheridan)\par \par \uc2 10:42 EDT - A clinical trial of the antibody therapy, daratumumab, in multiple myeloma patients who were resistant to other treatments showed early signs of benefit. The study, published in the New England Journal of Medicine, addresses a critical topic: Even when cancer drugs work for a long time, resistance often emerges at some point, and there are not many other options. In the multiple myeloma trial, 72 patients who had received at least two prior therapies and whose disease was no longer responding to treatment, received the new drug. Of the 42 patients in the trial who got the higher tested dose, 36% responded to the treatment. Two-thirds of patients who benefited from the drug had no disease progression for at least 12 months. Researchers want to test the drug in a larger group in a later stage trial that might eventually lead to drug approval and new thinking about how to treat drug-resistant cancer. (amy.marcus@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:39 EDT - Despite plenty of criticism among political and business leaders, a Brazilian government plan to reintroduce the so-called CPMF -- a tax on financial transactions -- has a "good chance" of returning in the next two years given Brazil's struggles with cutting spending, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "We do think the return of the CPMF tax could lead to slower consumer loan demand, given that its impact would likely be mostly felt by individuals. However, a significant deceleration is unlikely, as consumer loan growth has already slowed to 10% yoy," it writes. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:29EDT - Since the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's Aug. 11 move to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken against the US dollar, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  estimates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with about $80B to offer stability. That would be about 20% of all spot-market activity. Trading in USD/CNY since that day has averaged $35B daily, \uc2 JPM\uc2  adds, which is nearly double normal daily volume for the pair. What does heightened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mean for \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves? It suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold more than $100B of reserves during the entire month of August -- a new record -- the investment bank says. With \uc2 PBOC\uc2  easing and eventual Fed tightening, the mix could fuel at least $600B in capital outflows from China in the next 12 months, \uc2 JPM\uc2  estimates. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 10:28 EDT - Let the drug pricing games begin. Following Thursday's \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for \uc2 Amgen\uc2 's (AMGN) Repatha, there are now two new anti-cholesterol drugs on the market, and pharmacy benefit managers are looking to play them off one another to win discounts. AMGN has discounted Repatha by about $500 annually compared with Regeneron (REGN) and \uc2 Sanofi\uc2 's (SNY) Praluent, but "the gross sticker price won't hold much meaning at the end of the day," says \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . The question is whether one side of the duopoly will seek to provide significant discounts in exchange for exclusive access to PBM customers, emulating \uc2 AbbVie\uc2 's (ABBV) approach in its hepatitis C competition with Gilead (GILD). PBM decisions from \uc2 Express Scripts\uc2  (ESRX) and others likely to come in September, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says. (joseph.walker@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:24 EDT - Danish drug maker \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2  (NVO) has an enviably strong market position in diabetes treatments, but strong drug pricing pressure and a coming wave of lower-cost generic insulins present big challenges to that market, Leerink says. The bank's recent survey of managed care companies show they are particularly targeting diabetes drugs as a way to manage costs, Leerink says. The same survey showed these payers expect lower-cost generics, known as "biosimilars," to take more than half the basal insulin market after launch. "While we believe these expectations are unrealistic, it may represent a near-term access headwind for novel insulins such as NVO's Tresiba," Leerink says. NVO down 1.4%. (jeanne.whalen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:57 EDT - Investors pulled the most money in one day from ETFs and mutual funds since 2007 on Tuesday, according to a note from BofA/Merrill. The massive $19B in redemptions marked the second largest day of outflows since daily equity flow data became available, the note adds. For the week ending Aug. 26, investors pulled $29.5B out of equity funds--the largest weekly outflows on record since data began being calculated in 2002. The big outflows speak to the intensity of investor fear over the past week when the stock market fell sharply, with the Dow industrials dropping nearly 11% in six trading sessions. Stocks have bounced back in the past two sessions, but the Dow industrials still remain more than 5% off last Monday's close. (corrie.driebusch@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:54 EDT - \uc2 Violin Memory\uc2  (VMEM) plunges to an all-time low as analysts question company's consistency. VMEM lost 19 cents a share in 2Q on sales of $15.3M, about $1M shy of Sterne Agee's estimate. Sterne Agee says VMEM's "continued cash burn due to depressed revenue levels," suggests another round of capital raising might be a reality. It sees the flash storage maker having a hard time meeting expectations for breakeven results by 1Q17. In a call last night CEO \uc2 Kevin DeNuccio\uc2  says company will "continue to look at whatever strategic options are available to us," though he's confident management can \b \highlight22\uc2 operationally\b0 \highlight\uc2  improve the business. VMEM dives 27% to $1.75, down 63% YTD. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com; @patmsheridan\par \par \uc2 09:54 EDT - \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  (BNS.T), which is sitting on a plump capital cushion, is open to doing more acquisitions in Latin America. Scotiabank posts a Basel III CET1 ratio of 10.4% for fiscal 3Q, among the highest of major lenders. "If we saw the right acquisition, we could go below the peer group average [on capital] and earn our way back. Or if it's something significant that we think is on strategy and very important for the bank going forward, we might access the capital markets," says CEO \uc2 Brian Porter\uc2 . Scotiabank considers Mexico, Peru, Colombia and Chile key growth markets. "As bank valuations around the world readjust, given what's gone on in markets and valuations in all of Pacific Alliance countries have come down ... we're poised and ready for any opportunities that come our way," he adds. (rita.trichur@wsj.com; @ritatrichur)\par \par \uc2 09:48 EDT - The year-long downturn in oil prices has offered precious little hope for investors gunning for higher oil prices -- until yesterday. The sharp price spike -- though reversed modestly today -- still has some rubbing it in. "Oil ultra bears got hit with a reality check," says Price Futures' \uc2 Phil Flynn\uc2 . "There is no doubt that the bear case of ever rising supply is a compelling one, especially if you put it in the context of a slowing global economy. Yet, it looks a lot less compelling if the slowdown talk is being overstated." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:42 EDT - Brazil's economy is mired in low confidence, high inflation and elevated interest rates, and that's likely to prompt an even bigger decline in output than expected given today's 2Q GDP report of 1.9% contraction that came with downward revisions of previous quarters. Neil Shearing of Capital Economics says the firm is lowering its growth forecast for the year, now estimating a 2.5% contraction from a 1% contraction previously expected. "Revisions to the back series mean that the contraction in GDP in the first quarter of this year was bigger than previously thought," Shearing says. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 28, 2015 10:49 ET (14:49 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150828eb8s0023w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
