\par\par \b \uc2 China's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  likely to ease policy again by end-December: Reuters poll\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sumanta Dey   \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2015\par 13:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Reuters) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is highly likely to ease monetary policy again by the end of this year, according to economists surveyed by Reuters, as it seeks to support a rapidly cooling economy and calm financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  cut interest rates and lowered the amount of reserves banks must hold for the second time in two months on Tuesday, acting amid pressure from a global stock market rout and massive outflows from its markets.\par \par \uc2 But with factory activity, exports and inflation slowing along with prolonged producer price deflation, economists polled predicted that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  would have to act again.\par \par \uc2 The median from a snap survey of around 20 economists showed there is a 80 percent chance of a further cut in the reserve requirement ratio (RRR) by end-December, while the chance for a reduction in the lending and deposit rates stood at 70 percent.\par \par \uc2 While prospects of further easing will calm investors who are jittery over the effects a China slowdown could have on global growth, some analysts fear Beijing's strategy might prove inadequate.\par \par \uc2 "The drip-feed of stimulus might not be sufficient to arrest aggressive bears, or significantly lift the economy in a demand-constrained world," \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  economists wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 "While China's latest easing measures are certainly welcome, we are worried that such reactive, ad hoc and uncoordinated (policy) might undermine conviction and efficacy."\par \par \uc2 The interest rate cuts followed a shock devaluation in the yuan earlier in August, a move policymakers said would aid financial reforms, although many saw it as a deliberate attempt to lower the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and help exporters.\par \par \uc2 For now, stock markets in China and elsewhere seem to have stabilized with Asian indices extending a global rally on Friday after data showed U.S. economic growth surpassed expectations in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 That could give some impetus to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates for the first time in a decade, although expectations it would do so in September have all but evaporated after the financial market volatility in the past week.\par \par \uc2 The poll also showed China's key lending rate will probably be cut to 4.35 percent by the end of the year, after which it is expected to stay steady, and the deposit rate is expected to come down by 25 basis points to 1.50 percent by the year-end.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to lower the RRR further, with the median consensus showing it will be cut to 17 percent from the current 18 percent by end-December and further lowered to 16 percent by the end of next year.\par \par \uc2 "There will be a need to inject liquidity in interbank market to neutralize the impact from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Flemming Jegbj\u230\'00\'E6rg Nielsen, analyst at \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Falling interest rates could increase capital outflows in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and drag the yuan lower, meaning more \uc2 PBOC\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as policymakers have pledged to protect the yuan from sharp falls.\par \par \uc2 While the increased liquidity from the reserve cuts will benefit big banks, demand for credit from China's vast population and businesses is tepid and remains a source of concern.\par \par \uc2 Asked what else the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  could do apart from cutting rates, some respondents said it could lend directly to select banks, expand its medium-term lending facility and take more steps to aid the real economy.\par \par \uc2 Economists also said China's housing market, a major factor in economic activity, is dangerously close to a steep correction and targeted measures from Beijing would be needed along with policy easing from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tackle the slowdown.\par \par \uc2 "We are expecting fiscal spending to be much stronger for the rest of the year after the significant disruption in the first half," said \uc2 Julian Evans Pritchard\uc2 , China economist at Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 "If Beijing wants to hit its spending target its going to have to step up spending."\par \par \uc2 (Polling by Beijing Newsroom and Shaloo Shrivastava and Khushboo Mittal in Bengaluru; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-28T11:03:12.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150828eb8s00bni\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw302\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4530\pichgoal600
