\par\par \b \uc2 China's "Black Monday" Wipes Out Global Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 829 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2015\par \uc2 M2 Presswire\par \uc2 MTPW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2015, M2 Communications. All rights reserved.   \par \par \uc2 In an effort to limit capital flight, the \uc2 People\u8217\'20\'19s Bank of China\uc2  announced it would pump an additional 150 billion yuan ($US 23.4 billion) into the financial markets and cut interest rates for the fifth time since November.At Woodbridge Research, we are dedicated to keeping investors informed, and this week has been monumental in terms of stocks as up to a trillion dollars was wiped from the global stock markets, a result of a massive selloff in China.\par \par \uc2 The rout in equities, commodities and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  began early Monday morning in Asia and extended throughout the globe. The day, quite fittingly dubbed \u8220\'20\'1CBlack Monday,\u8221\'20\'1D saw China\u8217\'20\'19s benchmark Shanghai Composite Index plunge 8.5% - its biggest drop since 2007 - on renewed fears that China\u8217\'20\'19s economic slowdown was intensifying.\par \par \uc2 The panic quickly spread to Europe, where shares slumped more than 5%, resulting in the worst daily closing performance since 2008. And, the US markets were equally volatile, with the \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  posting its biggest midday swing on record before closing on losses of nearly 600 points. The large-cap \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  declined nearly 80 points or 3.9%. Moreover, the \uc2 CBOE\uc2  VIX Volatility Index - a.k.a. the \u8220\'20\'1Cinvestor fear gauge\u8221\'20\'1D - surged 93% to 53 after the opening bell - its highest level since 2009.\par \par \uc2 Next, \u8220\'20\'1CBlack Monday\u8221\'20\'1D saw oil prices plumb more than six-year lows. US benchmark West Texas Intermediate (WTI) fell below $39 a barrel on the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2  - its lowest level since February 2009. International benchmark Brent crude settled at a six-year low on the ICE Futures exchange in London.\par \par \uc2 How did the events affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Well, the US dollar suffered heavy losses against global peers and posted its biggest single-day drop against the yen since 1998. The dollar index, a weighted average of the greenback against six global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , closed down 1.7%.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Chinese stocks plunged even further, rounding out the worst four-day performance for the Shanghai Composite since 1996. In an effort to limit capital flight, the \uc2 People\u8217\'20\'19s Bank of China\uc2  announced it would pump an additional 150 billion yuan ($US23.4 billion) into the financial markets and cut interest rates for the fifth time since November. The announcement helped lift European shares, but did little to boost the American averages, which sunk deeper into correction territory.\par \par \uc2 The dramatic fall in global markets over the past two days are an extension of the financial turmoil that has been brewing since China unexpectedly devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, on August 11. Since that day, more than $5 trillion has been wiped from global equities. China\u8217\'20\'19s abrupt decision to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in more than two decades came just three days after official data showed the country\u8217\'20\'19s exports plunged 8.3% in July.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about a protracted slowdown in the world\u8217\'20\'19s second-largest economy have been brewing for a while. China\u8217\'20\'19s gross domestic product - the value of all goods and services produced in the economy - expanded 7.4% in 2014, the slowest pace in 24 years and barely missing Beijing\u8217\'20\'19s official target of 7.5%. China\u8217\'20\'19s economy weakened again in the first quarter of 2015, rising at the slowest rate since the 2009 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts contend that this lower Chinese economic growth could further depress oil prices, which will continue to impact export-oriented nations like Canada, Australia and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  member-states. As a major source of global economic growth, a weaker China is likely to continue to weigh on investor confidence, resulting in a longer period of instability.\par \par \uc2 However, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the financial markets to curb intensifying capital flight and lift investor confidence. But, after spending more than $200 billion buying Chinese stock in less than two months, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has had limited success in boosting investor morale. With China\u8217\'20\'19s GDP growth unlikely to hit 7% this year, the latest bout of volatility won\u8217\'20\'19t likely be the last.\par \par \uc2 So, what does all of this mean for commercial real estate investors? That still remains unclear. Some analysts believe that Chinese investors may hasten their buying of United States assets, as it is a much more stable market. However, others wonder if the fallen value of the yuan may cause investors, particularly those interested in luxury real estate, to pump the breaks.\par \par \uc2 Distributed by TYLER BARNETT PUBLIC RELATIONS\par \par \uc2 Media Contact\par \par \uc2 Company Name: Woodbridge Structured Funding\par \par \uc2 Contact Person: Trish Mahon\par \par \uc2 Email: tmahon@woodbridgeinvestments.com\par \par \uc2 Phone: 866-865-7044\par \par \uc2 Address:14225 Ventura Blvd. Suite 100\par \par \uc2 City: Sherman Oaks\par \par \uc2 State: CA\par \par \uc2 Country: United States\par \par \uc2 Website: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.woodbridgeinvestments.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.woodbridgeinvestments.com}}}\par \par \uc2