\par\par \b \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Uses Unusual Tool to Tame Yuan Fall Expectations\b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2015\par 12:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI\u8212\'20\'14\uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used an unusual and complex financial tool Thursday to tame growing expectations for the yuan to fall, three people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for U.S. dollar-yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, causing their price to fall sharply, a movement that implies a stronger Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lower interest rates in the world's No. 2 economy in the future, said the people.\par \par \uc2 The move came after waves of sharp selloffs in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore markets, such as Hong Kong's, where the yuan trades freely, following Beijing's surprise nearly 2% yuan devaluation on Aug. 11.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to what each of the three people described as "massive" orders from a few commercial banks acting on the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's behalf, the so-called one-year dollar-yuan swap spread\u8212\'20\'14in rough terms, a measure of the implied future differential between Chinese and U.S. interest rates\u8212\'20\'14plunged to 1200 points from 1730 points Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore market, the spread dropped to 1950 points from 2310 points Tuesday, following the onshore move.\par \par \uc2 A drop in the spread for dollar-yuan swaps, which consist of a spot trade and an offsetting forward transaction, would also imply a weaker spot exchange rate at a predetermined future date.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives are typically used by investors seeking to hedge against exchange-rate and interest-rate fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chose a rarely used tool this time\u8212\'20\'14the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps\u8212\'20\'14to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it did so via a couple of midsize banks, instead of the usual big state lenders that serve as its agent banks," one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 PBOC\uc2  officials didn't immediately respond to written queries from The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 While the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has a firm grip over the onshore spot yuan market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value has tumbled in the free, offshore market since Aug. 11.\par \par \uc2 The yuan was trading at 6.4763 to the dollar in Hong Kong late on Thursday, compared with 6.51, the weakest closing level in years, on Tuesday. In the onshore market, one dollar was buying 6.4075 yuan, down slightly from 6.4105 Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "In a way, it's more effective for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage people's outlook of the yuan in the swap market due to the latter's forward-looking nature," said one of the people. "It's likely partly in response to aggressive yuan selloffs in the offshore market."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also communicates to investors that the authorities expect borrowing costs to decline in the future, the people said. The spread reflects expectations for the differential between interest rates in the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plagued by a slowing economy and a stock-market rout, Beijing has stepped up its credit-easing efforts by slashing interest rates and flooding its banking system with cash in recent months.\par \par \uc2 China has targeted year-over-year economic growth of about 7% for 2015, which already would be the slowest pace in a quarter-century. New data since July have called that target into question. In particular, a private-sector gauge of factory activity fell to a 77-month low in August.\par \par \uc2 This month's abrupt yuan devaluation was interpreted by investors as a sign that economic growth is slowing more than Beijing had anticipated.\par \par \uc2 Write to Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Write to Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 27, 2015 06:35 ET (10:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150827eb8r0014y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
