\par\par \b \uc2 Shanghai Up 5.3%: Did Beijing Prop Up The Stocks Again?! -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2015\par 14:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index closed 5.3% higher at 3083.59 today.\par \par \uc2 Most of the gain came in the last half an hour of trading. The benchmark index rose sharply from 1.8% gain to close 5.3% higher.\par \par \uc2 This trading pattern looks suspicious. Did Beijing move in to prop up the market again?\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg thinks so:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government resumed its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market on Thursday and has been cutting holdings of U.S. Treasuries this month to support the yuan, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Authorities want to stabilize equities before a Sept. 3 military parade celebrating the 70th anniversary of the World War II victory over Japan, said two of the people, who asked not to be identified because the move wasn't publicly announced. \uc2 Treasury\uc2  sales allow the government to raise dollars needed to bolster the yuan after a shock devaluation two weeks ago, according to different people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 If this was true, it would be very difficult for investors and market sentiment. On August 14, Beijing said the China Securities Finance Corp., the brokerages' \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which shot to fame for its bailout efforts, would no longer actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as long as there was no "systemic risk." The government also said China's National Social Security Funds and the insurance companies would no longer be required to "do their service to the country." The Shanghai Composite Index has dipped 1,000 points since.\par \par \uc2 The part about Beijing selling its foreign reserve to prop up the yuan is less surprising. Since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime change two weeks ago, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  let the yuan go with the market flow for only three days, and the onshore rate has stayed remarkably calm at around 6.40. The offshore yuan was trading at as low as 4.51 earlier this week and 6.48 recently. Beijing has been buying its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Overnight, the Deutsche X-Trackers Harvest CSI 300 China A-Shares Fund ( ASHR) rose 0.7%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 27, 2015 08:05 ET (12:05 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150827eb8r001f1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
