\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-China rate cut gives stocks, oil, dollar relief; volatility high\b0\par\par\uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2015\par 20:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with market commentary, European equities close)\par \par \uc2 * Market rebounds on cut in China rates, reserve requirements\par \par \uc2 * European shares close up 4.4 percent\par \par \uc2 * U.S. stocks trim gains, dollar rises, oil rebounds\par \par \uc2 By Sinead Carew\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Aug 25 (Reuters) - Markets rebounded on Tuesday with world stocks, oil prices and bond yields all rising after China cut interest rates and banks' reserve requirements in a bid to kick-start its wavering economic growth.\par \par \uc2 But while the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  rose as much as 2.9 percent during Tuesday's session, trading was volatile and its gains had receded to just a 1.3 percent in late afternoon trading.\par \par \uc2 The dollar turned around Tuesday to rise 1.2 percent against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the stimulus boost to China economy renewed focus on U.S. economic data and a potential \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  rate hike this year.\par \par \uc2 Global markets had been pummeled on Monday after Chinese shares fell almost 9 percent, prompting investor calls for remedial action from authorities that grew louder overnight after the Shanghai Composite Index slumped.\par \par \uc2 Monday's selloff was U.S. equities' steepest in four years, and put the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  and \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  composite indexes in correction territory, topping off its fifth day in a row of declines.\par \par \uc2 "Spinning a pot on a potter's wheel when it gets out of balance, it starts spinning a lot," said \uc2 Brian Battle\uc2 , director of trading at \uc2 Performance Trust Capital Partners\uc2  in Chicago. "This is all price discovery. We had a panic yesterday. As people get back in the market they're questioning what everything is worth. I don't think it's any one thing. What's most important is where its going to close. I think we'll have a really underwhelming close."\par \par \uc2 Economists said Tuesday's response - a 25 basis point cut in key rates and 50 bps off the reserve requirement rate for large commercial banks - sent a clear signal that Beijing, which has stepped in several times this year to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growth on track, was still willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But while investors showed relief, they were cautious about \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic outlook and how long it would take for its moves to stimulate growth, said Kim Forrest, senior equity research analyst at \uc2 Fort Pitt Capital Group\uc2  in Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are understanding that things aren't terrible but things aren't going to materially improve in the short term with the actions taken in China. It takes a long time to borrow money, to come up with plans, to build things," said Forrest.\par \par \uc2 At 2:16 p.m., the \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  industrial average was up 202.6 points, or 1.28 percent, to 16,073.95, the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  gained 22.58 points, or 1.19 percent, to 1,915.79 and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Composite added 88.20 points, or 1.95 percent, to 4,614.44.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBOE\uc2  Market Volatility Index, at 30.98, was still elevated, indicating lingering uncertainty, but it was far below the previous day's high of 53.3, which was the highest level since January 2009.\par \par \uc2 With the previous day's rush for safety reversed, U.S. Treasuries prices fell on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 MSCI's benchmark emerging stocks index was up 2.2 percent - on track for its biggest jump in just over two years after seven days of back-to-back falls.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European FTSEurofirst 300 index closed up 4.4 percent in its biggest one day gain in almost four years, recouping much of the previous day's loss when around 450 billion euros ($520 billion) was wiped off the FTSEurofirst 300's value.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil and metals markets also responded to Beijing's move as China is one of the world's biggest commodities consumers.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude futures settled up 2.8 percent at $39.31 per barrel, while Brent rose 1.4 percent to $43.27.\par \par \uc2 But global oversupply and worries over the severity of the slowdown in China kept oil prices near the 6-1/2-year lows they fell to on Monday, when the market slumped 6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Copper, often considered a proxy for global economic activity, rose 2.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 In China, where market volatility has been at its most extreme, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy move - coming after a shock devaluation of the yuan two weeks ago - drew a guarded reaction.\par \par \uc2 "This is a big-bang move ... Frankly (it) shows a bit of panic in my mind," said \uc2 Andrew Polk\uc2 , resident economist at the \uc2 Conference Board\uc2  in Beijing. (Additional reporting by Tanya Agrawal in Bengaluru, John Geddie, Lionel Laurent, Marc Jones in Europe and China Economics writing by John Stonestreet; editing by Anna Willard and Nick Zieminski) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-25T19:36:03.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150825eb8p00nb2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
