\par\par \b \uc2 HK Bourse: Announcement From \uc2 Carry Wealth Holdings Ltd\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 4,153 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2015\par 14:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Following is the related link: \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2015/0824/LTN201508241271.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2015/0824/LTN201508241271.pdf}}}\uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                       INTERIM RESULTS \par             FOR THE SIX MONTHS ENDED 30 JUNE 2015 \par The board of directors (the "Board") of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the "Company") \par presents the unaudited consolidated results of the Company and its subsidiaries (collectively \par the "Group") for the six month ended 30 June 2015. \par  \par MANAGEMENT DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS \par  \par Results and Business Review \par  \par For the six months ended 30 June 2015, the Group's revenues amounted to HK$111.7 million \par (2014: HK$78.6 million) and gross profit was HK$42.2 million (2014: HK$9.4 million). \par Profit attributable to equity holders was HK$14.3 million (2014: loss of HK$13.8 million). \par The basic and diluted earnings per share were 1.77 HK cents and 1.75 HK cents respectively \par (2014: basic and diluted loss per share were both 1.85 HK cents). \par  \par During the period under review, the US economy has continued its moderate growth. With \par the improvement in the job market, lower fuel prices and strengthening housing market, \par consumer sentiment has gradually revived. However, the world's second largest economy, \par Mainland China, was losing growth momentum. Economic data has indicated that factory \par activity and market consumption in the country were weakening. While in the Euro zone, \par consumer confidence was still weak even after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had launched \par quantitative easing to stimulate economic growth. Countries such as Greece, Spain and Italy \par still faced crippling levels of debt and high unemployment. Despite this unstable business \par environment, the Group's revenue from the garment manufacturing and trading business was \par maintained at HK$79.5 million (2014: HK$79.0 million) and the gross profit amounted to \par HK$9.9 million (2014: HK$9.8 million). \par  \par  \par                                                       1 \par  \par With a target to achieve GDP growth of 7%, Mainland China's government has implemented \par monetary policies including reduction of bank interest rates and reserve ratios to encourage \par funds to flow into the financial market so as to stimulate the real economy. With such a \par massive injection of liquidity further boosted by margin financing of stock trading, the Hong \par Kong \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index and particularly the Shanghai Composite Index skyrocketed during \par the review period. For the six months ended 30 June 2015, the Group's securities investment \par business recorded a revenue and gross profit of HK$32.3 million (2014: gross loss of HK$0.4 \par million). \par  \par Despite a general increase in factory costs, the selling expenses to the garment manufacturing \par and trading revenue ratio was maintained at 1.8 (2014: 1.8) and the administrative expenses \par increased slightly to HK$22.7 million (2014: HK$22.3 million). \par  \par Segment Analysis \par  \par a)   Garment manufacturing and trading segment \par  \par      In the US, both the job and consumer markets continued their improvement. Household \par      consumption and spending was also increasing. As a result, revenue from the US \par      customers increased by 18.2% to HK$29.7 million (2014: HK$25.2 million) and \par      accounted for 37.4% (2014: 31.8%) of the segment revenue. \par  \par      Affected by the slowest economic growth rate in Mainland China since 2009, market \par      demand and household spending dropped. However, with the concerted effort of the \par      Group's sales team, revenue from Mainland China customers remained at HK$39.4 \par      million (2014: HK$39.4 million) which accounted for 49.5% (2014: 49.8%) of the \par      segment revenue. Revenue from European customers was HK$7.8 million (2014: \par      HK$7.6 million) and accounted for 9.8% (2014: 9.6%) of the segment revenue. \par  \par      In summary, revenue from the garment manufacturing and trading segment was \par      HK$79.5 million (2014: HK$79.0 million) and recorded a loss of HK$2.3 million (2014: \par      HK$2.5 million). \par  \par b)   Securities investment segment \par  \par      In the period under review, triggered mainly by massive funds from Mainland China \par      investors, the Hong Kong stock market surged with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index reaching a \par      seven-year high of 28,442 points on 28 April 2015. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index closed at \par      26,250 points on 30 June 2015 (31 December 2014: 23,605 points). The Group's \par      securities investment revenue recorded a net gain of HK$32.3 million (2014: net loss of \par      HK$0.4 million) and the profit from this segment was HK$26.1 million (2014: loss of \par      HK$2.1 million). As at 30 June 2015, the Group held a portfolio of Hong Kong-listed \par  \par  \par  \par                                               2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Aug 2015 08:26 ET \par HK Bourse: Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par      equity securities with a fair value of HK$155.2 million (31 December 2014: 113.7 \par      million). \par  \par Liquidity and Financial Resources \par  \par Adhering to a conservative financial management methodology, the Group continued to \par maintain a healthy financial position. As at 30 June 2015, the Group's cash and bank \par deposits totalled HK$111.5 million (31 December 2014: HK$118.6 million). Working \par capital represented by net current assets amounted to HK$266.1 million (31 December 2014: \par HK$248.0 million). The Group's current ratio was 9.2 (31 December 2014: 8.1). \par  \par Capital Expenditure \par  \par For the period under review, the Group incurred a total capital expenditure of HK$0.4 million \par (2014: HK$0.1 million) for the additions of leasehold improvement and office equipment. \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Exposure \par  \par The Group's sales are principally transacted in US dollars and Renminbi. With a factory \par located in Mainland China and offices in Hong Kong and Mainland China, operating \par expenses of the Group are primarily denominated in Hong Kong dollars and Renminbi with \par some expenses also in US dollars. The Group is mainly exposed to US dollar exchange rate \par risk arising from sales transactions. \par  \par The Group will closely monitor fluctuations in the value of the US dollar and, if necessary, \par will enter into forward exchange contracts to reduce exchange fluctuation risks. \par  \par Credit Policy \par  \par Consistent with prevailing industry practice, the Group's business was transacted on an open \par account basis with its long-standing customers. The credit ratings of customers are constantly \par reviewed and their respective credit limits adjusted, as and when necessary. \par  \par Charges on Assets \par  \par As at 30 June 2015, the Group had no charges on assets. \par  \par Contingent Liabilities \par  \par As at 30 June 2015, the Group had no contingent liabilities. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                               3 \par  \par Human Resources and Remuneration Policies \par  \par The Group provides a harmonious working environment to employees whose commitment \par and expertise are critical to the long-term success of its business. The Group offers \par employees rewarding careers and provides them with a variety of training programmes aimed \par at enhancing their professionalism. It rewards employees according to prevailing market \par practices, individual experience and performance. To attract and retain high calibre \par employees, the Group also offers discretionary bonuses and share options to staff members \par based on performance of the individual as well as the Group. \par  \par As at 30 June 2015, the Group had a total of 940 (31 December 2014: 957) full-time \par employees in Mainland China and Hong Kong. \par  \par Outlook \par  \par At the start of second half of 2015, the volatile Mainland China stock markets and Greek \par rejection of the austerity measures imposed by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  had created instability \par which could adversely affect the continued global economic recovery. Looking ahead, \par growth in major economies could well follow divergent courses. \par  \par For the US market, the Group expects the economy there to recover gradually even if interest \par rates hikes are raised during 2015. With stronger economic fundamentals than before, the \par anticipated rise of interest rates should not significantly affect the momentum of the US \par recovery. Instead, the interest rate hikes would deliver a positive message that US economy is \par strong enough to wind down the unconventional monetary policy and return to normal. \par  \par In Mainland China, the central government had injected liquidity to the economy with an aim \par to meet the growth target set for 2015. While we have yet to see whether the looser monetary \par policy will stimulate real economic growth, money had flowed into the stock markets which \par subsequently experienced significant adjustments starting in mid-June 2015. Drastic \par administrative measures were immediately taken by the central government to avoid a further \par stock crash. Such measures are essential to avoid material set backs on structural reforms. \par Short term market disruption is unavoidable and it will take time to restore market confidence \par in China's stock market. \par  \par While in the Euro zone, the region has shown signs of slow recovery following the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's large scale quantitative easing. Though Greece represents less than 2% of the \par Euro zone GDP, markets are concerned the recent Greek debt crisis will unleash a chain \par reaction in which other heavily indebted countries such as Portugal, Italy and Spain may \par initiate similar policies and affect Euro zone unity. Indeed, mutual commitments and trust \par among Euro zone countries are vital for the region's economic recovery to persist. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                               4 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Aug 2015 08:26 ET \par HK Bourse: Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par As for the securities investment business, the stock market in Hong Kong had been adversely \par affected by Mainland China's stock markets. The aggressive administrative measures \par executed by China and a sea of Chinese companies halting trading of their shares have \par aroused the concern of international investors. There were signs of capital withdrawal from \par Hong Kong listed A-share funds immediately after Mainland \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  launched harsh and direct \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its stock markets. On the other hand, the expected US interest rates hike and \par any disruption of the unity of countries within the Euro zone could well trigger disturbances \par in stock markets. Though the current low valuation of the Hong Kong stock market and the \par expected opening of the Shenzhen-Hong Kong connect could be positive factors for \par investment opportunities, the Group anticipates pressure on its securities investment result \par during the second half of 2015 in view of the recent plunge in the Hong Kong stock market . \par  \par Looking ahead, we will continue to execute the established business strategies and seize \par development opportunities through focusing on our current business segments. At the same \par time we will carefully explore diversification into other potentially lucrative areas. As \par always, the Group pledges to seek and evaluate every opportunity and will strive to achieve \par long-term sustainable growth to maximize returns to its shareholders. \par  \par  \par  \par INTERIM DIVIDEND \par  \par The Board has resolved not to declare any interim dividend for the six months ended 30 June \par 2015 (2014: nil). \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                              5 \par  \par CONDENSED CONSOLIDATED INCOME STATEMENT \par  \par                                                             Six months ended 30 June \par                                                                                    2014 \par                                                                  2015 \par                                                                              (Unaudited) \par                                                           (Unaudited) \par                                                                                HK$'000 \par                                                              HK$'000 \par                                                    Note \par  \par  \par Revenues                                            3                             78,642 \par                   