hat affect the application of accounting policies and \par    the reported amounts of assets and liabilities, income and expense. Actual results may differ from \par    these estimates. \par  \par    In preparing these condensed consolidated interim financial information, the significant \par    judgements made by management in applying the Group's accounting policies and the key \par    sources of estimation uncertainty were the same as those that applied to the consolidated financial \par    statements for the year ended 31 December 2014, with the exception of changes in estimates that \par    are required in determining the provision for income taxes. \par  \par 2. Accounting policies \par  \par    Except as noted below, the accounting policies applied in the preparation of these condensed \par    consolidated interim financial information are consistent with those used in the preparation of the \par    Group's audited consolidated financial statements for the year ended 31 December 2014. \par  \par    The following new standards and amendments have been issued and effective for annual periods \par    beginning on January 2015 with no impact on the Group's results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial \par    positions: \par  \par       HKAS 19 (Amendment), 'Employee Benefits' \par       Annual Improvements Project, 'Annual Improvements 2010-2012 Cycle' \par       Annual Improvements Project, 'Annual Improvements 2011-2013 Cycle' \par  \par    There are no other standards or amendments that are effective for the first time for this interim \par    period that could be expected to have a material impact on this Group. \par  \par    Taxes on income in the interim periods are accrued using the tax rate that would be applicable to \par    expected total annual profit or loss. \par  \par 3. Segment information \par  \par    During the period, the Group is principally engaged in garment manufacturing and trading and \par    securities investment. \par  \par    Operating segments are reported in a manner consistent with the internal reporting provided to the \par    Board of Directors. \par  \par    The Board of Directors considers the business principally from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  nature, with two \par    segments identified: garment manufacturing and trading segment and securities investment segment. \par  \par    The Board of Directors assesses the performance of the operating segments based on a measure of \par    adjusted operating results. This measurement basis includes results of the operating segments \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                   10 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Aug 2015 08:27 ET \par HK Bourse: Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carry Wealth Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . -6-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     before corporate administrative expenses, finance income and tax, but excludes material gain or \par     loss which is capital in nature or non-recurring nature. \par  \par     Revenue recognised during the period is as follows: \par  \par                                                                          Six months ended 30 June \par                                                                                                 2014 \par                                                                                2015 \par                                                                                          (Unaudited) \par                                                                         (Unaudited) \par                                                                                             HK$'000 \par                                                                            HK$'000 \par  \par     Garment manufacturing and trading: \par      Sale of garment products                                                   