\par\par \b \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Inaction Expected to Blitz HK Stocks; Futures Plunge -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,242 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2015\par 03:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] Hong Kong equities are seen starting the week on the back foot after the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 declines to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the weekend, as some in the market had speculated. \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index futures are hammered alongside a sell-off in regional equity benchmarks, down 4.4% ahead of the market open. "No major action was taken by China over the weekend which means Asia will be left largely to its own devices this morning," says IG's Evan Lucas. The \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index fell 6.6% last week to 22,409.62, while H-share exchange-traded funds listed in the U.S. sunk, faring slightly better than plunging A-share counterparts. Some think the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will have to act soon: "Some policy easing moves, including a RRR [reserve requirement ratio] cut, look highly likely in China this week," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 dipped to its lowest intraday level in more than 18 months in early trading, echoing losses in global markets at the end of last week and amid further declines in commodity prices. "The ongoing sell off in commodities and concerns about growth in China have more people thinking in terms of global deflationary pressure," Ric Spooner, chief market analyst at CMC Markets, says. The \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 is now down 1.8% at 5121.1, having brushed a low of 5076.4, with resources stocks leading broad losses. The price of oil in the U.S. slipped below US$40/barrel Friday for the first time since 2009, while copper and other prices also declined. Santos (SO.AU) is down a further 5.9% after it last week launched a strategic review and said its CEO was stepping down, while \uc2 Woodside Petroleum\uc2  (WPL.AU) is 3.2% lower and Oil Search (OSH.AU) is 2.4% weaker. Among the miners, \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  (BHP.AU) and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) are both down 2.6%. The major banks are between 1% and 1.8% lower. \uc2 BlueScope Steel\uc2  (BSL.AU) is among the few stocks bucking the trend, up 14% after reporting a swing back to profit for the last fiscal year. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @RobbMStewart) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortescue Metals Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (FMG.AU) may need a cast-iron stomach Monday. The miner's share price has plunged deep into the red after it reported a net profit of US$316 million for the year through June, down sharply from a profit of US$2.74 billion the year earlier. While the result was weighed heavily by tumbling iron-ore prices, it was also well below the US$436 million median of six analysts' forecasts compiled by The Wall Street Journal. Fortescue is down 6.8% at A$1.785/share. Its shares are 35% lower on the start of this year. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @RhiannonHoyle) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD slumped to its lowest since Aug 12 as Australia's benchmark \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  200 stock index collapsed 2.4% early Monday. Investor panic has spread after U.S. stocks plummeted 3.2% Friday, sparked by fears of China's economy slowing further still - amid the possibility of its equity bubble bursting. The AUD/USD pair is on the verge of triggering a bearish technical signal that might weigh it toward a new 6-year low. If AUD/USD ends Monday below 0.7296 it will be inside the daily Bollinger downtrend channel, and hence more likely to fall past the mid-August low of 0.7216 - which was the lowest it has traded since April 2009. AUD/USD is now 0.7253 from its Friday close of 0.7319, representing a 0.9% fall for the Aussie versus the greenback. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0028 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese stocks are down due to persistent global slowdown concerns. The benchmark Nikkei Stock Average is down 2.3% at 18993.87 after hitting the lowest intraday level since April 1 at 18928.12 earlier. The safe-haven yen is trending higher, denting Japanese exporter stocks. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (7267.TO) is down 4.4% at Y3,786.0. \f28 \uc2 \f2 TDK Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (6762.TO) is 2.6% lower at Y7,420.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0026 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR climbed from Monday's open to reach a new 17-year peak of 4.2200. Malaysia's ringgit has depreciated 9.6% against the U.S. dollar so far in August - its biggest monthly move since 1998. Further weakness is likely, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves - which have been used to defend the ringgit - continue to fall. Data released Friday showed a drop to $94.5 billion from $96.7 billion two weeks ago, a reminder to the market that persistent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market to prop up the ringgit is unsustainable. Global risk aversion has set in after U.S. stocks plummeted 3.2% Friday, sparked by fears of China's economy slowing further still, amid the possibility of its equity bubble bursting. USD/MYR is now 4.2200 from its Friday close of 4.1730, which represents a 1.1% fall for the ringgit against the U.S. dollar. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0015 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW opened Monday higher, hitting a new 4-year peak of 1,200.0 as demand for safe haven assets accelerated amid fears of a deeper global stock market selloff. South Korea's main stock index is down 0.5%, after Friday's 3.2% fall in U.S. stocks. The growing sense of major risk aversion setting has sparked deleveraging in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - which is becoming contagious across asset classes. \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields slipped as investors piled into traditional safe haven assets such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds and gold. But there is speculation of \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepping in soon with fresh monetary policy easing measures, which could alleviate the stock market collapse and soothe global investors' nerves. USD/KRW is now 1,196.8 from its Friday close of 1,195.0. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0007 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD has climbed sharply Monday, implying a weaker Singapore dollar, as risk aversion escalates. The Singapore dollar is close to setting a new 5-year low against the greenback, which could happen if USD/SGD surpasses the Aug. 12 mark of 1.4164; the pair is currently at 1.4145. Friday's sharp fall in U.S. stocks, which ended 3.2% lower, has set off panic-buying of traditional safe haven assets such as the U.S. dollar and gold. The financial market rout which began with China's stock market cracking under short-selling pressure - sparked by weaker economic data - is likely to keep the U.S. dollar buoyant. But there is speculation of \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepping in soon with fresh monetary policy easing measures, which could alleviate the stock market collapse. USD/SGD is now 1.4145 from its Friday close of 1.4078. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 23, 2015 21:10 ET (01:10 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150824eb8o0006l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
