\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Stable Thursday Despite Shanghai's 3.4% Drop -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 241 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2015\par 13:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0731 GMT [Dow Jones] The 3.4% drop in the benchmark Shanghai Composite index Thursday hasn't had much effect on China's yuan. Often such a selloff is an indicator of an exit of offshore capital, and therefore of weakening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, the onshore USD/CNY has been stable near its last closing level of 6.3955 for most of the day, though the drop in USD/CNH to 6.4460 from 6.4608 implies a stronger offshore yuan versus Wednesday's close. The appreciation in value of the offshore yuan is a reflection of broad U.S. dollar weakness after the U.S. July FOMC minutes showed hesitation to raise rates for fear of an even stronger U.S. dollar. The stability of the onshore yuan may hint at market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities, despite the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  saying a week ago that it would meddle less in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value.\par \par \uc2  Some analysts suggest that another yuan devaluation could be on the cards, regardless of \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's claims that last week's devaluation was a one-off. USD/CNY is now 6.3961 from its Wednesday close of 6.3955. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor JSM\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 20, 2015 03:31 ET (07:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 20-08-15 1109GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020150820eb8k00015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
