\par\par \b \uc2 Fed Turns Wary Eye to China as Liftoff Looms\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kristen Scholer   \par \uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2015\par 08:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 A lot of eyes are on China these days. A newly-released document shows it's on the radar of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  too.\par \par \uc2 In minutes from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's July meeting released Wednesday afternoon, the Fed said "the possibility of adverse spillovers from slower economic growth in China raised some concerns."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cautioned a "material" slowdown in the world's second-largest economy could pose risks to the U.S. outlook. But it downplayed China's stock market plunge this summer, saying it seemed to have had a limited impact on Chinese growth so far...at least through the end of July.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the minutes are backward looking and cover \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sentiment at the end of last month -- before \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid slowing growth.\par \par \uc2 Still, in the minutes, the Fed said that the largest U.S. firms have relatively strong financial positions and low direct exposure to China (and Greece). Perhaps a reason why the effect of tumult abroad has been contained to date.\par \par \uc2 "The usual objections to early action [liftoff] were set out again, including the shortfall in GDP growth (though this will be changed by the upward revision to Q1 growth and the likely 3-1/2% rate seen in Q2), the absence of unambiguous evidence showing wages accelerating, and fears over the slowdown in China," said Ian Shepherdson, chief economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics.\par \par \uc2 "Against this backdrop, though, the hawks point out that a substantial cumulative tightening in the labor market has taken place, so the Fed cannot assume that leaving rates on hold is riskless." Mr. Shepherdson joins the bulk of economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal who expect a rate rise to come in September.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2  hinted it may be nearing liftoff in the minutes when it said...\par \par \uc2 Most judged that the conditions for policy firming had not yet been achieved, but they noted that conditions were approaching that point.\par \par \uc2 That echoes a change in the Fed's July statement that said it needed to see "some further improvement" versus just "further improvement" in the labor market before considering that part of the equation sufficient to justify a rate rise.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 19, 2015 15:15 ET (19:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 20-08-15 0615GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020150820eb8k0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
