\par\par \b \uc2 Why China's Yuan 'Reform' Merits Skepticism\b0\par\par\uc2 By Greg Ip   \par \uc2 944 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2015\par 03:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Both Japan and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used aggressive monetary stimulus in recent years to boost their economies and in the process send their exchange rates down sharply. China last week directly engineered a much more modest devaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, in what it said moved toward a more market-determined exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Despite longstanding calls for a market-oriented yuan, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  responded with skepticism that China had in fact delivered that. Some in Congress were downright hostile. Around the globe, accusations of "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" rang out.\par \par \uc2 China might rightly wonder if there's a double standard, but the double standard exists for a reason. China has embraced many market-friendly reforms in recent years, but boosting growth and jobs remains the government's paramount concern. China's trading partners are right to suspect that the real purpose of the so-called reform is competitive devaluation that boost exports and growth at the rest of the world's expense.\par \par \uc2 The economic case for a lower yuan is relatively straightforward. China's economy this year has slowed, and the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  has reacted as a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically would--by cutting interest rates. In Japan, the U.S. and the eurozone, cutting interest rates would naturally depress the exchange rate as investors moved their money in search of better returns abroad.\par \par \uc2 But Chinese authorities maintain strict controls on the cross-border flow of capital and over how far the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may fluctuate against the dollar. That frees China to set the exchange rate independent of monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 For most of its recent history, China used that freedom to keep the yuan pegged artificially low against the dollar so as to boost exports and suppress imports, an effective strategy to both speed up industrialization and create factory jobs for millions of rural workers.\par \par \uc2 But the yuan is clearly less undervalued today than it once was. With central banks in Japan and Europe still easing monetary policy and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  about to tighten, the dollar has shot up, dragging the yuan with it. The surplus on China's current account--the balance of all exports, imports and investment income--had shrunk from 10% of GDP in 2007 to 2% last year, smaller in dollar terms than Germany's.\par \par \uc2 Investors, who have been granted steadily more freedom to move big sums in and out of China, have reacted to lower yuan interest rates as markets suggest they should: by selling the yuan, pushing it to the bottom of its permitted trading range, and forcing the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars and buy yuan. A truly market-determined yuan would free the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  of such obligations and enable it to act like a normal \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with interest rates and exchange rates jointly determined by a single monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Yet the rest of the world should worry that is not the real aim of last week's reform and devaluation, and that many countries will end up the losers. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  studied the impact of a 10% yuan decline on 30 countries, and in 29, output fell. (China was the exception.) Japan and the eurozone would sustain a hit of around 0.5% to output, and China's Asian competitors far more.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth should not be a zero-sum game. When one country eases monetary policy, its trading partners should benefit from the resulting stimulus to domestic spending and imports. By contrast, China's economy seems geared to favor exports over imports, even with the headwinds of a higher yuan. Exports and export-related investment contributed about two percentage points to China's growth rate in each of the past three years, after subtracting imports that go into exports, according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . That's a slowdown from 2010, but still remarkable amid slower growth in its trading partners and a higher yuan.\par \par \uc2 China's current account surplus is likely to surpass Germany's this year thanks to cheaper commodity imports. In a world that is still awash in savings and short of demand, China remains its largest source of excess savings, and it's growing.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the devaluation came just weeks after the Chinese State Council announced a new push to boost exports, including via a more flexible yuan. Late last year it announced hundreds of rebates of value-added tax on exports and the volume of such rebates rose 12% in the first half of the year, far faster than exports themselves, notes Simon Evenett of Global Trade Alert, which tracks protectionist moves around the world.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  may be sincere when it says the devaluation is about liberalizing the exchange rate, not helping exporters. But the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's word matters far less in these matters than its counterparts in other countries. You may disagree with how Janet Yellen or \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  run U.S. and eurozone monetary policy, but you don't have to wonder if they're in charge. By contrast, \uc2 PBOC\uc2  decisions must pass muster with political overseers in the Communist Party who by and large do not share the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's liberal economic instincts.\par \par \uc2 When the government decided the stock market had fallen too far, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  found itself reluctantly pressed into service to support prices. When market pressures begin to push the yuan notably higher and exports lower, will the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  resist the pressure to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prevent it from happening? Until it does, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not expect the benefit of the doubt.\par \par \uc2 Write to Greg Ip at greg.ip@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 20-08-15 0143GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150820eb8k00027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
