\par\par \b \uc2 Market Discipline Remains Elusive in China\b0\par\par\uc2 By ESWAR PRASAD   \par \uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2015\par 08:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China has once again turned desperation into opportunity. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made the best of a difficult moment, tying a devaluation of the yuan with another step toward increasing the flexibility of the exchange rate. Who can argue with a move toward greater liberalization, even if the circumstances might not be ideal? But without broader institutional reforms, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economy will likely both stall.\par \par \uc2 Before last week's devaluation, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  apparently felt cornered, with its monetary-policy instruments failing to boost domestic demand and a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  riding high on the coattails of the strong U.S. dollar, thereby hurting Chinese exports. So the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  joined the parade of central banks, in advanced economies and emerging markets alike, using \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to protect export competitiveness and prop up domestic growth.\par \par \uc2 Such a foray into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars will raise howls from foreign governments and international institutions seeking to preserve order in global finance. But the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has deftly blunted foreign criticism by coupling its devaluation with steps to further tie the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value to market forces. More important, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has given itself greater freedom to focus monetary policy on domestic reform objectives rather than on maintaining an artificially stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  value relative to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 For all the troubles wracking the Chinese economy, policy makers are continuing-in their typical piecemeal, gradual manner-to pursue important reforms. The government is making slow but steady progress toward removing restrictions on capital inflows and outflows, liberalizing the interest rate on bank deposits, and now allowing for more exchange-rate flexibility. Just last week Beijing freed up the interest rate paid by commercial banks on certificates of deposit, another step toward interest-rate liberalization.\par \par \uc2 These reforms will benefit China's economy in the long run. They will also strengthen China's case for the yuan to be included in the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's basket of Special Drawing Rights, which would count as the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's official imprimatur that the yuan has attained reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  status.\par \par \uc2 Yet these reforms will have limited traction domestically and internationally unless China's government is willing to cede control and undertake broader efforts affecting more fundamental aspects of state control. A broader set of financial markets, including vibrant exchange of government and corporate bonds, is a prerequisite for effective resource allocation.\par \par \uc2 As long as the state acts as a safety net for state-owned enterprises and financial institutions, real market discipline will remain elusive. As recent stock-market volatility has demonstrated, market-oriented liberalization without the support of a strong institutional framework can yield greater volatility and more costs than benefits.\par \par \uc2 In the case of the boom-bust cycle that has whiplashed China's stock market, the government made matters worse by ricocheting between its desire to let the market discipline itself and its urge to maintain control and stability. Improvements in corporate governance, accounting standards and transparency of corporate audits-rather than more forceful government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -would make \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market more stable.\par \par \uc2 Concerning the yuan, it is important to recognize that the power of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in international finance comes not just from the size of the economy behind it, but also from the institutions that back it up. Independent courts, an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a political system with the ability to self-correct are all essential. Reforms to cultivate such institutions in China are necessary to allow other market reforms to achieve their objectives of high, balanced and stable growth.\par \par \uc2 Such long-term goals may seem far removed from the many challenges facing China's government right now. But as Chinese leaders proceed with financial and other economic reforms, the lack of strong and independent institutions will at best hamper their progress, and at worst heighten uncertainty and volatility across the economy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Prasad is a professor in the Dyson School at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 , a senior fellow at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  and former head of the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's China Division.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 19-08-15 0636GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150819eb8j0007h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
