\par\par \b \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Economist: Yuan Won't Depreciate Significantly -- Report\b0\par\par\uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2015\par 06:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--The yuan won't continue to depreciate significantly, the chief economist at \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted as saying on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Financial News, a newspaper backed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ma Jun described the adjustment to the yuan's parity rate last week as a "special measure" designed to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's links to the market.\par \par \uc2 He also said that the move would enhance the short-term flexibility of the parity rate mechanism and that would "sharply reduce the likelihood of a large adjustment" in the future.\par \par \uc2 "Some of the conclusions [on the impact of the parity rate move] are based on the premise that the yuan will depreciate significantly, and these assumptions are incorrect," he was quoted as saying.\par \par \uc2 He said some analysts incorrectly concluded that the move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would push global commodities prices lower, lead to competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations world-wide and even delay a widely expected increase in U.S. interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ma also repeated previous statements that Beijing had no intention of triggering a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war."\par \par \uc2 He also repeated remarks by senior officials of the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had basically withdrawn from "routine" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and would only step in under special circumstances where the market rate had moved away from fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be made to move the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in either direction, he added.\par \par \uc2 He also cited a number of medium-term fundamental factors that should support the yuan in the future, and these included expectations of 7% annual economic growth, several reductions in interest rates and a recovery in property prices.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised the market with a change in its parity rate mechanism, a daily reference rate for the yuan against the dollar. The yuan is allowed to move up or down 2% each day against the U.S. dollar from the parity rate, which now appears to be set more closely in line with the closing market rate of the previous trading day.\par \par \uc2 The change in the mechanism effectively resulted in a 2% devaluation that unsettled markets around the world. After sustaining further losses, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since shown signs of stabilizing.\par \par \uc2 Write to William Kazer at William.Kazer@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 18, 2015 00:38 ET (04:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150818eb8i000et\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
