\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan stabilises after historic weekly loss\b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2015\par 05:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG, Aug 17 (Reuters) - China's yuan held steady\par against the dollar on Monday on a firmer official midpoint fix,\par pointing to continued efforts by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to calm frayed\par nerves after last week's surprise devaluation sliced 3 percent\par off the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's value.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate \par at 6.3969 per dollar prior to market open, firmer than the\par previous fix 6.3975. \par     The spot market opened at 6.3941 per dollar and\par was changing hands at 6.3943 at midday, 25 pips away from the\par previous close and 0.04 percent away from the midpoint. It\par traded within a tight range between 6.3915 and 6.3967.\par     The spot rate is currently allowed to trade with a range 2\par percent above or below the official fixing on any given day.\par     Traders say the stablisation of the yuan was mainly\par market-driven, and they have not seen any signs that major\par state-owned banks are \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in spot rates during the\par session as happened last week.\par     "I think the yuan will hover around 6.40, up or down by no\par more than 150 pips, 300 pips at most," said a trader at a\par Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai.\par     The yuan posted its biggest weekly loss on record and\par touched four-year lows after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's surprise\par devaluation of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by nearly 2 percent last Tuesday. \par     The move rattled world financial markets on speculation that\par Beijing would continue to guide the yuan lower, stoking fears\par the devaluation would spark a global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war.\par     In an effort to calm volatile markets, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  said late\par last week it sees no reason for the yuan to fall further. \par     Traders say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to remain under downward\par pressure as the economy struggles, but do not believe Beijing\par will engineer a big devaluation or a series of devaluations to\par boost exports.        \par     "In the near term, the RMB could continue to be under\par pressure given weak fundamentals as well as the strength of the\par U.S. dollar, but the PBoC could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if needed," said Larry\par Hu, an analyst at Macquarie.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move to weaken the yuan last week could head off\par further similar "adjustments", and the yuan is likely to move in\par both directions as the economy stabilizes, Ma Jun, chief\par economist at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said on Sunday. \par     The offshore yuan was trading 0.62 percent away from the\par onshore spot at 6.4339 per dollar. \par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par (NDFs), considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.5475, or 2.30 percent away from the midpoint.\par     One-year NDFs are settled against the midpoint, not the spot\par rate, and now that the trading band has been widened to 2\par percent in either direction, corporates are much warier of using\par the NDF to hedge given the basis risk inherent in them.\par     This has resulted in a loss of market liquidity in recent\par years and has frequently proven an unreliable measure of market\par sentiment.\par \par \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item                         Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint                6.3969   6.3975    0.01%\par  Spot yuan                    6.3943   6.3918    -0.04%\par  Divergence from midpoint*    -0.04%             \par  Spot change ytd                                 -2.98%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation              29.44%\par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument                   Current   Difference\par                                         from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan        *  6.4339    -0.62%\par  Offshore non-deliverable     6.5475    -2.30%\par  forwards               **              \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par     GRAPHICS:\par     The yuan in trade-weighted terms \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/sed74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/sed74t}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     The spot market versus the trading band \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://reut.rs/1MhsqOi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://reut.rs/1MhsqOi}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \par  (Reporting by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michelle Chen\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Shanghai Newsroom; Editing by\par Shri Navaratnam)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-17T04:41:25.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150817eb8h003s8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngbli