\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Stabilization Cannot Shake HK Stocks From Its Funk -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2015\par 06:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] Hong Kong equities decline in the morning session as banking stocks and energy companies take a tumble. ?This even as the yuan stabilizes and Asia-Pacific stocks are broadly rise. "Yuan devaluation chatter might gain less traction as the market gets its head wrapped around the concept of a more market-determined exchange rate," says IG. "Chinese stocks remained rather insulated from the yuan decline, still supported on government measures," he says, but that's less apparent looking at the Hong Kong market today. H-shares decline, underperforming a 0.1% decrease in the Shanghai Composite, with the \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2 China Enterprises\uc2  Index off 1.7% at 10,868.29. The \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index falls 1% to 23,754.21 as stocks price in Friday's better-than-expected Hong Kong GDP numbers, with a gauge of Hong Kong-listed energy shares down 2% as west Texas intermediate crude sets a fresh six-year low. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] The pace of Thailand's economic recovery in the latest quarter is a sign that the economy will continue to disappoint in the near term, say economists. While the country's 2.8% on-year growth in the second quarter was in line with consensus estimates, the seasonally-adjusted on-quarter growth of 0.4% still shows sluggishness in the Thai economy, says Krystal Tan of Capital Economics. "The recent decline in capacity utilization bodes poorly for private investment growth. Exporters will also continue to struggle amid waning competitiveness and lackluster global demand," she says. Meanwhile, ANZ notes that the sluggish household consumption and merchandise exports more than offset the improvement in tourism and government spending. "The land of smiles has turned to the land of frowns. Indeed, these are deep frowns that are unlikely to cheer anytime soon," the bank says. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @nopbkk) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0419 GMT [Dow Jones] China's devaluation of its yuan hasn't tipped off the latest round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wars in Asia because exports are driven more by overseas demand than by advantageous \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates, says Paul Gruenwald, chief Asia-Pacific economist for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "If you look at the data and what drives exports from this part of the world it's really more foreign demand than the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he says. Analysts expect rising demand from the U.S. and Europe to boost supply chains across the region, so Asian countries have less incentive to compete by weakening their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he says. What's more, weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have done little to boost the region's exports so far this year--Japanese exports have continued to struggle despite a weaker yen, for example. Still, some maintain that China's weaker yuan will "lead to a significant increase in renminbi volatility and depreciation of other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Nomura says. (Dominique.fong@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @dominiquefong)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sets its benchmark exchange rate at 6.3969 yuan per U.S. dollar Monday, stronger than the previous market close of 6.4465 but about flat from its last fixing of 6.3975. That seems to indicate China will not let its yuan weaken further than a certain threshold, judged by some market observers to be about 6.45 USD/CNY. "If we see too much depreciation in the rate, that's going to start the debate all over again," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief Asia-Pacific economist Paul Gruenwald. He thinks China will not let the yuan fall past 6.45. "We will have to see if authorities act on the line in the sand." (Dominique.fong@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0352 GMT [Dow Jones] A weaker yuan will likely do more harm than good for South Korea, say Seoul-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eugene Investment & Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists. The devalued Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will raise the unit prices of South Korean goods, leading China to cut down on imports. At the same time, increased demand for Chinese exports borne of a weaker yuan could increase demand for South Korea's intermediary goods, Eugene says in a research report. "Both positive and negative aspects are mixed here...but concerns are growing high that the effect of contracting [Korean] exports [to China] will be stronger than the effect of boosting exports," Eugene notes. A weaker yuan will weigh on the Korean makers of smartphones, steel, machinery, ships and cars because Chinese rivals are fast catching up in those sectors, it says. One reprieve for Korean exporters would be if the won depreciates against the U.S. dollar in line with the yuan, it adds. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0347 GMT [Dow Jones] The British pound, Singapore dollar cross rate could head toward 2.22 in the short term if the current trend continues, UOB said. "The strong rebound last Friday could be the start of the next leg higher towards our target at 2.2200. However, the recent high near 2.2060 is clearly a strong resistance and this level has to break before a sustained rally can be expected," the bank wrote in a note. It said it has moved its stop-loss level higher to 2.18 from 2.1670 previously. The GBP/SGD was trading slightly stronger at 2.2009 recently. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0343 GMT [Dow Jones] The Singapore dollar will likely continue to trend weaker in the short term, largely due to expectations that Beijing's move to allow the yuan to depreciate signals longer-term economic slowdown in China, says Oanda senior eFX trader Stephen Innes. "Traders may have felt a bit more comfortable shorting the Singapore dollar as opposed to other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the Singapore dollar offers far more liquidity than its local counterparts. We may see some increased positioning from that perspective going forward," he says. Innes argues that soft export numbers reported early Monday could provide additional incentive for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  to ease policy later this year. The Singapore dollar trades 0.1% weaker at S$1.4069 against the U.S. dollar late Monday morning. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's export growth is expected to cool further after averaging 2.7% on year in the first seven months of 2015, OCBC said. The bank cited a higher base of comparison from the second half of last year and reiterated its 2015 non-oil domestic exports growth forecast at between 0% and 2.0%. "Global demand also likely took a step back in July, given that only three of the top 10 non-oil domestic exports markets grew in July," OCBC said, adding that shipments to China, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the U.S. and Indonesia all reverted to contraction in July. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0339 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD has retreated to the base of its Bollinger uptrend channel as the U.S. dollar strengthens ahead of Wednesday's release of the Fed's latest FOMC meeting minutes. If EUR/USD ends Monday below 1.1083 the bullish technical bias will be lost and the pair could slide toward 1.0923 where the downtrend channel entrance awaits. The dollar has been firming up since Friday on upbeat U.S. retail sales and industrial data--which make a stronger case for the Fed to raise interest rates at its September monetary-policy meeting. China's stabilizing yuan is another factor that has brought U.S. interest-rate policy back into focus. \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday the yuan had weakened enough, and backed up that rhetoric with two straight stable daily yuan benchmark fixings. This has ended speculation that deeper yuan devaluation could derail the Fed's plan to raise interest rates. EUR/USD is now at 1.1094 from Friday's close of 1.1106. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KBC \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0339 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  exits its short NZD/AUD position from 0.9158, at 0.8873, for a 3.2% profit after lowering its AUD/USD forecasts. "We no longer see NZD/AUD pushing materially below 0.88 in 2015, and see the cyclical trough at just 0.87 in mid-2016," it says. While there are promising signs of NZD/AUD breaking through support at 0.8840, we are wary of holding this position ahead of Tuesday night's GlobalDairyTrade auction, it adds. It notes that the risks are skewed toward some stabilisation in dairy prices on the GlobalDairyTrade platform, or perhaps even a rise in prices. "This would likely prove NZD positive, especially for a market that remains appreciably short." NZD/AUD trading at 0.8867. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore faces 3 primary challenges to its exports, \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says after the island nation reports a 0.8% on-year fall in shipments of locally made goods. First, a deep trade recession in the region amid a depressed global economic backdrop that remains tepid and uneven. Second, regional perceptions that China may be pursuing a 'beggar thy neighbor' policy and focusing on reducing imports. Thirdly, electronics supply chains are evolving in a way that firms are shifting away from Singapore, ANZ says. Exports "momentum may not pick up strongly from here," ANZ says. The bank, however, notes that the July reading wasn't as bad as analysts had expected -- the median estimate in a poll of five analysts by The Wall Street Journal had predicted a 2.5% on-year contraction. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17 Aug 2015 00:29 ET \par Yuan Stabilization Cannot Shake HK Stocks From -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] Yuan devaluation should tighten the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's liquidity in the short term, as corporates buy USD/CNY to hedge \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  positions and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market by selling USD/CNY. ANZ said in a research note that China's external debt denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stood at $868 billion at the end of the first quarter, "as most of the debts are unhedged, the debtors should be quite eager to cover the positions." Foreign-invested corporates should also increase USD/CNY buying to hedge the exchange-rate risk for their retained earnings, ANZ said. But a sharp depreciation of the yuan against the U.S. dollar isn't in line with China's stance, and ANZ expects the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  to stabilize the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by selling USD/CNY, which should lead to yuan-liquidity drain and higher money-market rates in the short term. Higher money-market rates should drive China's domestic bond yields higher, particularly in the front end, ANZ said. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 17, 2015 00:29 ET (04:29 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150817eb8h00094\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
