\par\par \b \uc2 Volatility Reveals Beijing's Market Anxiety\b0\par\par\uc2 1,011 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2015\par 04:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Mark Magnier And James T. Areddy \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BEIJING-- Turmoil in China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stocks has highlighted the anxiety the Communist Party feels over the power and dynamism that markets represent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Even as market-opening policies have allowed hundreds of millions of Chinese to break out of poverty to build the world's second-largest economy, the party's ambivalence can be seen across the economic landscape. The leadership keeps fences around the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , restricts capital flows and operates a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that lacks independence.\par \par \uc2 Many Chinese economic planners have a solid understanding of markets. Still, there remains a "deep-seated narrative of the evils of capitalism, even though they embrace huge parts of it," said Kerry Brown, director of the \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2 's China Studies Center.\par \par \uc2 The tug of war has left China dragging its heels on financial overhauls necessary to continue improving living standards at a pace Chinese people have become used to.\par \par \uc2 "The government knows it has to implement a more efficient system because it no longer has double-digit growth that can buy people's loyalty," said Mr. Brown. "It's an epic clash of systems."\par \par \uc2 The stock market is a basic building block of any financial system, including China's, but no Chinese president has publicly embraced share ownership. "Where does the stock market's money come from?" asked Jiang Zemin a month before the Shanghai exchange's 1990 opening, according to an account in a biography of the former president by American banker Robert Lawrence Kuhn.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  pledged to let markets take a "decisive role" in the economy, and when stocks rose it was called his bull market in state media. But as the Shanghai Composite started falling sharply in mid-June, regulators under him quickly halted initial public offerings, leaned on state institutions to buy shares and blocked large shareholders from selling. On Friday, China's stock regulator said government entities would hold shares for years as needed to stabilize the market.\par \par \uc2 "They're happy to let markets go in the direction they want, namely up," said Alexander Wolf, economist with \uc2 Standard Life Investments\uc2 . "It's like a car they don't allow to go in reverse."\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the yuan lower, a move aimed at more market-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, then quickly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a too-rapid fall. In a rare news conference the next day, the PBOC's No. 2 official, \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2 , said the bank had stopped "regularly" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control the yuan's value, but that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during "external shocks."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry\uc2  and Communist Party didn't respond to requests to comment.\par \par \uc2 Western countries also interfere in markets at times, but tend to do so during major meltdowns, as with the 2008 financial crisis, rather than when they dislike the market's direction.\par \par \uc2 "Every aspect of China has been controlled by the government, but it's more and more affected by market prices and they're not used to the new dynamic," said Li Xi, professor at the \uc2 Hong Kong University of Science and Technology\uc2 . "If people protest in Tiananmen Square or petition with their complaints, they can round them up, jail them, send them home. But markets are different. They can't catch someone who's 'against them.' "\par \par \uc2 That hasn't stopped leaders from trying. A particular focus during the stock-market rout has been short sellers, who bet on a decline in a stock or an index. On July 9, China sent police to the securities regulator seeking evidence of "malicious" short selling, a move Mr. Li likened to "using a machine gun to kill a rat."\par \par \uc2 Much of a communiqu\u233\'00\'E9 issued last week when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  introduced its devaluation of the yuan focused on fighting terrorists, tax evaders and money laundering--hardly a welcoming tone, said Alicia Garc\u237\'00\'EDa Herrero, economist with investment bank \uc2 Natixis\uc2 , part of France's \uc2 Groupe BPCE\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ideally, stock markets funnel capital to the most promising parts of the economy and deny cash to low-return players. But in China, that threatens powerful state interests, so Beijing has ensured that the equity markets empower the party machine, some analysts say. According to data provider Wind Information Co., 34% of stock market capitalization remains in the hands of controlling shareholders, usually government entities.\par \par \uc2 China's wary approach to markets extends to an array of practices, including behind-the-scenes maneuvering often through state companies and banks. In May, even as it pushed ahead publicly to liberalize interest rates, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  quietly asked commercial banks to keep deposit rates lower than the official rate in a bid to assist borrowers.\par \par \uc2 One likely reason for \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's early \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market is Beijing's extreme sensitivity to social unrest in a system where discontent is often repressed.\par \par \uc2 "There's fear in the government, which is most fearful of the people," said the \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2 's Mr. Brown.\par \par \uc2 When markets tank, leaders have little reassurance to offer the common investor. At the height of the stock-market crisis in June, Mr. Xi pressed on with a scheduled trip to Russia. During the administration of his predecessor, \uc2 Hu Jintao\uc2 , the stock market jumped by a factor of six in a bull run until October 2007, then dropped more than 70% over the following year. But the state leader isn't known to have voiced support for share ownership, or even talked publicly about it at all, according to Mr. Brown.\par \par \uc2 "The stock market is something the leaders try to avoid," says \uc2 David Li\uc2 , dean of Schwarzman Scholars at \uc2 Tsinghua University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But China's bid to move from smokestack industries and debt-fueled infrastructure to an economy built on consumption and high-tech entrepreneurship will require the government to stop hovering, even if that means allowing some disconcerting swings, many economists say.\par \par \uc2 In a recent report on China, \uc2 Citibank\uc2  said: "No volatility, no market."\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 16, 2015 22:24 ET (02:24 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150817eb8h0004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
