\par\par \b \uc2 China yuan to move both ways, more 'adjustments' unlikely:  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  economist\b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2015\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Reuters) - China's move to weaken the yuan last week could head off further similar "adjustments", and the yuan is likely to move in both directions as the economy stabilizes, Ma Jun, chief economist at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 shocked global markets by devaluing the yuan <CNY=CFXS> by nearly 2 percent on Aug. 11. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  called it a free-market reform but some saw it as the start of a long-term yuan depreciation to spur exports.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's drop last week and its increased flexibility could help "sharply reduce the possibility" of similar adjustments in future, Ma said.\par \par \uc2 In the near term, it is more likely there will be "two way volatility," or appreciation and depreciation of the yuan, Ma said in a question-and-answer statement sent by email.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would move only in "exceptional circumstances" to iron out "excessive volatility" in the exchange rate, Ma said.\par \par \uc2 Ma played down market fears that a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" could be triggered by China's devaluation, which dragged some other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multi-year lows.\par \par \uc2 "China has no intention or need to participate in a '\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war'," Ma said in the statement.\par \par \uc2 "There is no need to worry" that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the yuan as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy stabilizes, Ma said.\par \par \uc2 "In the future, even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, it may be in either direction," he added.\par \par \uc2 Ma also said that he expected the economy to grow around 7 percent this year - in line with the government's target.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Matthew Miller; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-16T10:46:20.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150816eb8g0099d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
