\par\par \b \uc2 New Zealand/Australia Morning Call-Global markets\b0\par\par\uc2 910 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2015\par 21:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -----------------------(07:19 / 1919 GMT)-----------------------\par  Stock Markets                                                  \par S&P/ASX 200    5,356.54  -31.33  NZSX 50        5,696.45  -41.24\par DJIA          17,477.40  +69.15  Nikkei        20,519.45  -76.10\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NASDAQ\f28 \uc2 \f2          5,048.24  +14.68  FTSE           6,550.74  -17.59\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P 500\f28 \uc2 \f2         2,091.54   +8.15  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2      23,991.03  -27.77\par SPI 200 Fut    5,300.00   +9.00  TRJCRB Index     197.97   +0.14\par \par  Bonds                                                          \par AU 10 YR Bond     2.793  +0.000  US 10 YR Bond     2.201  +0.000\par NZ 10 YR Bond     3.355  +0.000  US 30 YR Bond     2.845  +0.000\par \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (Prev at 7pm NZST)                                  \par AUD US$          0.7373  0.7384  NZD US$          0.6528  0.6545\par EUR US$          1.1109  1.1163  Yen US$          124.30  124.31\par \par  Commodities                                                    \par Gold (Lon)      1118.25          Silver (Lon)      15.21        \par Gold (NY)       1113.70          Light Crude       42.50        \par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par - Overnight market action with latest New York figures.\par \par     EQUITIES\par     NEW YORK - U.S. stocks ended a volatile week higher on\par Friday after upbeat U.S. economic data and as euro zone finance\par ministers agreed to launch a third bailout program for Greece.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  industrial average rose 69.15 points,\par or 0.4 percent, to 17,477.4, while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P 500\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained\par 8.15 points, or 0.39 percent, to 2,091.54.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2  Composite, which swung 167 points from\par its low this week to its high, added 14.68 points, or 0.29\par percent, to 5,048.24.\par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     LONDON - Britain's top equity index edged lower on Friday,\par with a decline for commodities stocks, such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP\f28 \uc2 \f2  and\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Dutch Shell\f28 \uc2 \f2 , overshadowing gains in travel group\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 TUI\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     The blue-chip FTSE 100 index closed down 0.3 percent\par at 6,550.74 points\par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     TOKYO - Japan's Nikkei share average edged down on Friday\par amid profit-taking and falling oil prices that generated selling\par in shares tied to commodities.\par     The Nikkei average fell 0.4 percent to 20,519.45 for\par a weekly loss of 1 percent. The broader Topix lost 0.2\par percent to close at 1,664.46, ending the week down by 0.9\par percent.\par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par     NEW YORK - The euro held near its session lows against the\par dollar on Friday after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  confirmed a deal\par to lend cash-strapped Greece up to 86 billion euros over three\par years following talks in Brussels.\par     In late U.S. trading, the euro was down 0.4 percent\par at $1.1103, slightly above its session low of $1.1099, while it\par was down 0.5 percent at 138.00 yen, hovering near its\par session low of 137.94 yen.\par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     TREASURIES \par     NEW YORK - Short-term U.S. Treasuries yields rose on Friday,\par helped by a third straight monthly gain in U.S. producer prices\par that brightened odds for a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  interest rate hike as\par soon as September.\par     Five-year Treasuries, which are one of the\par shorter maturities most affected by Fed rates policy shifts,\par were off 2/32 and yielding 1.5871 percent. The long bond\par , in contrast, was up 13/32 in price and yielding\par 2.8361 percent.\par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     COMMODITIES \par     GOLD\par     NEW YORK/LONDON - Gold turned slightly lower on Friday as\par the dollar shifted higher on encouraging U.S. data and as\par investors weighed the impact of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on\par the timing for the first U.S. interest rate increase in nearly a\par decade.\par     Spot gold was down 0.2 percent at $1,112.36 an ounce\par by 1805 GMT after hitting $1,126.31 on Thursday, its highest\par since July 20.\par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     BASE METALS\par     LONDON - Copper slipped on Friday in response to a stronger\par dollar and weak demand, even though there was relief that\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appeared to have stopped guiding the yuan\par lower, returning some buying power to metal importers.\par     Three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange \par ended down 0.3 percent at $5,170 a tonne. The metal has had a\par volatile week, hitting a six-year trough of $5,062 a tonne in\par the wake of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves.\par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - - \par     OIL\par     NEW YORK - U.S. crude oil edged higher after falling to a\par fresh 6-1/2-year low on Friday, posting a seventh weekly loss\par amid concerns over global oversupply, while Brent futures\par slipped as the front-month September contract approached\par expiration.\par     U.S. September crude settled at $42.50 a barrel, up\par 27 cents, and continued to seesaw in post-settlement trade. It\par reached $42.96 after falling to $41.35, the lowest front-month\par price since March 2009, and finished off more than 3 percent for\par the week.\par     Expiring Brent September crude fell 19 cents to\par settle at $49.03, but gained nearly 1 percent for the week,\par snapping a string of six weekly losses. Brent prices are well\par above the 2015 low of $45.19 from January, despite its recent\par slide. Brent October crude also fell.\par     For a full report, double click on \par     - - - -\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-16T20:24:36.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150816eb8g00h7i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
