\par\par \b \uc2 PRECIOUS-Gold turns lower as dollar rises, snaps 7-week losing streak\b0\par\par\uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2015\par 20:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Gold ended 5-day rally on Thursday, longest since May\par \par \uc2 * Gold on track to close week up for first time in 8 weeks\par \par \uc2 * China's gold holdings up 19 tonnes in July (Rewrites paragraph 1, updates prices; adds comment, second byline, NEW YORK dateline)\par \par \uc2 By Marcy Nicholson and Clara Denina\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK/LONDON, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Gold turned slightly lower on Friday as the dollar shifted higher on encouraging U.S. data and as investors weighed the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the timing for the first U.S. interest rate increase in nearly a decade.\par \par \uc2 U.S. industrial output advanced at its strongest pace in eight months in July as auto production surged, another bullish sign for third-quarter economic growth that boosted the prospects of a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest rate hike next month.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was down 0.2 percent at $1,112.36 an ounce by 2:05 p.m. EDT (1805 GMT) after hitting $1,126.31 on Thursday, its highest since July 20.\par \par \uc2 U.S. gold for December delivery settled down 0.3 percent at $1,112.70 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 The precious metals complex moved in the opposite direction of U.S. crude oil, which turned higher after reaching a 6-1/2-year low.\par \par \uc2 "I think there were allocation shifts in the market today," said Phillip Streible, senior market strategist at RJO Futures in Chicago, explaining that it appeared some investors re-allocated their money to oil from precious metals.\par \par \uc2 Spot silver saw deeper losses of 1.7 percent to a session low of $15.15 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Despite the day's turn lower, gold bullion was set to end a seven-week losing streak after China's yuan devaluation increased uncertainty over the global economy, and pushed investors into assets perceived as safer such as gold.\par \par \uc2 "The market was caught by surprise by the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and there was some safe-haven buying but physical demand is still not strong and only a test of the $1,110 could change that," MKS SA head of trading Afshin Nabavi said.\par \par \uc2 Volatile markets were soothed as the yuan steadied after \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there was no reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall further given the country's strong economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 The precious metal has gained nearly 2 percent in the week so far, after a seven-week slide that was its longest retreat since 1999.\par \par \uc2 China's gold reserves rose to 53.93 million ounces by the end of July, up from 53.32 million at end-June, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. The data was released following a June adjustment that was the first in more than six years.\par \par \uc2 China's gold demand this year is expected to at least hold steady with last year at just under 1,000 tonnes, the \uc2 World Gold Council\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Spot palladium fell 0.3 percent to $615.50 an ounce after touching a two-week high on Thursday. Platinum was down 0.4 percent at $987.74 an ounce. (Additional reporting by Manolo Serapio Jr in Manila; Editing by William Hardy and Meredith Mazzilli) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-14T19:14:38.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150814eb8e00aes\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
