\par\par \b \uc2 Soothed by c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 ,  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  market takes Friday snooze\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pete Sweeney   \par \uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2015\par 12:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan flatlined on Friday, with the exchange rate barely budging and volumes low, as traders said a combination of soothing messages from regulators and buy orders from state banks kept the market in check.\par \par \uc2 The spot market opened at 6.3990 per dollar and changed hands at 6.3918 at market close, only 72 pips from the previous close and 0.09 percent away from the midpoint.\par \par \uc2 For the entire day, the yuan was in a tight range between 6.4050 and 6.3990.\par \par \uc2 "After a few days of volatility, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to want the yuan to show some stability for now, possibly also next week," said a trader at a major European bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted a record loss of around 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 shocked global markets by guiding its daily midpoint setting down nearly 2 percent, the sharpest such adjustment in China's modern \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The move, explained as a re-pegging of the midpoint fixing closer to the market rate, led the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall around 3 percent in massive volumes. Official data showed spot volume of nearly $57 billion, the biggest day in Reuters records kept since May 2013.\par \par \uc2 Volumes have come down sharply and the domestic market has stabilised after the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said on Thursday there was no reason for the yuan to fall further given China's strong economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Volatility vanished from the spot market on Friday. Prior to Tuesday - when the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said it aimed to introduce more two-way trade into the market - the exchange rate had for months also moved within a narrow 0.3 percent range, which traders blamed on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by state-owned banks to prop up the yuan.\par \par \uc2 The cost of that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now appears to be quite high, which may explain the sudden depreciation.\par \par \uc2 BIG JULY \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  SALES\par \par \uc2 On Friday afternoon, government data showed \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commercial banks together sold $38.9 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July, the biggest sales on record.\par \par \uc2 The data "showed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  together with major state banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in July. In coming months, if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  eases, the data will return to normal," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 The drastic nature of Tuesday's adjustment, which came without clear warning, is still reverberating in global markets, as investors try to assess the weaker yuan's implications for Chinese consumption.\par \par \uc2 Some warned of a looming \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, others that China was abandoning its commitment to economic restructuring, but others suggested investors may have overreacted.\par \par \uc2 "I continue to believe that the actions and words of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  signal that they have taken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a new starting point, and this is likely to revolve around a trade-weighted regime," said \uc2 Andy Rothman\uc2 , investment strategist at Matthews Asia.\par \par \uc2 "They will continue to manipulate the exchange rate, but with a less obsessive focus on the dollar."\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan was trading at a 0.76 percent discount from the onshore spot at 6.4405 per dollar, indicating offshore investors expect further depreciation.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Lu Jianxin; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-14T11:12:27.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150814eb8e00adp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
