\par\par \b \uc2 Morning MoneyBeat: Amid China Woes, Rate-Sensitive Stocks Still Shine\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kristen Scholer   \par \uc2 1,089 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2015\par 12:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Morning MoneyBeat is the Journal's pre-market primer packed with market updates, insights and must-read news links. To receive this morning newsletter via email, click here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/MoneyBeatUSSignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/MoneyBeatUSSignup}}}\par \par \uc2 MARKET SNAP: At 6.20 a.m. ET, \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  futures down 0.25%. \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yield down at 2.17%. Nymex down 0.52% at $42.00; gold up 0.26% at $1118.4. In Europe, FTSE 100 down 0.10%, DAX down 0.45% and CAC 40 down 0.42%. In Asia, Nikkei 225 down 0.37% and \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  down 0.12%.\par \par \uc2 WATCH FOR: 8:30 a.m. EDT. July PPI. [Expected 0.1% vs. 0.4% in June. Ex-food and energy expected 0.1% vs. 0.3% in June.]; 9:15 a.m. EDT. July Industrial Production. [Expected 0.4% vs. 0.3% in June.]; 10 a.m. EDT. August Preliminary \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  Consumer Sentiment. [Expected 94 vs. 93.3 in July prelim.]\par \par \uc2 THE BREAKFAST BRIEFING\par \par \uc2 Despite a looming rate rise, Wall Street isn't counting out rate-sensitive stocks.\par \par \uc2 "The ongoing uncertainty [emanating from China] could continue to push investors into safe havens in the summer months," wrote \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  equity strategist Savita Subramanian.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets this week, devaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  not once but three times as the world's second-largest economy cools. While market participants have said the country's moves this week should have a small impact on the U.S., questions remain as to what other measures, if any, China may take.\par \par \uc2 "One of the questions driving this uncertainty is what the impact of further Chinese stimulus will be," said Ms. Subramanian.\par \par \uc2 By devaluing the yuan as growth slows, Chinese officials have added disinflationary pressure to the global markets. For the U.S., where inflation is already running below the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's 2% target, cheaper Chinese exports have a downward effect on U.S. import prices and make American goods less competitive on the global scale.\par \par \uc2 At times when growth isn't perceived to be strong and rates are low, investors tend to turn to high-dividend yielding, or rate-sensitive, stocks to seek sure, less-risky means to income.\par \par \uc2 While China's actions dragged down global markets this week, U.S. stocks have largely kept their cool with the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  up 0.3% in the past four sessions and safe-haven bets like utilities and telecoms outperforming.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Golub, chief U.S. market strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , says that, to the extent that weaker growth results in more gradual policy normalization, utilities, telecoms and REITs -- rate-sensitive sectors -- should prosper. Commodity- or growth-related groups, on the other hand, would face headwinds, he says. Those include energy, materials and industrials.\par \par \uc2 Economists still overwhelmingly expect the Fed to raise interest rates in September. Higher rates could lessen the appeal of rate-sensitive stocks as yields on other securities, like bonds, increase. Still, many expect the first rate rise to be small and the course of policy normalization to be slow. Therefore, safe-haven plays could be attractive even leading to liftoff.\par \par \uc2 Amid increased uncertainty in the markets, Wall Street doesn't think the clock has run out on rate-sensitive names.\par \par \uc2 Morning MoneyBeat Daily Factoid: On this day in 2003, a major power outage swept across the eastern United States and parts of Canada, affecting 50 million people, including residents of New York, Cleveland and Detroit, as well as Toronto and Ottawa.\par \par \uc2 - By Kristen Scholer\par \par \uc2 STOCKS TO WATCH\par \par \uc2 J.C. Penney\uc2  is expected to post a 50-cent loss in the second quarter on revenue of $2.8 billion, according to an average of analysts' estimates by FactSet.\par \par \uc2 After Thursday's close, King Digital shares slumped 7.6% to $14.05 on heavy volume. The "Candy Crush" maker's earnings topped Wall Street estimates but said gross bookings, a measure of customer orders, for the second quarter dropped 13%.\par \par \uc2 Nordstrom shares surged 4.6% to $78.40 after the company reported a better-than-expected second-quarter profit.\par \par \uc2 MUST READS (LINKS)\par \par \uc2 WSJ Poll: Economists See September Fed Rate Liftoff: An overwhelming majority of private forecasters polled think the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will begin to raise short-term interest rates next month.\par \par \uc2 China Stock Futures Signal Deeper Pessimism: As global financial markets try to gauge the impact of a weaker yuan, China's stock-index futures are signaling that investors are more pessimistic than current Shanghai share prices suggest.\par \par \uc2 Market Turmoil Saps Momentum for China Reform: \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fighting to open the country's markets, but questions are emerging about whether its creaky financial system, and the people who run it, are up to the task.\par \par \uc2 Can You Judge an IPO by Its CEO?: Wall Street is hardly a beauty pageant. But a new study suggests that when it comes to taking a company public, appearances can matter.\par \par \uc2 Oil Prices Slide to 6 1/2 -Year Low : Crude-oil futures on the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2  dropped to a more-than six-year low on escalating concerns about the mismatch between global supply and demand.\par \par \uc2 Eurozone Economic Growth Slows: The eurozone's economic recovery lost momentum in the second quarter, underscoring deep-rooted fragility amid a cooling Chinese economy and concerns about Greece's debt.\par \par \uc2 Greek Parliament Passes Bailout Agreement: Greek lawmakers approved the country's third bailout agreement with international creditors after an all-night debate in parliament, hours before European finance ministers meet to decide whether to endorse the deal.\par \par \uc2 BP Manipulated Texas Natural Gas Market, Judge Says: A U.S. regulatory judge ruled \uc2 BP\uc2  manipulated the Texas natural gas market in 2008 and said fines against the British energy company could increase because the scheme took place after earlier market manipulation.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -Hedged ETFs Soften Blow of Dollar's Rise: Investors are pouring billions of dollars into a fashionable corner of one of the fastest-growing areas on Wall Street: exchange-traded funds that aim to protect investors against the return-crimping impact of a rising dollar.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Believes ISIS Used Chemical Weapon on Kurds: \uc2 Islamic State\uc2  militants likely used mustard agent in an attack earlier this week on Kurdish forces in Iraq, senior U.S. officials said, in the first indication the militant group has obtained a banned chemical weapon. \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 14, 2015 06:52 ET (10:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150814eb8e0015z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
