\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  hit by yuan losses, ringgit at 17-year low\b0\par\par\uc2 868 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2015\par 08:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Yuan to suffer largest weekly loss ever\par     * Sliding oil hits ringgit; Malaysian stocks, bonds fall\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell\par on Friday and are set for weekly losses, defying China's efforts\par to soothe jittery global markets after yuan devaluation, due to\par a bearish outlook for the world's second-largest economy and its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Malaysian ringgit hit a fresh pre-peg 17-year low\par in thin liquidity as falling oil prices raised concerns over the\par country's exports. Offshore funds, including real money\par accounts, dumped the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with falling local shares \par and government bond prices.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 set its daily yuan\par midpoint rate firmer than the previous session for\par the first time after this week's devaluation. That helped the\par renminbi stabilise. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  had stated on Thursday\par that there was no basis for further depreciation in the yuan,\par given strong economic fundamentals.\par     Despite such efforts, the outlook for the yuan and China's\par economy remains pessimistic, which will hurt other emerging\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  further, traders and analysts said.\par     "China is doing the right thing for themselves. They will\par weaken further down the line," said Kay Van-Petersen, Asia Macro\par Strategist for Saxo Capital Markets.\par     "China became the biggest trading partners to a lot of Asian\par countries. With a weaker yuan, their exports are going to go\par lower. This is a kind of double whammy," he said.\par     Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dropped to multi-year lows as\par China devalued the yuan on Tuesday after a series of\par disappointing economic data suggested the economy needs further\par stimulus. That prompted fears of a "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war."\par     \par     YUAN, RINGGIT ROUTS \par     The devaluation forced the yuan to its largest weekly loss\par ever of 3.0 percent against the dollar, according to Thomson\par Reuters data.\par     The ringgit has declined 3.3 percent throughout the week,\par which would be the biggest weekly slide since May 2010.\par     On Friday, the Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell to 4.1500 per dollar,\par its weakest since Sept 1. 1998. Malaysia pegged the ringgit at\par 3.8000 from September 1998 until 2005.\par     "The most pessimistic targets have been met and now we are\par seeing traders price in a move to reach 4.25," said Stephen\par Innes, senior trader for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  broker Oanda in Singapore.\par     "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is signalling very little fight in defense\par of the move."\par     Malaysia's international reserves fell to $96.7 billion,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data showed last week, fuelling doubts over the\par government's ability to bolster the ringgit amid capital\par outflows. Some analysts see the possibility of capital controls,\par but Bank Negara Malaysia Governor Zeti Akhtar Aziz on Thursday\par said Malaysia did not want to peg the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     Investors have lost confidence on sliding commodity prices\par and a corruption scandal linked to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Najib Razak\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par and \f28 \uc2 \f2 1Malaysia Development Berhad\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     The Indonesian rupiah has lost 1.7 percent so far\par this week, hitting a fresh 17-year low on Wednesday. The\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell on bond outflows amid worries about the effect of\par falling commodities prices on exports.\par     India's rupee has declined 2.1 percent for the week\par as China's devaluation hurt sentiment. If the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  maintains\par the loss, it would be the largest weekly slide since August\par 2013.\par     The Singapore dollar has declined 1.3 percent\par throughout this week as the city-state's economy contracted in\par the second quarter, raising some speculation about further\par easing. \par     With Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakening sharply, central banks of\par Asian countries such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Malaysia, Indonesia and South\par Korea have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow the declines, traders said.\par     South Korea's won ended the week down 0.6 percent\par as several traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities were\par estimated to have sold about $4 billion in total on Tuesday and\par Wednesday. Local markets were closed on Friday\par for a holiday.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0515 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   