\par\par \b \uc2 Stronger USD Weighing Kiwi Down -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2015\par 08:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0605 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD has spent the session under pressure after USD gained ground on a solid U.S. July retail sales report. The positive data just reminded everyone that even though the Kiwi got a reprieve there is an underlying trend, says \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions corporate dealing manager Chris Hunter. The pair is at 0.6534 late in New Zealand and Hunter says it has support at 0.6510 and resistance at 0.6580. Overnight investor attention will turn to U.S. industrial production as well as the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  Survey of Consumers for further direction. Hunter says any bad news will likely be brushed off while any good news will just add to the USD's strength. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0607 GMT [Dow Jones] The exit of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its stock markets will be long and painful but a necessary part of opening market access to the world, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  chief China economist Chen Xingdong. "Investors need to be aware that the government's exit from the market could break down psychological barriers and cause the stock market to head downwards once again," Chen says. "However, investors and the government need to embrace any such development as a return to market pricing. The government needs to let the market function, as long as there is no new panic. We believe that the bailout exit process could last more than six months." \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  could scale down its market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a variety of ways, from allowing initial public offerings to resume to removing trading limits for domestic and foreign institutional investors. "In order to regain market confidence and investor trust, China would reaffirm its market-oriented reform," Chen adds.(Dominique.fong@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @dominiquefong) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] The ringgit could move towards 4.2500 against the U.S. dollar, says Oanada's Stephen Innes, as it sinks to new 17-year low. Innes says the slide comes as "confluence of negatives builds" with China's yuan devaluation being the most recent to hit the ringgit. Investors also appear to shrug off better-than-expected second quarter gross domestic product figures and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Thursday assurance to eschew capital control measures. The ringgit sinks to 4.1150 to the U.S. dollar--representing a 2.6% interday decline and bringing its year-to-date loss to 15.1%. "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is signaling very little fight in defense of the move," Innes adds. (jason.ng@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @ByJasonNg) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0559 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 ended down 0.7% at 5696.45 as investors continue to digest the impact of a weaker yuan and the consequences of China's move. While on Friday, Beijing set the yuan's daily rate against the U.S. dollar roughly unchanged from the previous day, jitters remain and even companies that reported strong FY earnings failed to benefit. Nuplex (NPX.NZ) shed 0.7% to NZ$4.15 while Steel & Tube (STU.NZ) shed 2.1% to NZ$2.75. Earlier Nuplex said net profit attributable to shareholders rose 35.1% on the year to NZ$70.8 million. Steel & Tube said its full-year net profit was NZ$21.4 million, up 20% on the year. Investors will be keeping one eye on China and one eye on company reports next week. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0554 GMT [Dow Jones] Jefferies upgrades \f28 \uc2 \f2 Li Ning Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (2331.HK) to 'hold' as shares of the Chinese sportswear maker add another 3.2% Friday. The Beijing-based company announced better-than-expected results Thursday, with first-half losses narrowing due to higher sales. Management is confident new initiatives like a smart shoe made in partnership with smartphone maker Xiaomi and a lower-end line of apparel will boost the company back to profitability for the first time since 2011. "We turn positive on Li Ning due to its improving channel inventory and same-store sales growth and better cost control," says Jefferies. Yet the still-loss-making company could stand to improve. "We believe its channel profitability and retail capability needs further improvement," says Jefferies. (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @anjiezheng) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0553 GMT [Dow Jones] Some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants still remain skeptical about China, contending the yuan devaluation is not over. "After all this, I think the devaluation may be more than what they've done so far," says Toshihiko Sakai, senior manager of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial products trading division at Mitsubishi UFJ Trust and Banking, noting that there are clear signs that the Chinese economy is turning for the worse. "I think it's pretty normal to assume an eventual 10% devaluation," says Sakai, adding that China may act slowly as early as beginning of the autumn with half an eye on the U.S. response. A top \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  official described a 10 devaluation as "nonsense." (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0509 GMT [Dow Jones] Although the Philippine government is sticking to its official growth target of 7%-8% in 2015, it is lowering its expectations for the year after an underwhelming 5.2% rise in first-quarter output. Finance Secretary Cesar Purisima told lawmakers this week during a session on next year's budget proposals that 6% growth in 2015 would be a satisfactory performance, given the economic uncertainty elsewhere in the region. Second-quarter growth figures for the Philippines are due to be released later this month, and are again expected to fall short of the 7% mark, according to most analysts' predictions. (trefor.moss@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0445 GMT [Dow Jones] The Indian rupee remained weak Friday despite strength in other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The rupee fell to 65.30 against the U.S. dollar, compared with Thursday's close of 65.10. Analysts say it hasn't fallen as much as other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently, and that the decline is necessary to keep India's exports competitive. U.S. dollar demand from importers and government-owned banks has been relentless, while the unwinding of short positions by offshore traders and domestic speculators is also causing the dollar's sharp rise, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anindya Banerjee\f28 \uc2 \f2 , associate vice president at brokerage \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kotak Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The market will pay close attention to the Chinese yuan in the near term, Mr. Banerjee says, adding that he expects the Indian rupee have support at 64 against the U.S. dollar and face resistance at 65.50 or 65.60. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Can tourists and students fix Australia's trade balance? The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised hopes recently by saying the weaker Australian dollar was providing more demand and investment in export-orientated services industries. However, the boost from tourism and education to the nation's GDP could fade before long, says Paul Dales, chief Australia economist at research firm Capital Economics in London. Even if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was to fall on a trade-weighted basis to around US$0.58 from US$0.63 now, rising net exports of tourism-related services would only add 0.2 percentage points to real GDP growth until the end of 2016, he says. While the long-term outlook for services exports remains rosy, the Australian dollar would need to weaken much further before tourism and education could compensate for the faltering growth in mining, Mr. Dales says. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @verasydney) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] Hong Kong equities creep upwards in the morning session as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 's yuan fix strengthens for the first time in four days. "Market sentiments are noticeably more composed today," says IG, citing the stronger fixing as a factor. But the return of calm to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets also gives breathing room to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 : "What is more interesting was the fact that implied probability for a September Fed hike jumped back to 50%, reflecting market perception that the Chinese move is no longer a huge consideration in the U.S. monetary policy," IG adds. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index advances 0.2% to 24057.10, while H-shares are up by the same magnitude, while the Shanghai Composite climbs 0.8%. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index increases 0.2% to 11098.61. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0419 GMT (Dow Jones) \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  is thought to have sold around $100 million in total in the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to slow the USD/IDR rise after most regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  got hammered again. Dealers detected the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  selling dollars from 13,770 rupiah to 13,790 rupiah. The pair is now at 13,785, vs. 13,750 at last close Thursday. On Thursday, as Bank Indonesia Gov. Agus Martowardojo called the rupiah's recent weakness "unacceptable," the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was estimated to have sold up to $600 million. i-made.sentana@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: WWE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/USD pair is striking against the first upside target at 1.1170 after rebounding from the key support at 1.1090 overnight. It is well supported by the 20-period intraday moving average, which has crossed above the 50-period one. The intraday relative strength index is well directed within the buying area between 50 and 70 lacking downward momentum. If the pair breaks above 1.1170, it could head towards the next upside target at 1.1215 (the high of August 12). Only a break below 1.1090 would turn the intraday outlook bearish and call for a decline toward the first alternative downside target at 1.1030. This content was produced exclusively for Dow Jones by Trading Central (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ), a leading investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 14, 2015 02:07 ET (06:07 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150814eb8e000n0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
